      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Ais epitaph for a 'faithful tram-car conduct Or—he took his last fare well: IF you value the friendship of your friend, never agree with him when be calls himself a fool. ON ONE CONDITION.—" Do you believe in dreams?' "Yes—if they come true." THg desire of some men to wobble round in a big place rather than fill a small one, accounts for many reversals of fortune. MEN' are frequently like tea—their real strength and goodness arc not properly drawn out until they have been in hot water. Firisx Gossip: "Do you believe that awful story they are telling about Miss Prim?" Second gossip: "Yes. What is it?" _ _ .</p>
        <p>_ Porxrt TRAMP.—Judge: "What is your name?" Tramp: "Allow me to exchange cards with your honour." " BRIDGET, did you hear me call ?" Yes, mum, but you told me the other day never to answer you hack." A COLLEGE student is reported as saying that on Lhe whole lip rather enjoy.) his studies. They furnish a needed relaxation from his athletic work. "WHAT kind of a „memory have you?" "Bad —very bad." "In what particular?" "It remembers thins that were better forgotten." HE: " M ahel, tell me, do you love me?" She: "Yes, darling; I would die for you." lie: "No, dear; I prefer your hair as it is at present." t Pinar: "This is your birthday, Tommy. What can I do for you that will cause you pleasure?" Tommy: "Spank Johnny 1" WIGGLE : "How long did you know your wife before you married her, Satupon?" Satupon (mournfully) : "I didn't know her at all till I married her."</p>
        <p>IT is astonbidn,g how much scorn, indignation, and contempt a woman can put into two words. If you do not believe it, just listen while she speaks of someone she dislikes as "that man."</p>
        <p>Tom "Has Harry paid you that money he borrowed? ' Dick: "No, and I don't care if he never does." Tom: "You don't !" Dick: "No. I have the fun of seeing him trying to avoid meeting me." A ElCYCLE'dealer has utilised the happy thought of presenting a. pair of crutches and a b ,x of court plaster to each purchaser of a bicycle. He is monopolising the trade. A GOOD story is told of the Indians, who replied when a missionary asked them if they werq willing to abstain from work on Sunday: "Yes, and not only on Sunday, but on all other days as well." ACCORDING to an old superstition of the medireval Church, whenever a cock crows a lie is being told. The reason that cocks crow so persistently in the early morning is because the morning papers are being set up. " THERE are two things," remarked Fogg, in a contemplative mood, "that I don't understand. One of these is, how the world got along before I came into it; and the other, how it is going to get along after I have left itl" DAWKINS : "I was a great friend of your late husband. Have you any little thing of his you could let me have to remind me of him?" Discon- solate widow: "'What's the matter with me?" Exit Dawkins.</p>
        <p>MOTTIER : "Johnny on your way home from school stop at the shop and get me a sugarstick and a bar of soap." Father: "What do you want with a sugarstick ?" Mother: "That's so he'll. remember the soap!" -</p>
        <p>THE trouble with a young fellow in love is, that he imagines everybody else is in love with the girl too; while, very likely, no one but himself ever looks at her. ALGY : " Weginald, what did you say, you know, when you were pwesented to the Pwince of Wales?" Reginald (American dude, who'd been presented to 11.R.11.) : "Why, deah boy, the first thing I did was to apologise for the Amewican We vol ution." FEA.NIC : "I hear that young Simpkins started to commit suicide when his girl jilted him. Did he carry out his intention ? " Jack: "No. He met another girl on the way, and fell in love with her." _ .</p>
        <p>UNDERSTOOD THE SEX. —Hostess : "Why do the Chinese cripple the feet of their girl babies so inhumanly ? " Traveller: "To save their darlings lifelong suffering." "How can it?" "When Chinese girls grow up they are able to wear small shoes without torture." A SMALL Bo v s VIEWS. —Mother : "There goes another one of those dirty tramps. I can't see why they don't keep clean." Small Son: " Mebby w'en they was little their mothers made them wash so often they got tired of it." JAKE: "Here's an advertisement in th' payer</p>
        <p>for that dog yeti found. The man wot own; fiim offers a reward." Jim: "How d'ye know it's a mane" _ Jake: "Th' paper says 'no questions</p>
        <p>asked.'"</p>
        <p>A COUNTRY' lady, who was recently in London, astounded a female pickpocket, who, in an omni- bus, had thrust her hand into the lady's pocket, by turning and coolly inquiring, "Why do you put your hand in my pocket, when I have my purse here in my band ?' NEVER WAS WEST. —Veteran : "I presume you never heard an Indian war-whoop ? '' Civilian: "No • but I've heard boys coming out of school." "WILLIE," said the young mother to her abject slave, "you must go to work and raise a beard right away." " What for?" asked the bewildered husband. "To amuse baby. His grandpa was here this afternoon, and it would have done your heart good to see how the little darling enjoyed pulling the old man's whiskers. He pulled some of them clean out." "YES, I'm in the lecture business," said the long-haired passenger, "and I'm making money. I've got a scheme, I have, and it works to a charm. Big houses wherever Igo." "A scheme?" "Yes. I always advertise that my lectures are especially for women under thirty years of age and men out of debt. You just ought to see the way the people come trooping in. Trrus Titmouse was infuriated, but the editor of the "Western Wind" shut him up in two seconds. "Is this the newspaper office?" inquir?d Mr. Titmouse: "It is," responded the man at the desk. "Didn't this paper say I was a liar?" and he began prancing. "It did not." "Didn't it say I was a scoundrel 2" "It did not." " Well, some paper said it." "Possibly it was our contemporary down the street," suggested the editor, as he picked tip a paper-weight. "This p ,per never prints stale news.'' "You and Freddie don't seem as friendly as formerly," said one young man to another. "Oh, we are just as f wiendly as evah, I assure you." "But you never go about together any more." "We cawn't, don't you know; we both have new suits of clothes, and the colours don't hahmonise. Walking down the stweet togethah we'd be a per- fect wiobi-"</p>
        <p>Everybody venders lir.w the PearP 11 IrtlrrV Comp•nv Manley, cau set; 000 , lets of ceot;!ery t,r one guinea '£) 15). Get a post card 7, t &amp;re, and t. A beautil er eervice. a chna Ira 54.1 vier, sles &amp;c.. &amp;c. ovrYtOdV pl Fur ably atirpri,eo. All for ORO ruines. If you vvish to savo money, vrr:te to the PEARSON kUTTltiir CO, y,LUtbat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Artillery Volunteers throughout the country will hear with great satisfaction that the Queen' has given another proof of the interest she takdes in their arm of the service by increasing her subscrip- tion to the National Artillery Association. The Queen has hitherto given a prize. of £lOO to the. association, which is known as the Queen's prize. She had added to that another MO, to be offered as a Queen's prize to position artillery, whit) could not specially compete for the other prize. I bear, also, that the Prince of Wales is likely to increase the prize he cffers in the same direction.</p>
        <p>Sir Courtenay Boyle is receiring the greatest credit on all sides for the patient and hopeful per- severance he bas shown in his efforts to bring the boot strike or lock-out to an end. The Governmmit also are sharing in the general congratulations, for. the Board of Trade has had the courage to make a new departure in such quarrels, and has not waited until both parties, after infinite suffering, were worn out, and only wanted some one to build a ,olden bridge whereby they might each escape from the conflict with drums beating and colours flying. The pre,ent arbitration • was not only a courageous step to take, but was one distinctly in 'advance of the intervention ol Lord Rosebery in the coal strike and of Mr. Asquith in the cab srrike,and its success will encourage the Government, and pro- bably also any succeeding Tory Government, to enter the field as a conciliator in f nture industrial quarrels of magnitude. It is possib!e, however, for the Government to take a step further forward till, and to intervene as conciliator before the con- flict breaks out at all. The building trades. for example', are on•the eve of a colossal struggle,which, if not prevented, will begin on the let of May, and will throw 40,000 men out of employment. Wby ,hrgild the Board of Traee not endeavour to bring about an agreement before the strike begins?</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the visit of Hasrulla Khan, the second son of the Ameer of Afghanistan, to this country, only await the Royal snaction. What is known is that he will be received in State at Porte- mouth, that he has a retinue of seventy-three per- sons with him, and, as Buckingham Palace will be occupied, he will stay at 'Dorchester House, the residence of Colonel HoHord. Equerry to the Prince of Wales. Aldershot is to give him a review, the Queen is to receive him in State, and he may visit the principal centres of industry in bnglancl. He may reach England about the third week in May.</p>
        <p>Some very hard things are being said of Mr. Beaufoy, the member for Kennington, P.Tul -the situnticn is now marked by the appearance in the field .of a genuine Liberal candidate, who declares himself a strenuous supporter of all the items of rhe Newcastle programme. T'e is put forward by the Temperance party, and will command, there- fore, the Temperance vote, and that of a large num- ber of Liberals who di,approre of Mr. Beaufoy's conduct. Unless the Liberal chieis,before the else- non come:.; round,' can heal the split among the, Liberal wire-pullers ot the constituency, the quarrel will be the means of handing the sent over tn the Tories. It is manifestly Mr. Beaufoy's duty to Liberalism to retire ; but while the selection of the wire-pullers turns upon personal fri..ndships. in- stead of political principles, it. is obvious that the interests of the constituency will be prejudiced.</p>
        <p>The proposal has been once more made to erect a statue to Dickens in London, and the ground ad- vanced is that it would but tress Dickens's waning reputation. But Dickens had a foreboding that alter his death the usual appeal would be made for trio&amp; to raise a mamori-1 of him. and he adjured them earnestly in bis will to do nothing of the kind. In the teeth ot th. expresaert rlestro of the novelist the statue project is gratuitous cfficionaties,. But is it, true that Dickens's reputation is waning 1 Newspapers and magszines no longer write about him any more then they write about Shakespeare or Thackeray. They are now busy with the liter- ary products of our own time—the " new fiction." land the literary aimedf the "new woman' and her school. How many of all the story writers of the present. day will have it said of him or her, at the end of 30 years, as can he said of Dickens. that nearly three-quarters of a million of copies of their works have been sold? How many of them at, the sod of 30 years will even be remembered by name?</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Allen (Sewcasile-under-Lytne) has aban- doned all hope of making tffective propress this ~,ason with bis Bill dealing with " tied " houses. Ile intends, therefore, to proceed by resolution. and win move, if an opportunity ,offers, on the 17th of Slay that the" tied ' house system should be pro- iJibited by law. The hon. gentleman's Titan would be to give all licensees power to buy their liquor. whether malt or 'spifitimus. where they choose, and make null and void nit contracts to the contrary.</p>
        <p>A strange s4arement tp•p been made to the mili- tary authorities by three officers stationed at.Devon- uort. They declare that while walking along th • line pf the sunken batterie on the Cornish side of the liver they suddenly came upon three men Misil% engaged in eiretching the defences and in carefully noting the position of the batteries. They soon dipeovered that. the visitors were Frenchmen, and the belief is alcn entertained that they were military or naval officer-. In the, nbsenee of any power of arrest they got clear away. As a matter of fret our men had no i•lea as to what they should or could do.</p>
        <p>A specimen of the great auk, i now extinct arteries was. after an interval of twenty-.ix yew's, effered tor sale by auction on Tuesday. The event brought •neether many well-known ornithologists, among t "em the representatives of sernral, State nnd pro- vincial museums. The higheQt cff,r was 3.50 guinPnA hut, as tee reserved price was not reached the hint did not change hands. There are only eightr known snecimens of the species in eNi.Ptence, twenty-four of these being in (heat Britain. An egg of the great auk sold on Tnenticy afternoon for .ElBO ; publican of-Camden Town being the purchaser.</p>
        <p>•The acceptance by Arelldescon Farrar of the Deanery of Canterbury has excited considerable surprise. During the last few veers the income of the Deanery has fallen considerably. until novs it is one of the poorest of all, whvresse Dr. Farrar is now in the enjoyment. of very considerable emolument. From the many offices he combines in hie own per- son. Be vvill thus be called upon to make con- siderable pecuniary sacrifices in becoming successor to Dean Payne Smith. his lose to London will he keenly fel.t.tor there is no other Anglican preacher who possesses such power to impress the multi- tude. his influence has widened considerably in recent, years since he identified himself so closely with the Evangelical party in the Church,althongh it cannot be forgotten that that Fame party looked askance at him when he first brought. out his ser- mons on "eternal hope" But times have changed -ince then, as is seen in the fact. that, he is to pre- .-ide next, week at, the evening meeting cf the Church Missionary Society in Faefor Hall ; and his appointment to the Deaners; of Canterbury has pro- duced the liveliest, satisfaction in Low Church circles, not, however. unroinviel with regret that he was not reserved for something better.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cuninghame Graham is threatened with an t net]; on his birthright. Mr. Graham. as is well known, "claims" the Earldoms of Glencnirn, Men- it h, end Airth; and, according to the "New York World." a commercial traveller of the name of flraham is this slimmer to cross, the Atlantic to prove, "by documentary vvidence." that these arldoms are his own. Tt is true that the "New York World "speaks of one of the FArldoms as that of "Strathearn;" but as the Earldom of St.rathearn was annexed to the Crown nearly five hundred yenrs no it must now he mentioned in conjunction with those of Menteith and Airth merely by a slip of the pen.</p>
        <p>Captain Youngbusband, who is a crent authority Ott matters concerning India, holds a reputation in the British Army that is quite unique. lie is one of the boldest, and most intrepid explorers of his time, and has gained for himself a certain celebrity more widespread perhaps than that of any otllee traveller. Be is a member of the Indian Staff Corps, sturdy and well-built, of middle height and soldierly appearance. In moments of dancer he is stern and resolute, but at otiber times his naturil djAuosition is to jie one of the gentlest and most affectionate of men.</p>
      </div>
