      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL '27. 1555.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL '27. 1555.</p>
        <p>The earliest form of Vie glove was a mere bag for the hand.</p>
        <p>Outer blinds for windows were unknown until the fourteenth century. Tae Venetian, or interior blinds,* are so called bemuse they were first used in Venice. The scarfs known es cravats oe croats came into France about 1658. They were copied frsm the shawls which the Croats wore round their necks, and were im- mediately adopted and exaggerated by the ladies. The French and Italian perfumers of the sixteenth century made a noted cosmetic by pulverising togetl er the wings and clawa of pigeons, turpentine, lilies,eggs, honey, sea shells, mother-o'-pearl and camphor. , The statistics of life insurance people show that in the last twenty-five years the average of man's life has in- creaSed five per cent., or two whole years, from 41.9 to 43 9 years. Assuming that the twenty million women of England shed tears twice a sear, a statistical fiend has figured out that the products f the combined weeps would about equal the displacement of a one hundred aid eighty ton yacht. ' • Because in Spanish etiquette the King can only asso- ciate with Ida equals the boy Alfonso can have no play- mates. Be cannot p!av with his own sisters as an ordin- ary boy might. Ple. 1:e Of to he has always had, among them a fine r• ceing-house, covered with the skin of is real pony. This aria his black cat Ferrito have been two of his greatest.coinfort,so he is a real boy after afl. Some wise head and .kindly heart—perhaps the Queen- Mether's—managed to get around the rule that cut that boy off from companions of his own age. A boy regi- ment has been formed for him. In it„are four hundred and fifty boys in uniform led by a hand. . A funny story comes from Australia. A New Zeala id youth wrote to a lady proposing marriage, and then tor a whole week he waits4l in vain for an answer. Being driven to fury at lasi by yhe lady's cool n. gl ,ct, he arose and proposed o anoth• r pit I, and was twcepti d. Next day the original yowl, who had been out of town, returned and received the letter' and replied with an enthusiastic affirmative ; and the distracted youth,find- ing himself affianced to two girls at once, despairingly packed up his high collar and left his native shores for Australia.</p>
        <p>A Rocking (hair for Indigestion. A not unpleasant remedy for indigestion is suggested by a medical autholity. He declares that by resorting to a slow arid regular r (Icing motion after mea'is, gastro- intestinal peristalsis (tile worm-like motion of the inta- tines when aiding the digestion of their contents) is timulated, and persons whose digestive functions are habitually sluggish, thercby experience marked relief. He especially, recommends this measure in cAes where there is any want of tone in the stomach. The rocking chair should be so construc!ed ac to be set in motion without much effort, -CA the inclination of the axis should he sufficient to place the patient in an almost horizontal position.</p>
        <p>Girls and Manners. There are many girls at the present day who think it the correct thing to, :cultivate a reputation for good fellowship, but they will find it does not pay in the long run. They must seek to eschew all conduct that does not lie parallel with lines of refinement, duty, and womanliness. To a true woman lasting homage is always given. As some writer has recently remarked, 'The shores of Bohemia are composed ef. jagged rocks on which many a hark hearing the name • Woman's Reputation 'has been shattered. Women cannot be too careful in their manners." It is very easy to overstep the border ; therefore, always be on your guard for ' thorough happiness and the best results the world can give.</p>
        <p>A' Marriage Club. There is a novel kind of dub started in one of the American States. It has been formed specially for bdping persons to marry. It is said that the organisa- tion opposes marriages that are liable to cause misery, and its members combine to aid unions which May be conducive to happiness. All the wa,rkings af the order are secret, and the pressure is brought to bear so fully that no one ever knows that he or she is being in- fluenced hy such an organisation. Meetings are held once a week, and each member makes a report in reg rd to certain cases. At the annual meeting just held the president remarked—" You have probably noticed the increase in gocd marriages during the past year. Well, most of them were du rto the influence of our society, end they have proved to be happy, unions. We have , about a dozen marriages well under way, and they.will take place within a few months. Our organisation works merely in the interest of humanity."</p>
        <p>What N;tions Steep On. The ancient rt on th flo;.r or on a divan covered with skins. I ,U V Ve Ages beds were made or rushes. lesthe- - U. is believed that feather b'ds were km ~ ' ; • .ns, since a inention in one of the poets ; ; ,-;irirms that they slept on f.eit hers is su- co-e -;) this kind of bed. Relit, gabaliis had d also an air mattre,s 218 0- 222 A.D. F-t 1•, ; re employed bY the better clwee3 in Et.g;'...l ; lie clays of Henry VIII.. thotigh—rney- wor, . luxuries and were expen- sive. The beriSiekt'. -1,, Egyptians, Romans, and Greeks much re-p.,:;.. oor ;vvn couches. To the pre- sent day hed3tea4.a liaod and some parts of Ger- many are fitted op 7'P it leather beds, on one. which the stot•!-,r 1. vibile the other is used for cover- ing. Fancy .;t•it a, lung it) these hygienic tVes ! The Russian peas ‘: ill, le beds on top of the family oven for the sa:te (.1 :he warmth aNvem forth by the fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAREST NELTAB,</p>
        <p>We are to be deluged with fancy stuffs this season, and, to tell you the truth, I am not at all sorry. Satin cloth just now is a very popular material, and is shown in a variety of patterns, as well as plain. Blue is undoubtedly the colour of the moment, in a number of tones that almost 13, ill description. We have gendarme, Austrian, corbflowert and hyacinth, periwinkle, electric, and blue•grey,be&amp;iries shades that, to me at least, are nameless. Bias is followed closely by brown, and thie hue can no longer be dubbed sombre, but is to be seen in pretty tones of golden, tan, and nut- browr, besides no wares. Reseda is especially pretty teal seams, and A svelcome change. Apple, moss, and rry;rtle green are also colours of the hour, as %ell as a deep tons of. strawberry, magenta, and tomato red, and some lovely greys. However, you must nut. think that there are no plain materia;s "going," as the phrase is. There are a very number of tweeds, boiges, corduroys; and Amazon clothe.</p>
        <p>What I can describe as nothing less than a beautiful dress was tne following model I saw at my marine's. It was made in black Sicilian alpaca, the iritq.t cat full wish strapped seams, and thet.coat finished in the same way. There was a large collar, with revere of 'white satin, over which was placed a black net, with 6;ru spots and sprigs, and a kilting of the trams net, with an edging of Valenciennes lace, falling all rcund.. The front was made of white chillm, v. 41 r cd, and the neck finished, with satin ribbons and rosette. The accompanying hat was of black fancy c,raree straw, trimmed with blackbird anti white chiffon.</p>
        <p>All sorts of materials are used for capes, which are of increased variety and beauty. Besides velvet and cloth—dainty models previously described—all sorts of shots and figured silks are being pressed into the eervice, trimmed with lace and embroideries and lined with silk plaid. A cape of shot taffetas is figured with delicate chinds flowers, vague and only half seen in cortain It is for evenirez wear. The largesb cape, of the taffetas, reaches the waist. Over this is a second cape of rich white lace, and above this a third cape of taffetas edged all round with white feather. The hood, whicto can be drawn over the head, is a fleecy mass of white chiffon edged with feather. Cciffon is to be seal as a trimming of many capes. One very short cape' not' rearming the waist, con- sists of six panels of dark brown velvet, each lined with pink silk and edged with a thick bottillonne of pink chiffon. A wreath of May or pink orchids forun a collar. Tnis lov-ly little garment makes a smart wrap over a rich gown for weddings, garden parties, races, ,etc.</p>
        <p>For evening wear one sees some dressy blouses, which have been imported from France. Purism- nes are reokoning as blouses even the low-necked bodices which finish in a draped belt, and are covered with a flounce or berthe which falls from the bust to the waist. Strictly speaking, this is not a blouse, though at the same time it can hardly be rocker:ad among the fitted corsages which die. play all the line of the figure. But leaving this style aside, Blglishwomen owe to Paris some lovely blouse creations for evening wear—very Parisian, very dainty, very chic., 'There are low fall bodices of shot satin, strapped with broad bands of velvet (embroidered with jet and gold sequins), three bands froet and beak, the two outer ones continuing over the shoulders for shoulder. straps, which are the only covering the shoulders possess. The sleeves are a huge satin puff, down which falls straps of embreidered velvet. More youthful are the low satin bodices covered with full, draped gauze of a second shade, hung from s neck- piece of lace or gauged or smocked gauze. The sleeves are often of gauze or else of the satin. The gatzl sleeves are often four or five email puffs or bouillonnd ii the light material, or the sleeve is one great puff draped with bows or dowers.</p>
        <p>Most of the most fashienable hats are distinctly of the Empire period, with the characteristic high crown, and the edre of these have ruches of silk. bordered chiffon, while feathers stand erect in the front from a rosette of velvet buttoned with paste. A novelty is crinoline straw embroidered in silver spangles; and another novelty is a brown straw with tucks of bleak straw at intervals all over it,. This latter, by the way, forms a delightful hat in the mushroom shape, with a large pink bow at the back and two feathers at one side.</p>
        <p>Yours lovingly,</p>
        <p>JENIII.</p>
        <p>There have been twenty-seven emes of insanity in the Bavarian royal family during the last hundred sears.</p>
        <p>Tdramtus.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fiity years by millions of mothers for their childrcn v/W le teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it Froduces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain. and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a buttoa." Of all Chemists, is. lid. per bcttle.—.Advt.</p>
