      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</head>
        <p>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LIBBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Mad Betrothal," "lone," "Parted by Fate," "Floribel's Lover," etc., etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>DISOWNED. WE must now return to Doris and the fatal moment in which she plunged madly from the rocks down into the swirling water below. The contact with tue water brought Doris to her senses and the realisation that she had no right to take the life God had given her. When she rose to the surface, it was the dark, dense shadows of the willows that fringed the bank. • Clutching the long branches that drooped to the water's edge, Doris succeeded in drawing herself</p>
        <p>- into safety. - "May God forgive me," she murmured again, falling upon her knees. "I think for the moment must have been mad—yes, driven mad by my</p>
        <p>terrible woe." Kneeling there, she heard Frederick Thornton wildly calling upon her name. A levy, bitter laugh that was half a sob fell from her lips, then site</p>
        <p>knew no more. For long hours Doris 'lay among the grass in the dense shadow of the trees, her white .stark face</p>
        <p>upturned to the night sky. Surely the pitying moonbeams, drifting down through the swaying branches, newer looked upon a sadder sight in all</p>
        <p>their rounds.</p>
        <p>At length consciousness returned to her. For a moment she was stunned, bewildered. Then the low wash of the river as it laved the banks brought</p>
        <p>a remembrance of what had transpired to her and</p>
        <p>how she came to be lying there. . Doris struggled to her feet and stood irresolute for a moment in the path. Where should she go? Which way should she turn? "It does not matter much," she murmured, and again she turned her white despairing face to the great city. "Cast adrift again on the streets of New York," she moaned. " well, I will look my cruel fate bravely in the face, I will teach my heart to for- get him. I will tear his hits...B out of my heart, though it take a lifetime. fshall never cross his path again—never! ,From this hour I leave tim old life behind me." The light broke, the sun rose, and another day was begun in the bustling city of New York, to. • ward winch poor, beautiful, hapless Doris, the child of fate had turned her steps. The saini sun which had crossed the zenith was shining just then upon a strange scene which was transpiring in the far-off village of Beech Grove on the Chesapeake. An hour before, a magnificent equipage had entered the village, and the driver had stopped one of the pedestrians, and inquired the way to Madame Del mar's seminary. Tne carriage contained a lady and gentleman. Both were greatly agitated, though the gentleman did his best to soothe his companion. "As we draw near the seminary gates, my heart beats so tumultuously, I almost fear it will break, Hulbert," she said, smiling through her tears. "Joy never kills, my dear Dora," he replied. "Calm yourself, darling." "How can you talk of being calm, Hulbert," she cried, "when I have been looking forward to ' this moment for nearly eighteen years? It has haunted me in my dreams, been ever present in my waking hours. My one prayer to Heaven has been, Heaven hasten the hour in which I am. to meet my child, and clasp her once more in her mother's lovinearms." ' "Doris tfiusi., have grown into a beautiful young girl," said the gentleman, thoughtfully. 'She has your features, my dear." • . "Will she meet me with a glad cry of love, or with coldness, I wonder ?" murmured the lady. "It must have been a great cross to her never to have known her parents; and the knowledge that she had been forsaken in her infancy by those who should have been her protectors. Poor little Doris !—my pretty litttle golden-haired baby I" And the lady ctimmencecl to weep afresh. • "You forget, Dora, it was done for the best— the very best," said Hulbert Brandon, a look of ! pain crossing Ws noble face. "You forget that our history—our past—has been no common one— our romantic meeting and hasty, secret marriage, and our dismay at findin, out there had been the bitterest kind of a feud' existing between our families for years. You were obliged to keep the . birth of ohr child, as well as our marriage, a secret from them, te ling them you were visiting friends during those months. " I will never forget how we met in secret at the park gate one night; how they discovered us standing there together, and tore you from my arms. They took you abroad, keeping you abroad long years; and in dis.guise I followed, , and Was always near you. We would have been happy, even under these disadvantages, if we had but had our child with us. .</p>
        <p>" You felt nore contented when the nurse wrote you that she had placed the child at Madame Del- mar's, and you received her letters regularly for years that she was watching the child faithfully from afar; and that Doris, who was known as madame a ward, was growing up into a beautiful young girl. "Now, after all these years, death has dissolved these bonds which bound us to secrecy,..and we can acknowledge the marriage which was solemnised eigeteen years ago at last." "And, hurrying here, we find the old nurse passed away two months ago," sighed Mrs. Brandon. "That is why her letters ceased to come. But we will speak no more of the awl. topic. What a joy- ful future we will have,. Ifulbert. We will take our daughter to our beautiful home, and surround her with every luxury wealth can purchase and love lavish upen her. How well we shall love her to make up for these long years of enforced separ. ation. Little Denis will be quite an heiress too— heiress to a Million. I hope it will ,not make the child proud." Huloert Brandon pressed his wife's hand. At that moment the carr age turned an ,abrupt curve in the steep road, and the towers and gables of the grim stone building, with the gilt letters, Madame Delmar's Seminary," burst upon their view. The lady trembled with agitation as her husband aseistel her to alight, anti led her through the great arched gateway up the lilac-bordered path that led to the steps of the entrance. A tidy maid answerei the summons and showed the visitors into the reception room. .' They preferred not to send you their card," said the girl to madame, in delivering .their mes- sage. "They said-,-" Madame cut the maid short. "No cards ! Humph ! Inferior people, most likely." And a dark frown settled on her face at being awakened froin her mid-day siesta. "indeed, they're not, madame," declared the naid. "The lady is a real lady, with a silk dress on as stiff as a board and diamonds on as big as partridge eggs." Madame Delmar walked down the stairs vAth a feeling of curiosity. " Why did they not send up their cards?" she wondered. Opening the door and entering with a stately mien, she found herself iu the presence of her visitors. She took in at a glance the elegance and quality of the lady's apparel -the diamonds that sparkled like suns, that depended from her shell-like ears and on her dainty white hands. 'Madame Delmar saw that the gentleman waS stately and of commanding presence--evidently a, thorough gentleman, and wealthy. The wife was loorcing into madame's face, her own face turning from red to white. There never was'a grimmer sight than Madame Delmar presented as she advanced to greet them. She was rigidly upright, as one encased in strong whalebone; there Wab not one bend in her. Her , hair was iron-grey, drawn etraight back from her temples into a little knot at the back of her head; her drese was iron-grey, ill made, ill fitting, with big horn buttons down the terribly long, straight waist that ended in a welting cord just where the too ample skirt began. A sudden fear filled Mr.,. Brandon's anxious heart. Did her beautiful little Doris have a happy li e of it here with this grim, austere • madame? In a few, brief words Hulbert Brandon told his .story—the story that stunned more like a weird I romance than reality to madame's astounding ears.. "My dear Hulbert, ask madame to send for Doris to come to us at once 1'" cried Mrs. Brandon, excitedly. "Don't you see the moments are like hours to me? I must ,eo my child.". And, with a heart throbbing almost as painfully as his wile's, Mr. Brandon asked that Doris might be brought to them wiihout a moment's unneces- sary delay. "I am a wealthy man," he said, with emotion. "I will make you a rich woman ft* life for the care you have oesLowed upon our littie D:als.22 ,</p>
        <p>They both saw Madame Delmar's face turn from red to white, then deepen into a dull grey. How could she tell them? How dare she face them and tell them that, less than two months ago, she had driven the poor, homeless, friendless girl from her door—driven her forthin the storm and darkness of the terrible night, to live or to die, as God saw fit? Closed her doors against poor, helpless, hapless Doris, and forced her to face the cruel world? She had the grace to feel ashamed. A terrible fear seized her as to the .punishment that might be inflicted upon her when they discovered what she had. done. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Warring and tyrannizing over a helpless, lone girl was one thing, and answering to a stern, outraged father for those offences was quite another matter. Words seemed to die on her lips. "You do not answer, madame," cried Hulbert Brandon, springing forward. "Is Doris ill? Has anything befallen our child? For the love of Heaven, speME, I implore you I See, my wife is almost fainting with anxiety 1" " I—l suppose I must speak sooner or later," faltered madame, huskily, hiding her ghastly face in her shaking hands: "Try and be prepared for a bitter blow."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Brandon reeled and fell backward unconscious in her husband's arms. Thus she was saved from hearing the cruel falsehood that fell so glibly from Madame Delmar's In Heaven's name speak !"cried Mr. Brandon, hoarsely. "Suspense is killing me. I cannot endure it. What of Doris ?'</p>
        <p>"She' is not here. She is gone 1" muttered madame.</p>
        <p>"Gone !" he repeated, hoarsely. understand."</p>
        <p>" do not</p>
        <p>"She left the seminary two months ago, stole away at midnight under the cover of darkness. She eloped with some one. Whom, or where they met or went, I know not. This is all I can tell you. I am sarry for you, sir. I always loved Doris, dear—sweet little Doris." "Oh, my God! my God groaned the unhappy father, "have we returned for this! It will kill my poor wife. It would be better if the girl had died in her infancy. From this hour I disown her. Henceforth I have no daughter. I will not search for her. Let the girl follow the path she has chosen."</p>
        <p>CRAFTER XXII. AN ADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>MADAME DELNAB, the dignified principal of the young ladies' seminary, felt considerably purturbed at the strange turn affairs were taking. After her visitors had taken their leave she quickly sought her brother and told him the whole story. "If the girl had been here they might have settled a handsome annunity on me for life for providing for her," she groaned. "Yes, by send- ing her away I have lost a little fortune. Was there ever such an unlucky contretemps 1"</p>
        <p>_ "It serves you right, I should say," replied John Delmar, "for turning poor little Doris away from home in the bitter storm that night."</p>
        <p>"Who Would have supposed, after ,all these long years, her parents would have claimed her, and that the creature I had always looked upon as a dependent and a burden—bora to be my especial cross—was in reality, a millionaire's daughter, heiress to a million of money? Oh, dear, if I had , but known that! I would give the world to find Doris again, the poor, dear girl! I—l—am afraid I was a little too harsh with her, John. I never meant to turn her from our door out into the cold world that stormy night. I only meant to frighten her into explaining where she had been. I never thought she would take me at my word and go. Oh, dear oh, dear! if I could but find her." "You can never tell when you are entertaining an angel unawares," said John, bluntly. " The poor child had a hard enough life of it here. She was a girl of spirit. I wonder she did not rebel long before; you were so cross, so cruel, with the child." "I meant only to discipline the girl properly," faltered madame, her florid face flushed guiltily. "One must be very strict to keep up the reputa- tion of a fashionable seminary for young ladies." "There ought to be a line drawn somewhere between severity and strictness," declared John, emphatically. Would you reely care to see little Doris again ?" he asked, abruptly, ' eyeing madame curiously. She started to her feet, forgetting her usual calm' demeanour, her face turning from white to red in her great excitement. "Do you know where she is, John?" she asked, quickly. "There is something you are trying to keep hack from me—something you are trying to hide front me," she declared. "I see it in your face." "It is fortunate that I do know where she is, that I may restore her to her parents," answered John Delmar, gravely. Madame Delmar was too astonished—too amazed —for words. She listenedlikt one dumbfounded, while her brother related tiNt pitiful story of how he had followed poor little Doi is out into the storm, begging her to return, and how she had persistently rd l used.</p>
        <p>"Madame has turned me from her door," she Bobbed, 'and never again will I willingly cross her threshold." And, finding all remonstrance useless, how he had at length given Doris a letter to the Granvilles in New York, and she had gone on there. "I shall go to New York at once and fetch her back,' (colored madame; and, without delay, she put herciecision into execution. To het dismay, she learned, as Doris had learned before her, that they had left the city long since. •What, then, had become of Doris? Cast adrift, homeless, friendless, on the great desolate streets of New York, where had she gone? What had become of her? If ill had befallen poor, pretty Doris, the guilty, heartless woman felt that Heaven would hold her accountable for it. Meanwhile, Mr. Brandon and his heart-sick wife had returned to their elegant villa, on the banks of the St. Lawrence. But, from the hour in which they turned from Madame Delmar's seminary, Mrs. Brandon had grown rapidly 111,—a brain fever had resulted from the shock, and the deep, keen bitter disappointment she had sustained in learning of the flight of her beautiful young daughter from the seminary while she was on her way to claim her, after long years of separation. Her distracted cries for Doris—only for Doris— were pitiful to hear. This was her one dream by day and by night. The bereaved father asked God to pardon him for the rash vow he had uttered against his fair young daughter in the bitter anger of the moment. He made strenuous efforts to find her; stilPit was all to no purpose; if the earth had opened and swallowed her, she could not have been more com- pletely lost to the world. And, lying on her sick bed, the poor mother turned her face to the wall, refusing to be comforted. Mr. 13randou was in aospair. He quite believed they would never be able to find Doris; but lie never expressed this thought to his wife. Her dream was never to be realised. She died with the name of "Doris" on her cold, stiffen-</p>
        <p>ing lips. In a few months' time her husband Was called upon to join her. He was a victim to an accident; a train on which he wets a passenger had been hurled down an embankment. He was picked up in a dying condition, and was barely able to make his will in favour of his long lost and missing daughter Doris before death claimed him. Then it was 'discovered that the gentleman, knottn as plain Mr. Hulbert Brandon, was, in reality, Hulbert Brandon Fielding, son of the late Lord Fielding, of Loam Abbey, ,Eng- lan d. Dr. Lancaster, of New York city, was named executor of the will. It was well the doctor knew the sad history of Lord Fielding's son; how he had returned with his wife, after long years of absence,,to td ii his child, mid the hard experience he had met with. . "Of course, 1 will do my best to find Niss Field- ing, the missing heiress,' said the doctor, thought- as he strolled leisurely down Broadway one morning, "but I fear very iii4ch it will be a work of years.' "It would be very fortunate if the young girl is modest and well-mannered," said the doctor's wife to her husband one day, "and more fortunate still if our son Karl would fall in love with her." " Heaven forbid ! groaned the doctor. "By all I can learn of her history from Madame Delmar, the very estimable principal of the seminary where she passed her early life, she is a regular little tartar, quite a barbarian, in fact, and she capped the climax by wilfully e'oping with some on from boarding-school. No doubt it is some shiftless vagai,4.,nd Whom the girl has fallen in love with arid m,cried."' "she is heiress to a million l" sighed the doctor's . wife, "and I should have liked our Karl to marry ' an heiress."</p>
        <p>time enough yet, declared thtfr doctor. "He is only twenty-four now. Men Would be better off it .they did not marry until thirty." Meanwhile, fate had destined long since that; the doctor's_handtiorne son and Doris should Meet. In explaining how it occurred, we must go back to Doris, and that night on Which she had left Thorn- ton Villa for ever, turning her face toward the crowded streets of New York. Hour after hour Doris traversed the sunlit street's, sick at heart, and discouraged, as many a young girl has been before. Why not go to the lady at the d ageelni ought atruck Doris. cy who once before,' in her time of need, had. collie to her d t h</p>
        <p>rescue ? Doris remembered the address, but it was so far Up town that she would have to take a car to reach</p>
        <p>As she stopped upon the riatform the car gave a sudden lurch -forward, precipitating Doris to ine roadway. The wheel would have passed over the tiny. foot that fell ivithin a hair's breadth of and crushed it, if it hitclnot been for the timerY: assistance of a young man, fortunately close at handt . who sprang forward as in an instant of time, tha seemed an eternity to the horror-strichen, Pal?: used spectators, and succeeded in rescuing lovely young girl from her perilous position. t l'or a moment Doris was 'quite stunned laY wb had transpired. • "I hope you are not hurt," said her rescuer, . kindly, as he assisted her to • her feet. A sharp exclamation broke • from Doris's lips, and she would !lave fallen to the. paverool again had he not put out his strong arra ell caught leer. " tun afraid my foot is sprained by t— fall," she faltered. "I —I cannot stand." "Let me' call a carriage for you, and take y‘ov.o tu° your home," said the young man, eagerly. are not able to stand." "No, no," faltered Doris, hiding her face in her hands, and bursting into tears. "Surely you may trust me ;I am a gentlentehiof he said courteously. "lam Karl Lancaster, !Is, , Dr. Lancaster, of No.—Lexington Avenue. am a doctor and a surgeon: Believe me, if, as jcihe fear, your foot or ankle is sprained, I eallupei.. of assistance to you; I beg of ,you do not tate." risia And he called a passing cab as he spo-ei her despite Doris's incoherent protestations, liftee his and placed her on the soft cushion, and Welt seat beside her. "Now, if you will give the driver your nionbetrl; please," he said, "we will soon have you '6l . your friends." oe "I—i tried to explain before, but, si Y r, f would not listen,” sobbed Doris, in the keenest; . embarrassment. " I—l am, a stranger le Tis"%c- es York. I have no relatives, no friends here. or just on my way up-towiu to the e,nployresb. 'agencSt, to apply for a position; and now i --° ; Heaven help me !—if my foot is spran ed wha shall Ido ?" she moaned. . Thip was certainly an extraordinary dilemma' but Mr. Litneaster was equal to the etnergencY• jet, "In that case I may still hope of being of servg to you," he answered. "You must put y Ors" _under my charge ; will you ?" "I have no right to be a burden upon t entirely he Oen- ness of a stranger," sobbed Doris, piteously. no ; I could not." But, under the circumstances, you must all°f; me to act for you," said Karl. " Believe 1126,,,1 ,will be a phase re ,to aid you," he added, cartlest'-',! and as he looked down into the fair girlish fiae%t himself told himelf never, in all his life, had he be'iej. a being so gloriously fair. (To be continued).</p>
