      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MEMORIAL TO LORD KIMBERLEY AND MR.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL TO LORD KIMBERLEY AND MR.</p>
        <p>ciLADS'IONE.</p>
        <p>W;cliffe Congregational Church was, nn Sun ing. unu..sua:ly wil filled, whyrt aceordingCu, ••• ment the Rev: John Yongo,(pastnr),</p>
        <p>arid denunciatory disceuree'sM the "subj7-se •‘ Mous massacre of Christians in Atm-ilia, duty in relation to it." For tile words 0 t rev. geutleman took " 0 Lord U d, to %ell v anise bslergeth ! 0 tied, to whom n veny,ekt vie shew 'Filyself. Lift up thyself, thou Judge s ; render a reward vs the proud. Lord how I wieked trump!, ? How long shall thee utl,r hard thing ? and all the workers of in q • • ,st- themselves ? They break in pieces Thy e, and afflict Thine heritage. They slay s t the strauger and murder the fatherless, ,i,es s sas, The Lord shall notsee, neither shall the G el regard it."—Paalin 94, verses 1 to 7. Atter referring in explamatary terns to themesmine Cf the 94th Psalin and the words of the text, tseet her the natural feelings of indignant displeasure itsain 4 ties doers of unrighteousness. the rev. gentleman said it s.yss because leelings ef that ~rder were called for arp!jii- fled, that he had read the ahovisyerses that ci,nit, 'rite story that bad to be told suggested thoughts ant ,s• ins I such, as the text contained. It was a a: nr: that con- strained them to invoke theinterference s)f U d ; arid even to plead for His just retribution. It was a suns, moreover, that called all Christian peeples may Vifql people, who had in them any humanity, to consider the- need and the method of effectual intervention. Bevier. tured to say that the pitiful and pitiable cry of ontraee,l,t pillaged, multilated, defiled, and massacred Anneni t, Isudly called, for the indignant attention of the v.•hole civilized world, and especially did it call far tie:inter- vention of this country. It was to be noted with ea, is- faction that Dr. Percival, tee new Bishop of Herefidd, preaching the ether deg on the oceS.io,l of his episcopal enthronement in Hereford cathe- dral, made emphatic reference to that ' gess- tion. Continuing, Mr. Yongo said that thes. events occurring in Armenia were a rep ,tition'of what took place 19 years ago in Bulgaria. Mails. ~t them would remember the fury and indignation, which raged through this country in 1876, when Mr Gladetetie tenk up the cause of that oppressed nationality, and-led the movement for its telief and deliverance ; the let a of bloodshed, vielence, lust and infamy which this nal ion then listened to turned its heart sick; end comnelled what was in many cases the conviction that the power of the Turkish rule in Bulgaria must code to sn end. Mr. Gladstone was severely criticised and crinsiir.st fir his famous declaration that the Turk must he h inidM I " bag and baggage" from Bulgaria, but his chi iss had had the candour since to admit that hp was erHrsly right in his view. As they now recalled those days ot in- dignant excitement and remembered the power e:certeti by that one fearless statesman for the . delivsrance of Bulgaria, they could only wish that, now, even in his most advanced age, he cosild be allowed to follow the limpulses that they believed were beating in his heart, and to raise once niore the voice which would yet he found the most potent of all living voices, in as,ertien of human rights arid liberties, and in denunciation of the fiendish policies Hi the tyrannical Turk —Mr. Veneta then quoted from a book by the Rev. D. F. flreene, M.A., a missionary who had until quite recently been resident in Armenia, the geographical seat of the rapid- qes and whiz!' was stated to be just now " the darkest spot upon the face of the globe.' In populai ion the Armenians were about four millions and entirely de- prived of the use of arms,the possession of which NOSS a serious crime. He showed that the chronic eeryth Se, of Armenia had been and was that of universal nnereasinn, which, said Mr. Greene " was now finding its climax and was crushing the life out of the people."' To further illustrate the frightful condition of thins in that country the preacher detailed some of the horrible Cr-;elties ant outrages which had been committed, and said that since 1822 Turkey bad done little else than pins -elite, harass and massacre its dependent people. Mr. Gladstone had pointed out that during his own lifetime Tuiikey had had its area of empire diminished in Europe by more than one [kit, and why ?—simply because of 'its Mis- deeds—a great record written by the hand If Almighty God against " injustice, against lust, aeainst the most abominable cruelty." It had been estimated that since 1822, in five successive series of perseentinns, Turkey had massacred 93,000 of its Christian populations, and it was now still sproceedinet in its career of rapine, lust, n nal blond. Since September 26 Taal'. 40 villages had' been des. troyed and probably 16,000 people macs inns&amp; There could be no doubt that this stricken land anti people of Armenia, dazed withlhorror, quivering with aqnnr, de- luged with blood, made the most forcible of ail conceiv- able appeals to their humane and Christian feeling. As was said at the outset, it was a case which made them thankful that there was a God who jedeerh, and who would assuredly avenge his people thongh ir might seem as it He long delayed,. They could well' ithrigine how these persecuted, disffononred, and maddened torios ple called upon God as the Ged of vengeance to "chow Himself." What resource had they other than presser ? They were helpiese. Bet now that their cry and their sorrow had come to their ears, it became a most urgent question whether their cry to God mieht not find answer through the arousing indignation and symnathy at eeery Christian. The preacher then proceeded to ask" What can he done ?" What can be ought to he. Much might ts, achieved by the expression of public opinion and popular , Christian sentiments It was to help such expression that he preached that discourse. Many simi- lar sermons were being preached. throliehreit, the- lend, and a great national renresentative meeting was to he held in London on the 7th May, over which the Mike of Argyle was to preside, and at which there had been I some hone that Mr. Gladstone might vet be led to speak; ' and could this country from and to end be again aroused as it • was in 1876 about. Bulgaria, the wickedness of the wicked would come once again to a check. Public opinion should he made to press very strenuously upon the Government. Lord Kimberley, the Foreign Secretary, recently assured a deputati in that waited upon him that the Govern- ' ment were frilly alive -to the situation, but it was a delicate one and full of difficulty, and it was imp-retire s to proceed with caution. Usually MrYenee did not follow the lead of the" Daily Telegraph." but he was hound to agree with their words from a recent artiele —" There cannot he a doubt as to the duty of the Foreign Office. The Parliament must be bid at once and uninistahably to put a wholesale stop to these infernal persecutions, not next week or next month, or next year. but new. These horrors must cease." It seemed to the rev. gentleman that renresentatinne shetild he made to the Government in favour of the nemployment of emery reasonable intervention to prevent the 'proerese and the repetition of these atrocities. Indeerl.thet enneared to hea matter of the most sacred dute,and fort his reason. It 1876 an AnelreTurkish Convention was entered into just before the treaty of Berlin,by which the Sultan pro- mised England t) introduce necessary reforms into the government and for the protection of the Christian fi,nrl other suSjecte of the Porte in these territorinr, and in order to enable England to make needful provisions for executing her engagement, namely, the keeping., nf Russia out of Armenia, the Sultan assigned the island-of Cyprus to be occupied and possessed by Eng- land. Then followed the Berlin Treatv.by which religious . liberty and the Public exercise of all forms of religion were euaranteed to Bulgaria and other provinces and to all Turkish subjects throughout the emnire. The eiit article et that treaty bound Turkey to carry omit wit bent further delay improvements and reforms necessary in Armenia, and to guarantee the security of the country from further molestation. That whole treaty had been systematically broken by Turkey. It should, therefore he the duty of the other contracting Powers to bring Turkey to book, and it should he the Omelet dirty of England to see that the terms of the Convention between tis and Turkey should be respected and fulfilled. Doubt- less, the question was beset with manifold difficulties and dangers. hut if there was need for actin,. in 1878 there was yet greater need in 1895, and if in 1876 action' issued in beneficial results, they might expect similar conaequences to follow now. Why, in teal fact, they , ought as a nation to have more impressively intervened I Term Rem Instead of that, tn avoid diffieult complica- tions they had been practically propping up the Turkish s power. In so doing they had " done a great wrong to the nation which were tinder iti yoke, and that wrens, 'which they had had themselves done it was their duty to undo." So far from being now better off than at the time of that treaty, Armenia was infinitely worse. For thetehe responsibility !merely lay at our door. That was la grave fact needing most serious consideration. The whole case, therefore, seemed to warrant the very general adoption of an untasnal procedure, and he sug- gested that besides sending the expression of an AMXMLIS : interest in the deliverance of Armenia to the meeting already referred to, they alionld direct their crienmtinice- tion also to the Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs These constitutional courses were (Men to all com- munities of pimple in this kingdom, and if ever an occa- sion had arisen in which the churches should address the Government it was now, when fellow Christiana, whom by treaty they hai undertaken to preterit. were being oppressed and massacred almost to extinction. That was the time to sneak and act in tho' Name of Christ, and for the welfare of down-trodden men. 1 On concluding his discourse, which, it is needless to say was listened to with rapt attention. Mr. Ynnea read ! the following memorial t•—• That this eengreeatinn as- sembled on Sunday evening, April 21, 1895. in Wynliffe Congregational ("herein Warrington; haying listened to a statement of the recent and present events in Armenia, in which the Christian people of that emin.re are suffer- ing horrible outrage, torture, imprisonment, end mass- acre, desires to join in the wiciels--spreading expression , of indignant abhorrence of such awful inhumanity and of strongest denunciation of the Government under whose sanction it has been perpetrated. It wishes also to express its conviction that, in view of our treaty obligations as a nation, we are solemnly bland to take i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>TH E E KAM:I-NMI, SATURDAY, APRIL 27, 1. VISIT OF Tlil4] REV. DR. DALLINGER TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TH E E KAM:I-NMI, SATURDAY, APRIL 27, 1. VISIT OF Tlil4] REV. DR. DALLINGER TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>all possible and immediate measures along with other Europelln Powers to stop these outrages and to Make. them impossible of repstition. This congregation would respectfully but urgently call upon her Majesty's Government to leave no reasonable means unused or de- kyed that can tend tia the accomplishment of this end. it'd this congregation fervently and eat nestly pravF,may God speedily deliver and save Armenia." This having been done, ho asked those in favour of it being for- warded to the London meeting, Lord Kimberley and Mr. Gladstone to hold up their right hand. The con- gregation without exception did so, and the memorial was 'accordingly forwarded in due course.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON SPIDERS.</p>
        <p>The Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour in connection with Bold-st. WesleyaniChurch have during the winter season been the mei* of bringing some eminent lecturers and preachers to Warrington-,but they surpassed all their previous ventures on Sunday and Monday, when they were successful in inviting the Rev. Dr. Dellinger to preach and lecture. The doctor has made a name second to none in microscopical research, and the society are to be congratulated upon being suc- cessful in bringing such an eminent scientist to Warring- ton. They have been well repaid, for at each service on Sunday and also at thelecture on Menday,there were large audiences. The rev. gentleman preached two eloquent sermons on Sunday, the morning discourse being based upon the words "These ought ye to have done, and not to leave the other undone," and the evening, "In dillgence not slothful, fervent in spirit, serving the Lord." During the evening service, the choir sang an anthem very efficiently, Mr. Levi Richasdson, taking the solo part. The schoolroom was crowded on • Monday evening to hear Dr. Dellinger's lecture upon "Spiders, their work and their wisdom, with a glance at mind in animals." The lecture was illustrated by splendid transparencies taken from nature. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. KIRWAN expressed his regret that Mr. T. Glazebrook Rylands, J.P., who had been announced to take the chair that evening was unable to be present owing to the state of his health. he was; however,glad to say that he had been successful in prevailing upon Dr. T. Starkey Smith, J.P. to take his place. Dr. SMITH said that his duties that evening were of an informal character. Dr. Dellinger was well-known throughout the world, and it was unnecessary to intro- duce him to a Warrington audience as he had been to Warringten a few years ago. He was sorry that Mr. Rylands could not be present that evening, but it would not have been wise for Mr. Ryladds to have taken any risk in coming. Dr. DALLINGER,WhO was received with loud applause, said that the spider was very rarely an object of sym- pathy and consideration. Very mach more frequently it was the subject of loathing and disgust. The ant from the earliest times had commanded the interest and the admiration of mankind, and the bee, and even the wasp bad received their increasing study and respectful thought. But the spider, large or small, brilliant or sombre, in the house, in the garden, or at large, was the bugbear of the mistress and the maid. No doubt reasons could very readily be adduced for all this, but, in his judgment, they might all be summed up in one brief sentence, gamely, inherited 'prejudice. That this was in no sense justified, he would try to establish, by the faces which were to be given concern- ing spiders. His desire was to enable them to judge of that for themselves. The Greek word for spider was Arachne, and this was the name of a char- acter in mythology who was turned into a spider, and it was very evident that men in olden times learned much from the spider. The Hebrew 'word for it meant an agile weaver, and the German word Wilms came from a verb which meant to spin. The Swedish word spindler came from the same origin, add from it eame the English words spider and spinster, both of which meant precisely the same. (Laughter.) Spiders were objects of deep interest to all students of nature. Of course the spiders were by no mmHg the only spinners. The silk- worm and the caterpillar wove webs, but the spider had the external organs of her craft, and in that respect, it was a most remarkable creature. He must begin by asking what the spider really was. The so-called red spider which frequented greenhouses, and the harvest spiders of ,the fields were not spiders at all, but were a species of mite. The spider was not an insect. A spider was born a snider, na matter how minute it might be, and the head of the spider was always fast to itsthorax, and it bad a large abdomen. The lecturer then pro- ceeded to throw on the screen representations of the red mite, the harvest mite, and various spiders. Tbere were two classes of spiders, the sedentary and the wandering. The fiermer wove webs wherewith to catch their prey, and the latter leaped and sprang upon their prey, and were hunters. Dr. Dellinger then explained by means of illustrations the organs by which the spiders wove their wonderful webs, and also described the methodby which she wove the web. This portion of the lecture was particularly interesting. Coming to the subject of mind, he said that he was certain that the spider worked with a design and undoubtedly showed evidence of reason. He gave some strong evidence in support of his contention. The feet of the spider played a very important part in the spinning capacity, and the lecturer remarked that it was because the spider was a weaver, and net a glutton, that she was so strangely shaped and had such a large ebdomen., Another wonder- ful point was the ingenuity which the spider displayed in building snares in apparently impossible places. One of the places a view of which was thrown on the screen, was 15ft. apart. How was the bridge carried across the water ; hew, in fact, was the suspension bridge made ? The spider was a great deal heavier than the air, and it she threw herself .nte it sink,while finally, a spider dare not woe: he -lf water,which was absolutely inimical to I; r. , ther,fare, did she cross the interval ? He wo. it lin . eee used silk so light that it rose like a bele 'n t au She fixed herself firmly to her suppoi t,enet ell • nese r attempted to lay the cord until she knew then teas a ch Moot breeze from where she was standing to the pint towards which she wanted the other erne of the cons to go. It was a kind of a kite which wee beewn Cut suite it caught a point on the ether side te whicleehr wished to attach it. Then she hauled thee ‘rd In, tome: it tight, and it formed a suspension bridge. He had known spans 20ft. wide and the web and its bupports were absolutely correct genmetr;cally and architecturally. The largest span be had ever seeit; a -view of which was thrown on the screen, was 25 feet 'wide, Ind in this case also a perfect web was for 'rrat was a clear and beautiful illustration which thuy should not allow to escape from their minds, making it clear that even a common spider was not without the elementary tracings of what they call reasoning pcwer, and it was by that means that much of her work was accomplished. (Ap- plause.) The lecturer showed the rapidity with which the spider did ber work be moving views thrown on the screen :and, alluding to the 'charge, that she was " a little brute," he said he had carefully considered the matter, and there was not an animal any- where that was not more or less directly dependant upon' other animals or living things for its own existence. Ile was obliged' to include him- self and the audience in that categery. (Laughter.) In the case of the spider it did not simply live to eat. He had read of men in history who had done that. It was clear that the spider ate to spin, and there were lots of men who did not do that. (Renewed laughter.) Having ni trouble peopleron tp remarked that he had seen a beautiful web constructed in 37 minutes, and having shown by another moving illustration on the screen how the viler saved herself from destruction when, after an encounter with a powerful enemy, she had been thrown into the water, Dr. Dellinger referred to the building operations under water, to the Ingenious .way in which the spider cheated her enemies, and to the method adapted of storing food. In conclusion, he said that the spiders were workers, and inasmuch as wisdom was after all nothing more than the right application of knowledge; he thought he had *Sid enough to show that so far as the spider knew she ape)ied it wisely in every- thing she did. (Applause.) ' Dr. SMITH expressed the opinVin that the lecture was the most instructive they had had for some considerable time in Warrington. Councillor L. GREENING premed a vote of thanks to Dr. Dellinger. He said that the lecturer had a remark- able way of putting a scientific subject into popular language. He was sure that they owed Dr. Dellinger a hearty vote of thanks for conainz that day, and he also thought that the committee of management ought to be thanked for giving the Warrinwtsrbe:o of having such a subject dealt with in pnpular language. stHoh He was youngao sf st thatreneime na r marvels lohs would al dlecturetake them. es tmi ge aot ef Couecillor MONKS seconded the motion. He said that he was sure they would all agree that the lecture had been about the best lecture on ray scientific subject they had ever had in Warrington. He hoped it would not be ' long before they had Dr. Dellinger with them again. The Rev. G. D. MAseme suppoeted the motion. He was glad that the society with wblich he had the honour to belong had brought down Dr. Eeallinger and that it had been so well supported. The resolution was put to the meeting and carried with acclamation, and Dr. Dellinger briefly replied. Da. Deeetereta, in reply, asked the audience to be impressed with the fact that what he had called their attention to that evening was one of the common ob- jects. He had not carried them into a profound subject, and had only shown them-'how profound a common eubject might become, and, se; had been hinted, itethey would only, as young people With good eyes, carry out the study in their own garden‘ then they would not be without clear evidence, of a Most delightful kind,of the marvels of which nature was so full. They had no doubt wondered why he had referred to the spider as " she" or "her " all the eveqing. The fact was that the male spider wan, as a rule, much smaller than the female, and frequently when the male paid his addresses to the female she turned round and gobbled him up. (Laughter.) (Applause.) be aat nouo ss eti opportunityn. ivf A vote of thanks was afterwards accorded the Chair- man, on the motion of the Rev. J. Kinks's. seconded by Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., and the meeting then terminated. _</p>
