      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BETTER THAN GOLD.</head>
        <p>BETTER THAN GOLD.</p>
        <p>; There are but few things in this world more highly appreciated or more eagerly sought after than gold. Why, Not bee- Use it is an attractive colour ;nt be- cause it wsighs more than any other metals ; not be- i!,usn'it lengthens life. No. But it brings the neces- saries, and if plentiful, the luxuries of life. It gives a certain social standing to , its poSsessor ; it brings friends, flattery,and smiles, and veneers •ungentlemanly, J !or even otherwise questionable actions, and smooths pathways otherwise rough.. But when the dear 'wife, or enn, or datiziit.-r lies stile:ten with pain and feebleness ; when aisetlie racks the mind and body ; when the d-ar one loses that natural buoyancy, sparkle, -energ-v, and vitality ; then the glitter of gold becomes almost repul- sive ; its uselessness brings a loathing. Mrs. Bullock, o Pursley, Gloucestershire., says " I suffered a ter- rible attack of rheumatism and kidney complaint, and failing to obtain relief, resorted to the use of Warner's Safe Cure, which I am plsaied to say cow, pleteiy cw.d , and I. feel it a duty..it,%uactknivaowrllieedrg,se the L.:4i.! 'ea</p>
        <p>1.,-ver tire of roconmiending it. It stanas ow:quailed. Had it been</p>
        <p>£5 pr bottle ...I 70. uld have considered it cheap at the pric,.." Better than gold.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHUIWEE ANNUAL SALE OF</p>
        <p>WORK.</p>
        <p>The Vintial sale of wcrk in connection with St. Paul's Chnrch, Warrington, was held on Monday, in the Ken- drick-st. schoolroom, when there weie a large number present. This sale Of work, which has now been held for several yeara,is to meet expenses in conneedon with the working 'of the parish, and has met with ever-in- creasing success each veer. The opening ceremony on , Monday was performed by Mrs. Beamont, and there were also present, the Revs. H. V. Piet (vicar), A. H. Townlend (curate), E.C. E. Carleton, J. J. Lane- , ham, and J. A. Cooil, Drs. Adams, White, and Nuttall,: Alderman Sutton, Councillor Smethurst, Messrs. T. Morris, R. Davies, W. Chantry, D. Jones, May, R. T. Smith, 11.• W. Finney, A. Pigot, A. G. Dent, G. G. Ains- worth, and L. Davies. Mesdames Parr, Picot, Greenall (Grappenhall Dail), E. Greenell, P. Greenall,H.Greenalle A. Fleet, J. C. Pigot, Pickmere, Sharpe, Reynolds, Nit. tell, Thrimas,D. Jones, F. Monks, W. B. Roherts,H. Mil- ling,L Greenine,and J. Harrison, Misses Comber, (Mid- dleton Hit), Monks, White and Pierpoint. • The Vicen, in introducing Mrs. Beamont, said that the opening ceremony was never a very long or laboured .me, because tile business they had to do was so. very important. The sale that day was to be opener] by Mrs. Beamemt, Whose name had been so long and so closely connected with St: Paul's parish, and with other good , works in connection with the church in Warrington. The sale of work in connection with that parish had : always been a surprise to everybody. including them- selves' as each year they did better than the previous one. The explanation of it he thought was that every- body knew that the sale was a necessity and not a mere luxury. There were some who were under the impres- sion that St. Paul's was a rich parish, but he need hardly tell those present that it was a very poor one indeed. In regard to the endowment of the church, they only received £BO per, annum, and with the exception of that £BO all the expenses, including his stipend had to come out of the present generation. St. Paul's was the poor- est parish in Warrington in that reseect. There was large number of organisations in full working order and it was to keep these going that the sale was being held. Flo therefore appealed for help to carry on the work In the future as they had done in the past. Mrs. BEAltonT said that it was always a great pleasure to meet her friends in St. Paul's. She hoped that Mr. and Mrs. Pigot would receive the help thry needed. Though the parish had lost a very gnod helper, those present could to some extent compensate for the inss,by verY cordially supporting, that sale of work which she now declared open. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. R. DAVIES proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Beamont for her presence that day. He thought that most of thope present knew that he was equally opposed to (Uses, abllshment and disenclowment. He did not care whether it was in Ireland or Wales or wherever it was, it was equally objectionable. but he was glad that in that parish. they had something to congratulate themselves upon, that not much harm would come to them by dis- endowment He thonght that most gentlemen present would be aware of the great controversy which was going on in the papers oa the merits of monoreetalism and bimetalism. Both sides were eqnally certain that they were rieht as most disputants were, and were ever ready to prophesy ruin if their nostrums were not car- ried ints effect. Ho might tell them that at that sale of work they were not at ell particular which way they leaned. They rather leaned towards the gold currency, hut silver would not be despised. and even the simple coppers, if they fell in showers heavy enough, would be welcome (laughter), hut should such a thing happen that theY•found at the bottom of the baasome paper with the cold black letters of £5 at the corner, they would at once epres their ideas in fivour of a paper currency. (Laughter.) In conclusion he hoped that the sale of work would be even more successful than in the previous years._ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. T. MORRIs seconded the motion. He hoped that Mrs. Beamont would live Ion; to continua. to do the good , work she was now doing. He wished they could secure many more ladies in the Church of England like Mrs. Beamont. He hoped that the sale of work would be a very successful one. The VIM said that they met under the shadow of a great sorrow which had fallen upon the parish during the lost month. He alluded to the death of Mrs.Chorley. He hoped and trusted that the work she did. and her in- finence and example for good Would leave a lasting impression upon those who worked for St. Paul's parish. The motion was then put and carried unanimously, after which the business began. The room was very artistically laid out. and the stalls looked very well as they were laden with useful and ornamental articles, and draped with art muslin. The following is the list of the stall holders :—Nn. 1 stall. .Mrs. Peacocks, Miss Gibson Smith. Mrs. E. Woods, and Mrs. Pigot (St. Helens) ; No. 2 stall, Miss Geddes, Mrs. Heddes,and Mdlle Blanchet ; No. 3 stall,Mrs. Smethurst, Mrs. Chantrey, Mrs. James Richardson, Mrs. Amos, the Misses Richardson, Miss A. Smethurst, and the Misses Chantrev ; No. 4 stall, Mrs. Morris, Miss Conbrough,and Miss Button ; No. 5 stall, Mrs. Finney, and Milts Pinney; No. 6 stall, Miss Stelfox,Miss Crallr, and Miss Robinson; refreshment stall, Miss Westwell, Miss Braids, Miss Shawcross, Mrs. Fryer, Miss Piston, Miss Hazelden„Miss Houghton, Miss SCWArt (Wigan) bran pie, Miss E. Picton, and Miss Rathhone ; Bower stall, Miss A. Smet• hurst, and Miss D. Reed. The sale was only ooened for one day,and the total receipts amounted to £156 133. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRIV GTON- YMNA SLUM.</head>
        <p>WARRIV GTON- YMNA SLUM.</p>
        <p>LADIES' PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a prize medal competition was held in the Warrington Gymasium,Palmyra-square. The proceedings were confined to members of aye ladies' class, from which there were live competitors, Miss K. Brundrit, Miss L. Allen, Miss E. Daintith, Miss Dakiu, and Miss F. Jones. Mr. Kinsley, the instructor, con- ducted these ladies in their exercises and the judge was Mr. Haslain. of Manchester, the Northern County champion. The competition consisted of dumbell, parallel bar, bar-bell, vaulting-horse, ladder, jumping, club and rope climbing exercises. Each competitor did her best to secure the maximum number of marks-20 in everything, and though each excelled in different subjects the total result showed very little difference. Individually the marks scored were as follows :-Miss Brundrit : dumbells, 20 marks, parallel bars, 19k, bar- bells, 19k, vaulting horse, 20, ladder, 19, jumping, 19, 'clubs, 19k, rope climbing, 7 ; Miss Allen : dumbells, 20, parahet bars, 18. bar-bells, 19k, vaulting horse, 18, ladder. 20, jumping, 12, clubs, 2-0,. rope climbing, 18 ; Miss D iintith : duMbells, 19k, parallel-bar, 17i, bar- bells, 19. vaulting horse' 18, ladder, 18, jumping, 14, clubs, 19, rope climbing (two lengths in succession),2o ; Miss Dakin : dumbells. 19, parallel bars, 18k, har-bells, 18. vaulting horse, 17, ladder, 17k, jumping, 4, clubs, 17i, rope climbing, 10 ; Miss Junes : dumbells, 20, parallel burs, 20, bsr-bells,2o,vaulting horse, 18i, ladder, 19, jumping, 12, clubs, 19j, r-pe-climbing (two lengths in succession), 20. The respective totals were,therefore Miss Jones, 149 f, let prize,a handsome silver medal with gold centre ; Miss Daintith, 148, 2nd prize„ engraved silver medal ; Miss Allen and Miss Brundrit,tied at 143 k, each, 3rd prize, smaller silver medal ; Miss Dakin, Mr. Kinsley presented the medals to the deserving com- petitors at the end of the competition, and from the hearty way in which each exercise was taken up, it:was evident each young lady was determined to render her- self, if possible,entitled to the distinction of possessing a medal. A glance at the totals shows that the four win- ners did not vary much in skill as all were in the same " ten." Mr. Haslam had a difficult undertaking in dis- tinguishing between the competitors, but it was allowed that he had used very wise discrimination in awarding the marks. This competition will have a good effect, in that it will serve to quicken the waning interest, which s dun to the warmer weather, among the members.</p>
        <p>BE WISH IN nails Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Diseases of Kidneys. Bladder Stone. Sciatica, Rheumatism an Gout will find roe tic's Care in Holroy,Ps Gravel Pi Is, Try a small box and if not satisfied tour money Will be returned. Price Is. ;Id,. of all ofte,ntsts, or post free for 1% stamps. front Hormovu's Medical Hall, Checkheaton, Yorks. Do it be put off. If you cinnot get them write to the Pr. prietor and a box will be ut next post. —Adv. •</p>
