      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons reassembled an Monday with the new Speaker (Mr. W. C. Gra.t.r) in the chair. The second reading of the Fatttories and Workshops Bill was moved by Mr. ASQUITH. Mr. STUARF-WORTLEY, referr- ing to the suggested further restriction on children's labour, said this was not a time, when the country could afford to be toe free or too Quixotic in imposing fetters on'industry which were not imposed elsewhere. Oia the whole, he thought the HOME SECRETARY would meet with little resistance in passing the bill. Mr.Wiarra- LEY, while giving a general sun:, rt to the mea*ure,said he hoped it would be the last Factory Bill they would have for EOM time, as it was poisitde to legislate trade out of existence. A large number of Lancashire mem- bers would have strenuously opposed the Bill if it had been proposed to raise the half-time agb. Mr. BROAD- HURST recommended some enlargement of the scope of the Bill, including a provision raising the half-time age to twelve years. Mr. Scuwerna thought the age limit might well be raised to twelve. Mr. Awns, in the course of his reply to criticisms passed on the lill men- tioned that he proposed to add ia Committee a provision prohibiting for the future the use of underground pre- mises as bakehouses. He assurred ,hon. members that the Government would welcome apy practical sugges- tion made from any quarter of the House. Mr. TOMLIN- SON expressed a hope that the HOME SECRETARY would not commit himself to any enlargement of the half-time limit. Mr. BUXTON said if a proposal was made to raise the age from eleven to twelve and it met with a general assent of the House the Government would not oppose it. The second reading of the Bill was agreed to, and a motion by Mr. ASQUITH to refer the measure to the Grand Committee on Trade was carried by 67 to 27.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords the formal ceremony of ex- pressing the QUEEN'S approval of the election of Mr. GULLY as the new Speaker of the House of Commons was performed by the Lords Commissioners, Mr. GULLY attended, and according to the customary formala, sub- mitted himself" in all humility to Her Majesty's Royal will and pleasure."</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>A HANDSOME PENSION FOR THE LATE SPEAKER.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Sir GREY, answering a question, said her Majesty's Govern- ment understood that the terms of peace between China and Japan did not include any exclusive trading privi- lege.</p>
        <p>Mr. CAMPBELL-BANNataterr, replying to a question put by Mr. A. C. MORTON as to the Commandership-in-Chief of the army, said he could only repeat what he said in geptember, 1893, that after the recommendations of the Royal Commission on the subject he could not conceive that any future appointment of a permanent nature c•uld be made.</p>
        <p>The House having gone into committeei Sir W. Han. COURT moved the grant of a pension of £4,000 a year to the late Speaker (Mr. PEEL. Mr. J. K. HARDIE moved as an amendment that the amount should be £l,OOO. On the question being put Mr. HARDIE challenged a divi- sion. Being called upon to name tellers, the hon, mem- ber said he must ask the Chairman to name one of the Clerks at the table to tell along with himself. The Chairman therefore said that as there was no other teller he declared that the " Ayes had it." The motion was carried amid cheers. The House then resumed. Mr. J. Emu moved— " That in the opinion of this House, in order to secure greater Parliamentary control over and an administra- tion of its objects more in harmony with public opinion, the Charity Commissien should be reconstituted and placed ass subordinate department under a Minister of State." Mr. H. W. Lawson seconded the motion. M . GRIFFITR-SOSCAWEN moved and Mr. S. NORTRCOTE seconded an amendment declaring that as a large part of the work of the Charity Commissioners was of a judicial or quasi-judicial character, it would be contrary to the public interest to subject the Commission to the direct control of Parliament or of a Minister responsible to Parliainent. Mr. F. STEVENSON (Parliamentary Charity Commissioner) said the unpopularity of the Commis- sioners was due to a great extent to the conditions im- posed upon them by statutes, and also to the fact that they were not responsible to the House. In view of anomatics of the present situation, he welcomed the motion before the House. Mr. ACLAND, 'after mentioning that the funds now'under the charge of the Charity Commission exceeded three millions a year 1 (of which about a million and a half related to chari- table matters, one million to educational matters, and half a million,to general purposes),said the Government were ready to support the motion. They believecrthat the change fore-shadowed in it would not increase par- tisanship or political bias, but would enable long-desired legislation to be passed. The House, was counted out while the subject was still under discussion. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday the second reading of the Tramways (Local Authorities) Bill was moved by Mr. Seawater:. He explained that the object of the measure was to facilitate the working of tram- ways by local authorities. It was proposed that when a tromwav had been completed or acquired by a local authority under the Act of 1870 the Board of Trade might, in the event of the local authority being unable to demise the tramway upon such terms as in the opinion of the Board would yield an adequate rent,grant a license to the local authority to work the tramway, and the latter might thereupon work the tramway, ac- quire such plant, &amp;c., as might be necessary, and take toile- Mr. BRYCE thought the proposal was entitled to the favourable consideration of the House, because it only made a part of the law a provision which the House was generally disposed to give to any authorities which applied for it. The bill WAS read a second time and re- ferred to a Select Committee. . Mr. T. W. RUSSELL moved the second reading of the Sale of Intoxicating Liquors (Ireland) Bill, thtignain ob- ject of which was to extend Sunday Closing to the five large towns in Ireland exempted from the operation of the existing Act. Mr. DRIIIIMOND seconded the motion. Mr. W. REDMOND moved the rejection of the measure, contending that the question was one which ought to be left fot the decision of Irishmen in Ireland. The second reading was carried by 168 votcs to 69. Mr CRILLY ob- jected to the proposal to refer the bill to the Standing Committee on Trade,as proposed by Mr. RUSSELL. The closure was then moved by Lord F. HAMILTON, and the SPEAKER assented. A division took place, and the clo- sure was carried by 149 votes to 60. A third division was taken on the motion that the bill should be referred to the Standing Committee on Trade,and resulted in the motion being carried by 151 votes to 68.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Devonshire in a letter to Mr. Hulse. M.P., expressos the hope that "the good sense of both sections of the Unionist party " will be sufficient to re- move existide misunderstandings and "to restore the alliance which has existed se long and with such benefit to tbe country.°</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
