      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>VOYAGE OF JABEZ BALFOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOYAGE OF JABEZ BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>The agents of the Prince line of steamers state that the Tartar Prince, lrom Buenos Ayres, with Inspector Forest and his prisoner, Jabez talfour, on board, was due at St. Vincent on Thursday. The vessel, after coal- ing, would in her usual course proceed thence to Dept- ford,slowing down at Graves-end to pick up her Thames pilot. The Tartar Prince is due at Deptford ten days after leaving St. Vincent, or about the 5b of May. The Government have, it is understood, not yet suggested any alterations of the vessel's course from S. Vincent to London ; but should they in the meantime desire to land BaHoar somewhere on the South coast, the instruc- tions to her commander will probably be altered. Up to the present, however, the Tartar Prince is expectod to come direct to the Thames.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY SCENE AT SALFORD *TOWN COUNCIL. •</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY SCENE AT SALFORD *TOWN COUNCIL. •</p>
        <p>An extraordinary scene took placo at a meeting of the Salford Town Councilbu Wedii, .day between Mr. Roper and Mr. Mandley, members of the Council and magis- trates of the borough. Whilst Mr. Mand!ey was sneak- ing Mr. Row r more than once called him a liar, and Mr. Mandley retorted by describing Mr. Roper as "a common-plase, vulgarfellow." Mr. Roper was seen to lift his hand, and Mr. Mandley. asserting that he had been assaulted, left the Council Chamber, threatening to take out a summons against Mr. Roper. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>TRAFFIC ON THE NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY.</head>
        <p>TRAFFIC ON THE NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>GREAT DIMINUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. Webb, locomotive superintendent of the London and North-Western Railway, addressing a large gathering of delegates representing the Mutual Insur- ance Society, at Crewe, on Monday afternoon, said the decrease of trade during the past year or two had re- sulted in a very large diminution of mileage on the North-Western Railway. In the first four months of the present year their engines had run 409,000 miles less, and this decrease reflected itself upon other trades in the country. The North-Western consumed 3,500 tons of coal everyday. He was just arranging the coal con- tracts, and he was asking for a quarter of a millicn tons of coal less than they had usually taken. The erike in the coal industry lost them a great volume of trade, and everything that was now being done be increase the cost of production of work in this cOuntry was of great advantage to the foreigner. If there was a revival of prosperity it would not be braught about by these wretched strikes, but by employers and employed put- ting their heads together and tryicig to settle things in a more rational way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BAPTIST UNION.</head>
        <p>THE BAPTIST UNION.</p>
        <p>SPRING ASSEMBLY.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the Spring Assembly of the Baptist Union of Great Britain and Ireland took place in London on Monday. The report of the Council for the year ending December 31, 1894, stated -that there had been an increase in the number of churches and chapels, the number of chapel seats, the number of members. Sunday school teachers and scholars, local preachers,and pastors in charge, and a slight decrease in the number of baptisms. In the evening be Rev. J. G. Greenough (Leicester), the president for the current year. delivered his inaugural address, his subject being " The message of the Puritans to the democracy of to-day." On Tuesday a paper entitled "The betting fever "was read by the Rev. J. B iillie, who urged that the duty of reducing the fascination of betting rested mainly with the churches; for the public press was powerless because it was eager to share the spoil of the gambler. Resolu- tions were adopted calling upon the Government to suppress betting, to take steps to prevent further atro- cities in Armenia, and to appoint a national bnard of conciliation for the settlement of trade disputes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>NOTES ON • GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON • GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATlTriETics.</p>
        <p>FoarnALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington has this week been operating against the two neighbouring towns of Widnes and Runcsrn' and as a result a win and a loss have to be put down to their account. They will, therefore, have to finish up the season with a balance of wins and losses on the wrong side, even if they will to-day against Barrow. The sea- son has not been such a one which Warrington have been accustomed to, and perhaps four months' rest will do them good.</p>
        <p>The match against Widnes on Saturday was an eye- opener. The Chemicals have done so well this season that at the best, a close game was expected. On the contrary, Warrington had the game well in hand from beginning to end, and the wonder is that they did not gain more than a goal and two tries. The game was one of the most open ones ever seen on the Warrington ground, and the passing between the Warrington half backs and three-quarters was a treat to watch. That Warrington has an excellent back division at the pre- sent time is patent to everybody, and let them have a chance and they will make thingsiturn.</p>
        <p>The forwards on both sides were very evenly matched, although Warrington got possession of the ball the often- est. Taylor was the most prominent forward on the field, and the try he gained was a splendid one. Turner. Saunders, Burrows, and Bailey also put in some good work. The Warrington half backs had more opportuni- ties than the opposing halves, and they made the best of their opportunities, passing out to the three-quarters with good judgment. Bate and Houghton,in fact played a capital half back game. The three-quarters were in fine fettle. All four passed and re-passed as though they had played together all the season. Although only Bur- ton scored, all four were at various times very near re- cording a winning point. Broady also -showed what a tremendous speed he has by catching Peters when he ap- parently had a walk in. Boscow's display at full back put Breman's completely in the shade. Me latter was completely off colour,especially towards the close of the game.</p>
        <p>The score of eleven points to five is scarcely a fair index of the game. There ought to have been a greater difference in the scores. Warrington may, however, be congratulated upon crossing the Widnes lines oftener than any other team this seasm. Widnes have an ex- cellent record, but a few matches like Saturday's would soon spoil it.</p>
        <p>In the match at Runcorn on Wednesday.Runcorn had all the luck. Taking th 3 game all through Warrington had as much of it as their oppnnents but failed to score. Winning the toss was a good. thing for Runcorn, for Warrington had to face a dazz,ing sun in the first half an well as a moderate wind. When sideßwere changed,the sun had gone down and the wind had ceased. The first try was a gift to Runcorn and also the goal from the mark. The second try was undoubtedly an excellent one and well earned. With ail these drawbacks. hos-- ever, Warrington ought still tu have scored, and would have scored but that a fatal lack of finish was again observable.</p>
        <p>Runcorn were undoubtedly the beat team in the first half and they never lost any chances of scoring. In lhe second half, however, Warrington had Almost three parts of the game, and minors were TO plentiful that people lost count of them. There must have been at least seven or bight scored by Warrington. Chis.is, of cousre, cold comfort for the Innsers, for they might have scored a hundred and still have Insp. The three-quarter line again played well when they had the chance but there was not nearly the same amount of free pa vsing av on Saturday. Boscow was in fine form, hut the half backs and forwards were not equal to the Runcornians. The most prominent of the forwards were Turner,Taylor Saundemand Bailey.</p>
        <p>Runcorn had the assistance of Crompton, an old mem- ber of the team and at present a. Yorkshire county man and also of Cooper, the imternational player. These with Dunbabin and Warder made a fine three-quarter line. Houghton was also in fine form. The Runcorn for- wards take some beating, and when the scrimmage is broken up, they have a knee, of passing almost as well as the three-quarters. It is here that the secret of their success lies. The game on Wednesday, taken as a whole was a fine game to watch, and it is to be hoped that these fixtures between the twe 'clubs will be annual affairs, and that old grievances will be wiped off the slate.</p>
        <p>To-day the Warrineton club close their season in a game with Barrow, the chsmpinns both•nf the second division of the Lancashire Rugby League. and of the North Western League. The advance of this team upon last season is astonishing, and Warringtnn have, there- fore, a stiff job before them. 7he remilt of the match at Warrington was a try each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CRICK RT.</head>
        <p>CRICK RT.</p>
        <p>The cricket season coi-n—M-PI-ta in earnest to-day, when the Warrington first team journey to Sefton to meet that club. It is to be hoped that they will have a more suc- cessful season this season than they had last. They have an attractive list of fixtures and the clubs they will meet sre,as follows : Sefton, Preston, Manchester. Broughton, Northern, Bolton, Liverpool, Oldham, Wigan, Little Lever, Llandudno and Brookiands. This is a n attractive I list, and should Warrington have a suceesqful season, it cannot be said that they have done so because they mot poor clubs.</p>
        <p>For Chronic coughs and colds and weak hums there is no greater remedy than Scott's Bmtilii;on, This preparation no only cures the local trouble, but it also strengthens the system FO that the cause ot all the difficuly j removed. This Is more than merely overcoming the loyal initation. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>CAMDEN WESLEYAN .SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>There were large congre.gatioos in St. Paul's W,esieyan Chapel on Sunday when the anniversary services in con- nection withCamderi Wesleyan Sanday school were held. The Rev. D.A. de Mouilpield was the preacher. At the morning service. he spoke specially to the Sunday school children present. At night he preached from the text ' "Whosoever will be great amcng ou let him be your minister." Matthew ass.l6v. He concluded by etating that on the Sunday School books were the names of 550 children, and that the annual- expense in carrying out the institution came to about £56., and he asked for liberal support. During the services the choir, under the 'Conductorship of Mr. R. Morris. and the children attend- ing the schools, who had been trained by Mr. S. Bazley, sang anthems and appropriate hymns. The collections realized over £3O. A meeting was held in the Public Hall, Ibincorn:en Monday evening. Mr. E. Itnison. J.P., occupied the ' chair, and there were on the platform, -the Revs. T. Brackenbeiy,W. George.J. H. l'ite and Pnsnett ;Messrs. P. Speakman, .T.P., C.C.. S. Taylor, J.P..C.C., F. J. Nor- man, J.P., S. Pope,S. B4ckett,R. Rawlinson, and others. —The Chairman, in the course of a short address, said • one of the meat pleasant features of the chairmanswp of the District Council was that he was introduced to inoi- viduals,institutione,end organisations,which under °raj!)_ ary circumstances he did not come into contact with. ft A could assure them that he had had no dirties that had given him greater pleasure than taking some part in that evening's proceedings. (Applause.) The alert for which the meeting was hehl was one very dear to him. What- ever differences of opinion they.might have they must 'is% iss ,hat there was a good deal of purely sem- ,•;no their day schools, aud tlaey could not be satisfied with the education children received there, and therefore that policy had vastly increased the importance of their Sunday schools. They must recognise that it was necessary that a child should know something of God who made it,and the Saviour who redeemed it, and that was the instruction which Sunday schools were specially instittited to give. Something more than secular educes tion was required to teach a childato be pore, honest and true ; and it' it must be copplete, instruction should he given which went to make up a perfect man. So long as Camden School was devoted to the attainment of that end,it was work in which every persen.in Runcorn collie' heartily wish God speed. (Applausea—The Rev. D. A. de Moulinied then delivered an address cn"Paris,political. social, religious " He said Paris from every point of view was keenle interesting. She was a chameleon city. for she had passed through more chanees itt one century than any other. It was a study of life, strong, fruitful and notential. Paris was the brain of France; it was the Parliament of France, and its intellectual life was repress nted by the press there, for of the 5,179 papera published in France nearly 2,000 were issued in tlie city under notice. The Fourth Estate had been under a cloud, hut this bad been removed, and it now stood in the first rank as a political force, news gatherer and distiihiator. Yet it was far behind the Enelish Press, andespecially in a provincial sense.. The political force of France to-day was the best she had ever had. Never had the country realized her own thoughts or ex- pressed her awn ideas better than at the present time. , He proceeded to deal with the social aspect of his sub- ject. Parisians were noted for politeness. He dealt in a humorous way with the fashions as well as the, cus- toms, and then spoke of the Sabbath, remarking that no one was allowed to work on that day. Although it was generally considered that there was na home life in Pang he could assure them that greater devotion between husband and wise and parents and c'eildren existed no- where more than there. With regard to immorality, he did not see in his nine years' residence there so much as he had seen in Regent-st., London. It was not forced upon them in its loathsome forms—they oupt go to seek • it if they desired to find it. As to religion it was charac- Jerised RS the most godless, impious, free-thinking, sceptic tl, atheistical city upon the face of the world. There might be a good deal of truth in this. There had been a revolt sosainst clericalism, and when they re- membered the outrages that had taken place in the name of religion, they could not be surprised that she should have declared herself as she had against the State Church. Rut from there to Atheism was is very long step. Paris was a very religious cit-, and the churches were packed to suffocation. There remained the deep solemn truth that the heart of the people was crying for God, and there was a multitude of men who were working for righteousness. (Applause )—A vote of thanks to the lecturer was passed on the motion of Mr. Pope, seconded by Mr. S. Beckett.—The Rev. D. A. do Mnuilpied acknowledged the compliment, and pea-lensed a vote of thanks to the Chairman.—Mr. SPeakman seconded the motinn,the Rev. T.Brackenbury supported, and it was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SHA,yING AS IT IS TAUGHT.</head>
        <p>SHA,yING AS IT IS TAUGHT.</p>
        <p>"Not that gentleman, William—l'll attend to him," said my regular barber, after I had been lathered And , the young assistant had stropped his razor. "You must keep to casual; for another week. Bow do we barbers learn to shave ? Well, it; stands to reason that our firEt customers owould have a rough time of it if we ' didn't have some practice first. I'll tell you how I teach my apprentices ; hut, of course, every master has his own way. After lathering, the great lesson is to learn how to handle the razor with firmness and light- ness, and once that is known, it's half the battle won. I first practice my lad on a ,glass soda-water bottle. He has to lather it thichly, and with the razor only, get all the soap off and make it quite glossy aeain. That gives him practice in rounding a surface safely; when he can do that, I make him lather and shave a brosm handle clean, without showing any cuts on the wood. Tee next few lessons are devoted to shaving a hairy surface—such as a clipped rabbit skin, or even a hairy vegetable leaf. This wants a deal of care. 'Hum he trip.% his hand on human skin—mine, ni course. I needlit say I have to stared a few cuts before my a pprentice can venture on the public ; hut even then,the first Lona-fidet shave he's put to he's bound ti feel a hit nervous over. So I never risk ioqing a regular customer by allowin, experiments to be tried mama him—. strays' I don't so much mind."—" Cassell's Saturday Journal."</p>
