      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>rfij E 'EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>rfij E 'EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 27, 1895.</p>
        <p>AN imp6itant addition to the Ship Canal commercial resources of Man- chester was made on Tuesday by the opening of commodious geld Air Stores. It is quite clear that whatever may be the experi- ences of the Ship Canal in the- immediate future the people Of Manchester still have a lively faith that all their anticipations will be realised in time to come. Otherwise it is not likely that the Corporation would have gone to the great addi- tional expense of erecting cold air stores which, it is said, are capable of b old jug from 80,000 to 100,000 carcases of sheep. . The stores will not, of course, be used exclusively for the accommodation of Ship Canal traffic. They will be utilised by lobal trade men—such as butchers,. fishmongers and fruiterets—dealin,g in perishable articles, who may have-their surplus stock preserved from decay over the week-end or till the next market day for a small charge.. But the main purpose of the stores is t&amp; cilitate the import of cold meat from Australia and merica by way of. the Ship Canal. Without lairages and cold storage it is impossi- ble for Manchester to ,participate in this particular - trade, but the storage has now been provided and it is expected that the lairages will be cornpleted within a few weeks. At the formal opening of the stores on Tuesday the prospects of the Canal were naturally alluded to rather frequently. Mr. REUBEN SPENCER, in proposing the toast of "The Ship Canal Company and Directors" re- ferredto the difficulties which the company had still to get over" with their neighhours ut War- rington" and in other places, and he added that "so far as Warrington were concerned, he was convinced there were men of high principle inside and outside the Corporation to whom those con- connected with the Ship Canal would not appeal in vain." This reference is conciliatory as far as it goes, but it is ambiguous. Time main question in dispute between Warrington and the Ship Canal Company is with reference to the navigation of the river, and it is a question, of very great im- portance to the whole teem and can har-dly be settled by an appeal to a few gentlemen whether they be inside or outside the Corporation: The report of Admiral Sir G. H. RICHARDS, acting Conservator of the Mersey, which we publish this week, certainly does not tend towards making the matter less serious than it was before. He states "that the tidal portion of the rive'r at Warrington and for sonic miles below it has silted up occasion- ing a considerable loss of depth, which has been partly caused by the diversicw of the upper waters from their normal"' course. Tale silting up has decreased the sectional area and tidal capacity of the river." Admiral RICRAUDS is also of the same opinion as those who navigate tin; river, that the tide does riot rise so high as iteised to do before the Canal was opened, and he adds that unless adequate measures, are taken, the river between Warrington and Runcorn must become aeno distant time unfit for navigable or other purposes. From the concluding part of Admiral RICHARDS' report it appears that it is not only with Warrington that the Canal Company.</p>
        <p>will have to reckon in connection with the naviga- tion question, for he declares that "the Mersey Conservancy is equally interested in the preserva- tion of the navigation of the river up to the tidal limits, and is, moreover, deeply. concerned*that the flow of the upper waters should be maintained /no, their normal channel in the interests of Mae estuary '1 itself." Apart from these knotty problems, which tho Canal Company will have to solve in one 'aid shicede or another, and which were, in fact, barelidu li to, the official speakers at the ceremony of Tees; day did not take a dismal view of the Prospects ° the Canal. Mr. J. K. Thriewarz, the managl director of the Canal Company, spoke in gTatefu and flattering terms of. the services which the Manchester Corporation had rendered his cow- pany, but hasn't some one defined gratitude as "a lively .sense of favours to come." Mr.BYTHIIIL evidently thinks that the Canal Company ifi • en; gaged in a fight against time. The turning of &amp;riffle from its old channel into a new one is ic; slow process, while the demand for dividends° the settlement of other obligations becomes more insistent as the months pass by. There ig cer- tainly great force in his appeals to the traders of Manchester and district not to delay their Or port of the Canal. ''''They have already seegred many advantages directly and indirectly thrcags the competition of the Canal with the old I:Oafs. of transport, but if that competition were v6tu drawn the old rates would soon !iv-appear.</p>
        <p>IT can hardly be said just ucr,v" rs Small Wars, peace prevails in our time. Cons of a warlike character sr° being carried on in several parts of the 1: though not, it must be admitted, on a verY scale. In two instances at least British force3:rit taking an active part. From the latest new;.iji appears that the expeditions sent by the in"l`pd Government into' Chitral to relieve the beleaguer„-,1 garrison of Englishmen and their Indian ese—is has successfully accomplished its task. Chitra ; , a district on the North-Western frontier of In—n and its wild inhabitants have received which seemed to be necessary, that even mountain fastnesses could not protect Who, ) from the long arm of British authority. I'b other other instance, British warships have directed to enforce ' against the Governnie- of Nicarague a demand for redress of juries inflicted upon British subjects. Nicahrhll-.0 gua is a small Central American rePu"-j of some 300,000 people, and seems to be endoWes „ with a government possessing as little stabilitY most of the other governments in that part el,,t„be world. There can be no resistance wort'h spege""re of to the landing of seamen and marines fromen tb English ships, and the matter would be of little practical importance if it were not for t's, possibility of complicationswith the United Statet The foreign policy of the States so far as it 55°4' the American continent is regulated by what nf kno4n as the Monroe doctrine, which is a kin" "hands off" warning against all foreign intervetr tion with Americaa States. This famous deolorY tion of policy was nude so long ago as 18 re 2S 11,416 President Mpniton who in a message to stated, "We owe it to candour and to amicable relations existing between the ljni lt"0 States and those Powers (European) to doe that •we should ,consider any atten3Pt their part to extend their systole to ialr portion of this hemisphere as dangerous to 0; peace and safety." It was further stated in tb3_.., message We c3uld not view any interposi'llti; for the purpose of oppressing them (the ° otlo.o‘, American States) or controlling in anY manner their destiny by any European Power any other light than as the v manifestation of 3dr unfriendly disposition towards the Unite° States." But it is not at all likoly that thehe will be any differenCes between England and- at h° States over this ultimatum, to Niear''gg„s; In the first place, • England is not seeking a;'t extensions of territory, and probably would 1" „I'tia show any eagerness to have the troublesome the republic as a gift. All that is required is fr, payment of an indemnity for injuries receivod. a., the second place, the,United States can liar 'd undertake to protect all the unruly Central °lc/5 ; South American governments from just deres'for unless it also undertakes to be respensiblf! „.01 their good conduct and that is a task whlol' be lightly entered upon.</p>
        <p>tallt Qum- a number of irOP were C Teachers in onference. ed net, tional quieStions - discussed at the conference a t National Union of Teachers held in 3lanchestl last week. On Tuesday the conference dieess:eod the subject of a teacher's tenure of office' decided favour to p ofgilltnigtiotn the lersEducationnotexDp actlayaarittaixie :f tr;r tenure but. a right of appeal against arbitrarY . capricious dismissal. It seems that uncle'. t,o existing condition of things teachers are liable " sqffer under two tyrannies—the tyranuf h°•s practically irresponsible school managers aod tyranny of small and obscure School Board',..) The methods of the larger school Boards are Complained of in this connection, from the that these Boards are generally rep' resentative: different sections of the community, and, besida ; conduct their business" under the fail gisrltli. public opinion. But the case is difforent some small rural School Boards, often eoe'Ploiit of not more thaA nine mambers whoso strOng is not an enthusiasm for education but an irreP7l.., Bible desire to keep down the rates at all costs.o then, again, we are told, there are hundred: a cio. , voluntary schools ruled by one mati,and thou:o_, more in which :one man really controls ta; administration. If it be true, as alleed, that 1,, a result of these conditions there are " th°l-Is,al:be of teachers in • a chronic state of terror,'" "i National Union is well within its right in endeaor',- ouring to secure legal protection for its oi°lllbozi, and for the other teachers of the country. of Thursday the Union dealt with the subject,B child labour in factories and half -time erall`°,,r,q ment. As might be expected froln those who 750 at first hand the bad results, educationallY ary physically, of the half-tirne employment of yard, , young children, the teachers were 111°4; mously in favour of the abolition .of htlio time. As the speakers fully reeog"iasci' 01 - :. is a question not so much of converti employers as of converting a section of workierg; men who derive profit from the prematurO fJadeak, ..us.,: ings of their children, and there was ras43 a great 10; of truth in the words of the inovor of the tion when he said that it was " a disgrace to,, ; n trade unionists ef the North of Englad tball_ri in to the should have to plead in opposition question which so vital! corned their tr_d `.l y con 0 :4 unionism, to say nothing of their motherhood and manhood." fatherho</p>
        <p>___-... 11,0IIIIIMINIMMINVIII.P.../MIMI.NIN. In the British Army ..me soldier a,. in seven is lOW ti„ilidee..l. twraty years of age. Ihis, of course, includes crags aad band boys. , •</p>
        <p>• :V ; , 4-7'(• r 1p„ 'a sr. ; ,1 • r' , ; a, „ a , ; • ' rt. 'YI : : ; ; ; . ( k s' . : , ;.; t , • ' t • t 4 t „ ' , • t : ;</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Committee which was appointed three 13loath, ago by the Guardians, does not find favour With a certain section of the Bpard. Mr. J. •W. Bnowles was not in favour ef appointing a Ladies* earamittee in the first instance, andV-erefore it can at least be said that he was acting C. ,tently at the annual me 'ing of the Guardians in ,rying to restrict the con_aittee in its visits to the work- house. He did not want the committee to have lie same privileges as elected Guardians when visiting the House, and he did not appear to re- Member that nothing the Ladies' Committee pro- could have any force unless sanctioned by the Board, so that, after all, the elect of the rate- -143°41 need not fear haviitie final authority aken ont of their hands. But the result of the die- ch4B:6°ll shownl that the Board is very well satis- ed with the way the Ladies' Committee is dis- ellusßing its duties.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton had a side thrust at 19k,:8 OR the occasion of the opening of v., e 'and season in the Warringtonpark. He said "at no doubt some of those present were 41,%enstorned to spend the Sunday atternoons at but would probably spend them now in the ?en air as the weather had changed. jr;e, how- tvr, said there was an advantage enter- truntnent at the park as it would not then.be fnr Public worship He apparently thinks " the services at Wycliffe and other places are itirtii,rl3,,lhy entertainments, and nothing else, but there "ably as much or more good done at them e at all the churches in Warrington put Let hher• Anyone vi3iting the meetings cannot every.'t be struck by the heartiness with Which is ng in entered into, and the P.S.A. meeting 11137 4 service for public worship as any of service. Has Mr. Carleton ever been at one "se meeting% we wonder ? whlroblee vestry 'meeting-8 in Warrington have, as a ally r:PaBsed off very qUietly. ,St. Barnabas gener- it trries Cl! the palm for lively proceedings, and 118;v:°'ahlY does so this time. But to those who lett attended these meetings tor three or, four ProrB Paets the proceedings on Tho.sday were very blitsY. There Were one or two pa Ages at arms, nnne of them were (Zany moment.</p>
        <p>e,14 great deal of indignation is expressed by some ehurrell People that persons who do not attend the c°lne to the vestry meetings and take part th clisaussion. They claim that church goers th eY alone have a right to transact the business 7estry meeting. They have to thank the ptva"lialarnent for this " wrong," for everybody who esi even if he be an atheist has a right to baVcc. "a 4 aay at the vestry meeting. Let the church. 4tfti;lBasta ' blished and then it can m ;nage its own '" as it thialrs fit. It is no hardship on church- tne4 th t eOnfor 4: —es cannot contest the affairs of Non- hot R;i3atata, as some allege, because the latter are pcvn'"ta endowed but have "home rule" over there veB; Place. The presence of " strangers" at the lihh2 Iteetings is one of the penalties of the</p>
