      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SS. PAUL'S BIBLE ,CLASS, RUNCORN. A well-attended meeting of this class was held on Sunday afternoon, when Mr. B. Kniveton presided. The musical portion of the service included solos by Mr. John Leathwood and Mr. Robinson, late of Chester. The or- ganist was Mr. William Cooke. A RLINCORN COUNCILLOR'S RATES. , At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday morning,before Messrs. J., Haudley and F. J.Norman,a summons against Mr. W. M. Williams, district councillor for Newtown ward, was beard. Mr. Williams is manager of the Bridge- awater Arms, Percival-lane, and the amount sued for was owing on the General District rate. Mr. J. D. Jones the collector, stated that the amount owing was £5 16s. Bd. The magistrates ordered the immediate pay- ment with costs. No appearance was made on behalf of the defendant. SONS OF TEMPERANCE, RUNCORN. Mr. John Jones presided at a meeting of this society on Saturday evening. The programme consisted of a number of impromptu speeches. The subjects introduced included football, temperance literature, Sunday travell- ing on railways, the veto question, alcohoLand tbe tem- perance cause in Runcorn. . The following officers and members spoke on the various subjecta :—Mossrs. George Holt, D. Bisbrown, EdWard Key, Thomas J. Holt, David Smith, Thomas Clarke, Samuel Temperley, junior, and Thomas Cooke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>_ James Millington made his 17th appearance at Run- c3rn Police Court on Tuesday. He was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Church-st. the previous night.—Mr. Cooke, relieving officer, said the prisoner seemed to have a mania to go to his hou3e when under the influence of drink, for the purpose of obtaining an order for the Workhouse. Evidence having been given in support of the charge, the prisoner was fined 103. and costs or in default of payment 14 days' imprisonment. _ _</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHOREIC, -417NCORN. A successful social gathering in connection with this church was held in the schoolrooms on Wednesday even- ing. Tea was provided, after which a pleasant conver- sazione took place. Various interesting objets were exhibited, and in addition to a programme of vocal and instrumental music being given short addresses were delivered by the Vicar (Hey. Dr:Preston), Mr. Timmins (Vicar's warden),Mr. William liankinson, co-warden, Mr. Mums, representing the Sunday school, and Mr. J. Pritchard, on behalf of the day schools. RUNCORN COUNCIL AND THE SHIP CANAL CO.</p>
        <p>• On Tuesday evening at the annual meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Council,the agreement to be en- tered into between the Council and the Ship Canal was under consideration. It was thought that there were one or two points which required further elucidation and accordingly in order to arrive at a final decision, the meeting was adjourned until the following Monday even- ing. Attention was called to the toll charged by the Manchester Ship Canal Co., to *persons using the ferry boat in crossing the Canal. This and other matters in dispute between the two companies as well as the Wid- nes Corporation are to be discussed at a conference to be held in May when it is expected a settlement will be arrived at.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A very successful reunion in connection with this society was held on Friday evening in Bethesda Hall. An excellent progromme of dance music was played by ,a small orchestral band under the conductorship of Mr. R. W. Pierpoint. Mr. W. Crosly satisfactorily fulfilled the duties of M.C. A number of songs which were sung durinz the evening added interest to the proceedings. The vocalists were :—Miss Commerford, Messrs. Cullen (Widues), H. Branch, J. Booth, Jones, W. Crosly and I.. Scott. Refreahmects were provided by the lady mem- bers of the committee. Mr. J. Brundrit, J.P., and Mr. F. W. Crossley, the conductor of the society, sent letters of apology for non-attendance. Dancing was kept up —ith high spirit until early next morning. ASSAULT ON A POLICE SERGEANT AT RUN6OIIN. . .</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday morning,Arthur Allaby, Mersey-5t., Runcorn,was brought before Messrs. J. Brundrit and E. Imison, charged with assaulting Police-Sergeant Brewster whilst in the, execution of his duty.—Sergeant Brewster stated that on the previous day the prisoner was fined 10s.and costs for being drunk and disorderly. The fine was not paid, and at about 2.30 witness, in accordance with the magistrates' order, was accompanying the prisoner to the railway station for the purpose of taking him to gaol. When in High-st. the prisoner quickly turned round and struck him two violent blows in the face. With assistance he brought the prisoner back to the police station.—lnspector Hicks gave corroborative evidence, and said it was the pri- soner's 10th appearance.—The Chairman said that,trom the charge sheet it seemed to be a favourite amusement of the prisoner's to assault people. He Would be com- mitted to gaol for one month. SERIOUS ACCIDENT TO A CHILD AT RUNCORN.... _ _</p>
        <p>A child, 16 months of age, son ot Mr. George Wilson, 20, Handley-street, Runcorn, met with a serious acci- dent on Thursday evening last week. It appeared that the child was being taken a walk by his stepsister, Ada Emily Shaw, 14 years of age, who on her way home crosseit the waste land at the back of York-street. In doing so she fell into a lime pit nearly two feet deep, and the baby slipped from her arms, head first into the time. Mr. Charles Woolley.heard the screams and went to their assistance. Every effort was made to wash the lime from the child's bead and face at Mr. Woolley's house. The child was subsequently taken home, and has been attended by Dr. McLennan. It. is feared that its eyesight has been injured. 'LECTURE ON SOCIALISM AT RUNCORN. . .</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in the Foresters' flail, Devonshire Square, under the auspices of the Runcorn branch of the Independent Labour Party, on Monday evening. Mr. F. Brocklehurst, 8.A., the I.L.P. candidate for Bolton was the speaker, and he delivered an address on " A Socialist M.P.' In referring to the two great political parties of the present day he said from the socialist's point .of view they were not two parties but one, under twO names. They required a party that did not live in its pasts, or that had a past to live in, but one that lived in the immediate present—that grasped modern opinions, and represented the present-day opinion. The two political parties uttered similar sentiments upon different plat- forms. The division of the future would not be around theoretical ()Onions at all, but around different systems of society. The socialists would represent one scheme of social life, and Liberal and Tory would represent another. Tine dividing lines would be around property, and inter- ests, and would concern different social systems which could not possibly exist co-extensive one with the other. 'The speaker then proceeded to sketch out the socialistic programme, and spoke uf the steps•that would be taken by the socialist MP. to carry out their ideas in Parlia-. ment. A number of questions were afterwards asked, chiefly on the pledge which was agreed to at the I.L.P. conference the previous week, relative to the party's action at elections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BILLIARD PdATCH AT RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>BILLIARD PdATCH AT RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening more than usual interesting games of billiards were played at the Liberal Club, Run- corn, between teams selected by Mr. Totthill, of Birken- head, and Mr. Peter Corns, .of Runcorn Liberal Club. The room was crowded in every part. When it is con- sidered that the visiting team included such players as Mr. Armitage, champion of Liverpool, and the following well-known amateurs :—Gelbourne, Liverpool ; 'Hard- castle, Iloylake ; Dixon, Egrement ; Dale, Tuebrook ; Williams, Wallasey; and Dawson, Birkenhead ; and the home team assisted by Messrs. John Saddler, P. Quinn, J. Parkinson, and J. Quinn, of Widnes, it need hardly be Said that some fine play was exhibited. Mr. Dixon ran out with a break of 87, whilst Mr. t3olbourne's largest break was 47, and also several twenties. Mr. Dawson played a grand game and Mr. Williams compiled 48 in one essay and several twenties. Mr. S tiddler played in champion fashion and beat beat his Liverpool adversary by 8, and Mr. Parkinson also played a grand game, his . opponent, Mr. Hard- castle, only winning by three points.. Barnes also appeared to advantage, only losing by two points. The o: her Runcorn players who excelled themselves were Mesita. W. Brimelow and James Green who won their games. The following are the individual scores TOTTIIILL'a TEO/. ' 'CORN'S 'TEAM. Robt. Toithi 1. Birkeuted 150 W. Wright, Runcorn 144 Aimitaga, Liverpool 142 J. Saddler, Widnes H. R..Holbourne, L'cool 150 P. Quinn, Widnes ll5 Hardeastle, Hoylake ISOBJ. Parkinson, Widnes 147 Dixon, Hoemout Dale. •tuebrook Williams, Wallasey Dawson, Birkenhead l5O W. Ailey, Runcorn Hral),im, Birkenhead ... 118 W. Brimelow. Runcorn 150 Morgan, Birkenhead 123 3, Green, Itancern . l5O Wheolan. Widnes l5) Handley, Runcorn</p>
        <p>• ' 1583. 1452. RUNCORN VOLUNTEER RTFLE CLUB. The third Contest iirthe gold medal competition took place at Runcorn Heath on Saturday. rhe conditions on the whole wfra fairly favourable tor good shooting, the light being very goA until towards the finish, when those who were last as 600 yards found it rather difficult to bring up the much-desired hull's-aye disc. A strong and somewhat unsteady wind from the left, varying to the left front, was no doubt answerable for the loss of a few points, but as will he seen below, several were suc- cessful in putting on very po.l score :- 200 ii 500 600 To- yds. yds. yds. tal. Private James Edwards 29 ... 32 ... 32 ... 93 Private S. Joinson 29 .. 32 ... 30 ... 91 Colour-oeargeaut J. Mort seroant J. A. Hushton 29 ... 27 ... 27 ... t,3 Sergeatic D Edwards L wee- erleatit W. Nield sergeant W H. Harvey ' 3l ... 25 ... 24 ... 80 Private J. Houghton Band-Sergeant J. Gittens el3rgt.-Instrue:Or I. Callaghan 22 ... 24 ... 29 ... 75 Private S. Stubbs Lieuienant 13f undrit I ance-corpoml S. Thompson Sento:Up. Joynion Private 13. D. Matthews 25 ... 21 ... 22 ... 68 Private f./. Glover 22 ... 24 ... 19 ... 65 Private James Saddler Private C. IlAidhury Lance-Corporal R. Foulkes Bugler John Mort 23 ... 21 ... 12 ... 56 Private 3. Y. LeCouteur 27 ... 17 ... 12 ... 53 Lance-Corporal G. Oultram Sergeant J. W. Woodland Private W. Broady i sergeant W. Clooper ET ~. 3 ...absent— 29</p>
        <p>COMMITTER MERTLNGS OF RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL. During Wednesday and Thursday meetings of several committees of the Urban District Council have been held. At the Highways and Buildings, Mr. Taylor was appointed chairman and Mr. Bisbrown vice-chairman ; Water, Mr. Pritchard chairman, and Mr. Wilson vice- chairman ; Finance, Mr. Lightburn chairman, and Mr. Hewitt vice-chairman ; Markets and Cemetery, Mr. Grice chairman, and Mr. Kirkham vice-chairman. The other business was of a formal character. ELLESMERE-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. gathet ing in 'connection with the Christian Endea- vour Society, associated with the above-mentioned school was held on Tuesday evening under the presidency of the Rev. J. Stafford. A magic lantern entertainment was given when a ,variety of views were exhibited by Mr. Hoxworth. PRESENTATION TO THE REV. J. PULFORD WILLIAMS. A very interesting gathering was held in connection with St. John's Presbyterian Church, on Thursday even- ing, when a presentation consisting of an Oxford Bible with " Helps to the study of the Bible," and a purse of gold, was made to the Rev. J. Pulford Williams who is severing his connection with the Church on Sunday next Mr. J. Pritchard, who presided, in making the presenta- tion alluded to the services rendered by the rev, gentle- man during his five years'residence, in the town' and said he had been a very able and acceptable preacher, and had done much to further the success of the church. As a result, the conditions under which they worshipped had changed for the better. Instead of conducting the service in the old Chapel they had now a commodious church in Victoria-road. They trusted that his ministry in Cardiff would provide for him an extended field of labour, and that he would be able to tollow up what he so much desired, viz. his studies at the University. Messrs. Kirkham and F. Hewitt also spnke.—The Rev. J. Pul- ford Williams expressed hl3 thanks for the kindly feeling expressed to him by the congregatien with whom he had laboured. El 3 trusted that the church would continue to prosper. He was sorry to sever his connection with them, but he had considered the matter well before making his decision. An interesting programme was afterwards gone through in which Miss Hayes, Miss C. Pritchard, Misses Warburton, Mrs. Temperley, Messrs. Robinson and Charleston took part.</p>
      </div>
