      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURPAY, APRIL 27 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURPAY, APRIL 27 1895</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF RUNCORN • lIRBAN COUNCIL;</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the annual meeting of the R uncorn Urban District Council was held in the Town Hall,Run- corn. There were present :—Rev. Dr. Gastaldi' Dr. McKenzie, Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., E. Imison, J.P., J. Pritchard, H. J. Grice, F. Wiswall, J. W. Lightburn, G. H. lEirkhaM,D. Eiibrown, G. Hewitt, T. Dean, M. Sweetman, J. Wilson, R. H. Posnett, W. Collier, Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr:E.' Marshall (secretary), and Mr. M. Barker. (surveyor). Al the commencement of the proceedings Mr. Taylor presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TLIE OEMIOL TRA.DE CRISIS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>TLIE OEMIOL TRA.DE CRISIS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL AND THE ALKALI COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Urban District Council had under consideration at their annual meeting on Tuesday evening the subject of the chemical trade crisis in Runcorn. The proceedings were,of course,conducted in private. The matter was discussed at consider- able length, and it was decided that a deputation consisting of Messrs. Speakman, Pritchard, and Lightburn should wait upon the Alkali Company and present a memorial, the text of which is given below. Accdrdingly the three gentlemen proceeded to Liverpool on Wednesday and had an interview with Mr. Brock, chairman of the United Alkali Company. The deputation laid before him the fettling of .Runcorn concerning the state of the chemical trade, the rumours relative to the closing of work in the town, in a straight- forward and clear manner. As to the real nature of the interview great reticence is observed by the members of the deputation until Ate adjourned Council meeting to be held on Monday next. It is understood, however, that they yere informed that the matter should be laid before the meeting of the directors, and their decision shquld be forwarded to the Council at .an early date. The following was the memorial of the Council :—(1) "That this Council hears with much concern and regret that the directors of the United Alkali Company con- template closing their works at Runcorn. (2) That we respectfully point out to the directors that for a great number of years these works have formed the backbone of the trade of the town,and that under private and separate ownership they employed on the whole about 1,700 hinds, and were profitably carritd on, some ot them with conspicuous success. (3) That the severe restrictions to which these works have been subjected since the formati3n of thti Union have already materally affected the prosperity of the town, and reduced very many of the workpeople of the company to great distress. (4) That the closing of these works would seriously affect use financial position of this Council and bring ruin to property owners, tradespeoPle, and the workers of the town. (5) That this Council whilst admitting that the prolonged trade depres- sion must result in restriction and economies, cannot view as equitable the proposal to close the works at Bunco, n, While permitting others be- longing to do the Company to proceed to their full capacity. (6) That this Council resiiectfully offers to all in its power to assist the Directors in this crisis, and entertains no doubt that the workpeople and others directly interested would be willing to meet them in an accommodating spirit, if oppor- tunity offered. (7) That this Council begs to point out that the immediate eff,!ct of closing th.e works would be to prejudice the town as a manufacturing and business centre ; whereas its situation in respect of the Ship Canal and Weaver systems, cheap land and labour, its facilities for shipping, and its close proximity to the salt supply should lead to a directly opposite result. '(8) In conclusion, the Council desire to point out that their present action is not precipitate; on ther contrary, great pressure (not only local) has been brought to bear upon them for sometime past Their object is to place the whole situation betore the directors of the United Alkali Company, believ- ing that its importance and painfulness has not up to the present time been fully realised. They hope that the directers will at once issue such orders as will relieve the situation,or failing their disposition to do so, will make a definite statement as to their future policy." The seal of the Board was affixed to the memorial. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>. -.....____ A BANKING TRANSACTION.</head>
        <p>. -.....____ A BANKING TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>FARR'S BANKING COMPANY V. MEITER AND CO. By this action, which was heard at Liverpool Assizes on Thursday, the plaintiffs sought to recover on two bills of exchange, the one being for £162 19s. 9d., and the other for £l2 12s. 9d. Mr. Horridge represented the, plaintiffs, and Mr. L. Langdon was counsel for the defendants. From Mr. Horridge's, opening 'statement, and the evidence of Mr. Munro, one of the managers of the plaintiff's banking cAming, it appeared that the defence was that as the bills were accommodation accept- ances, and were not discounted by the plaintiffs, the parties.sued were not liable for the amount. The drawers of the bills were Messrs. Eyre and Collins, a firm of Liverpool rubber merchants, whose account with the plaintiff banking company was overdrawn in June, 1894, and the acceptors were the defendant firm. When the bills were presented for the purpose of being discounted the usual inquiries were made, as a result of which the plaintiffs declined to discount the bills, although they agreed t) advance 50 per cent. of the amount. On the 21st June, Eyre and Collin's debit with the bank amounted to £2,837. and on the 25th it had been increased to £3,112, which figure had never been reduced.—Cross examined, witness said that no memorandum of the arrangement was made at the time. He had recently hoard that the hills were merely accom- modation acceptances. Had he known,in the first instill:co that the: were accommodation bills, they would never have been taken at all.—Re-examinedovituess said it was a very usual thing for bills to be deposited as collateral security without being discounted at all. From the evidence of Mr. Eyre (Eyre and Collins), it appeared that of the amount represented be the two bills, Meiter and Co., the dekuclants had had £l5B par into their bank by Eyre and Collins. His Lordship, after hearing arguments, held that the defendants must have judgment against them for some amount with costs, anti to simplify the matter in refer- mace to appeal he thought it would be well to make it for the whole amount claimed, with a stay of execution for ten days. He suggested, however, that the parties should try to.come to some arrangement. Finally, they agreed upon a verdict against the defendant for £230 and costs, for which his lordship gave judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RELIEF OF THE CHITRAL GARRISON</head>
        <p>RELIEF OF THE CHITRAL GARRISON</p>
        <p>At last we have reports from Dr. Robertson himself which show uc how Chitral was "relieved." The be- siegers decamped on hearing of Colonel Kelly's rapid progress with the Gilgit column ; but the garrison had suffered very severely in the course of the protracted st.ingle. The tribesmen invested the fort very closely, and fired Dom behind their stone .breastworks inces- santly. Sorties were made from tie to Wilt by the garrison to drive the enemy from their positiodi near the walls or to blow up their mince, and on each occasion the Anglo-Didian troops suffered severely. In one of these encounters ttOrty-five of the enemy were bayonet- ted. The defence of Chitral uin he universally regarded as reflecting the highest credit on the Indian troops. Originally about 290 officers and men were shut up in the fort, and thecaeualties amount to 39 killed an 62 wounded. Dr. Robertson himself was severely wounded.</p>
        <p>It is stated by the doctor attending Mrs. Parnell that though she may survive the injuries inflicted by the footpads who attacked anl robbed her, her mind will be permanently deranged. The result of the polling in Mid-Norfolk was declared on Wednesday, and was as follows :—R. T. Gurdon (L U), 4,112 ; F. W. Wilson (L), 3,904 ; Unionist majority, 208. The figures in 1892 were :—Higgins (L), 4.0Q9; Ourclon (I, U), 3,599 ; Liberal majority, 470.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CHAIRIII.N.</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRIII.N.</p>
        <p>Mr. Speakman said he had very great pleasure in pro- posing that Mr. Imison be the chairman for the ensuirlg twelve months; The last three moaths experience of Mr. Imison had shown that he was eminently fitted for the position, and he 'Wed he would be spared to guide them in their deliberations. He trusted the Council would give him every assistance he required. They had been a happy family so far. They, bad done great deal of work, and there was a vast amount required doing during the next twelve months. He was sure Mr. Imison would be the right man in the right place. (Ap- plause.)—Mr. Grice,in seconding the mstion,said he en- dorsed the remarks already made.—Mr. Bisbrown said lie bad great pleasure in supporting the resolution. Dur- ing Mr. Imison's term of office he had displayed a great amount of- fairness. He had been very considerate with the members of the Council.—Dr. McKenzie sail he felt confident that Mr. Imison would fulfil the duties well, and steer the ship on a straight course.—Mr. Taylor said he had very great pleasure in supporting the proposi tion.- They had seen sufficient ot Mr. unison's ability tact, judgment, discretiou,and kindness to each member. of the Council. He was sure they could not make a better selection. On being put to the vote the proposi- tion was carried unanimously.—Mr. Inliaon, in taking the chair, expressed his gratitude for the kind remarks made concerning him. Any success that had attended his short tenure of office had beendue to the kindly way they had supported the chair. He was sure if be still received that support they would have a very pleasant year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ELECTION OF VICE-CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grice proposed that Mr. Lightburn be the vice- chairman of the Council. In him they had a gentleman thoroughly acquainted with the business of the Council, and one who had paid every attention in the past to its business.—Mr. Wiawall seconded, and said he had a very lively recollection of Mr. Lightburn's services when he was chairman of the Board of Commissioners. He considered he was one of the best chairmen they had ever had.—Dr. Gastaldi and Dr. McKenzie supported the proposition, the latter gentleman stating that he could speak of Mr.LightbUrn's unselfishness.—The proposition was unanimously carried—air. Lightburn in reply thanked the members far their kindness in electing him to the position. It was always pleasing to get the approval of their fellow men. • • THE COMMITTEES. Mi. Taylor moved that the committeeS'eonsist of the members of the whole Council with the exception of the Technical Instruction and Library Cordmittees.—Mr. Speakman seconded the proposition. Tgiere was a great amount of work to be done. He was not at all in favour ' of large committees, but at the same time he thoUght they had got on very well, and they might try it for another twelve months.—Mr. Lightburn said his experi- ence was that a small committee was best. It enabled the members to make a speciality of one or two depart- ments of the work. It was impossible for a' member to give his time to all the various brancheteof the work. One or two departments were enough for a member to do properly. What was everybody's business was no- body's business. He would like to gobs for small corn- mittees.'—Dr. McKeozie moved an amendment t eat the committees do not consiet of the whole Council. Be approved of small committees. It was a waste o time to have the whole Council a committee, as it took a great deal of time to discuss various subjec•s before coming to a decision.—Mr. Bisbrown seconded the amendment, and said the work of the Council was much better done by small committees. They had tried the method of having the wholeßoani on the committees some two or three years ago and it did not act very well. They had a difficulty in getting a quorum. There was also a disinclination to criticise the work of the creumittees itt the Council meeting.—Mr. Grice had great pleasure in supporting the resolution. Tie thought the committees had worked well during the last three months, consider- ing that half of the members; were new to the business. e—Mr. Wiswall said he could not see what wag the use of every member of the Couneil being on every committee. He did not know of any case where stitch a rule was established. So far as he understood the word commit- tee, it was for the purpose of a few to take a special *matter in hand. tie did think that they should have small committeee. He was not opposed, but rather in favour ot the new members becoming acquainted with all the businese, but he did not understand why the ;Ad members should et SO be on all the cotrun,ittees.—Dr. Gastaldi said if all the members of the Council were members of every comtmttee they were sure of a good attendance at the committee meetings. The business of the Ceuncil had been facilitated owing to every member well understanding what had been said and done, and thus preventing confusion. On a great manny occasions special commit- tees had to be formed. If the committees were formed of but a few persons, it would be very difiLmlt to select .speeial committees.—Mr. raylor replying to the remarks which had been made, said they had not had sufficient time for the new members to become acquainted with the business of the meeting. It would give every mem- ber an opportunity of getting a thorough insight into the working of the Council. He could say as chairman of the Highways and Buildings Committee, width for years had been composed of all the members that he had never had a dittie,ulty in getting through the work of the com- mittee, itial he thought it was admitted that it had beets done satisfactorily.—Mr. H. J. Grice said in the past, sometimes, in consequence of having small committees, they had had to send out for members in order to form a quorum.—The Chairman said the amendment was in effect that the Highways and Buildings Committee con- sist of the whole members of the Council, but that tee other committees ehould nnt.-0a being put to the vote the amendment was lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>TRH BIIIIIAL BOARD DUTIES.</head>
        <p>TRH BIIIIIAL BOARD DUTIES.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Speakman moved that the duties of the Burial Board should be delecated to the Markets Committee and that the committee be named the Markets and Cemetery Committee.—Mr. Taylor seconded the motion,and it was unanimously carried. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wiswall proposed that the following gentlemell compose the Technical Instruction Committee :—The members of the Council, Dr. MacLennan, Rev. Canon Wood, .B.f,v. J. Simpson, Messrs. E. Gatlev, W. Handley, W. Tlankinson9 John Holt, Edward Key,William Moston F. J. Norman,J.P., James Sayle, R. Rawlinson, J. R. Frith, and A. R. Norman.—Mr. Dean seconded the motion.—Mr. Speakman supported, and said that the Technical Instruttion Committee would bs one of the most interesting committees, and would have a great .deal of work to do. He would be personally pleased, and he was sure Mr. Taylor would be, in their capacity as County Councillors to assist the Comimittee.—Mr. Posnett said that some of the gentlemen proposed to be re-elected on the Committee had not taken;any interest in its work. It would he wise and business like to see a lista attendances of the members, and those who had not taken any interest in the Technical Institute should taisrunitted from the committee.—Mr. Grine thought it would be very unwise to single out any names and erase them from the committee.—Mr. .Wiswall said the com- mittee was only just getting to work. They would be able to tell better who attended •at the rind of twelve months.—The committee as proposed was then elected. FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE. . It was moved by Mr. Taylor, seconded by Mr. Dean, that the above committee comprise :—Messrs. W. Handlev,W. Hanliinson, W. Collier, ff. J. (Mee, S. Five, R. R. l''osnett, T. Riley, and the Revs. Calvin Wood and J. Simpson, with the addition of Mr. Cunningham. THE GENERAL DISTRICT RATE. Mr. Lightburn moved and Mr. Bisbrown seconded that the seal of tho Council be affixed to a general district rite of 2s. 9d. in the £ for the year ending 31st March.— This was agreed to. ,</p>
        <p>Couricil then went into committee to Considei the Ship Canal agreement.</p>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>VESTItY MEETING.—The Rev. GI D. Wray presided it the annual vestry Meeting on Thibraday evening last week. There were also present :—Messrs. 3. Stoked, J. Hancock, C. Percival, E. Haddeck, P. Wright, S. Blain, J. Hulme, F. Fryer, J. Acton, J. Leach, R. Price, W. West, S. Withington, and J. S. Jeacock.—Mr. J.H. Hor- ton was nominated by the Vicar as his wat6m—Mr. Fryer was appointed parishioners' warden, on the mo- tion of Mr. Hancock, seconded by Mr. Stokes.. The sides- men nominated were : Messrs. R. Wright, Rogerson, Gaze, Hancock, Buckley, Hadeock, Withington, Chad- wick, J. Aetoo, Percival, and Povey.—Mthssrs. C. Perci- val and J. Hancock were selected as representatives on the ruri-decanal conference.—The balance sheet, which was submitted at the Congregational meeting after- wards, showed that there was a. balance against the wardens of £9 21. 3d. The choir fund ac- counts showed a b dance to the treasurer nT £4 24. 6id. The balance sheet of tke mission church in Halton-road showed that the treasurer had a balance in hand of elovenpence.—There was a balance in , hand of £215. 10d. on the chnrch improvements accounta Votes of thanks were passed to the churchwardens.sides- men, choir, organist for their services. Sir Richard Brooke was also thanked for his gift of a new altar frontal. The question of the better lighting ot the church was referred to a committee composed of the Vicar, churchwardens, Mr. Fryer and Mr. Hancock. A ' Vote of thanks was passed to tie chairrpan and the , meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>At Melton Mowbray Ptty Sessions on 'rueaday, the Earl of Lansdale was charged with assaulting Mr. Henry Soden. of Tbe Friars. Derby. on April sth, striking him on the head with a hunting whip. As Lora Lonsdale had tendered an ample apology, which had been unhesit- atingly accepted, the summone was withdrawn.</p>
        <p>A STREET UPROAR AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>*POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>William Dawnall and Mary Ellen Downall, two strangers in Runcorn, were on Saturday night the cause of a considerable disturbance in Vicar-st., and at Run- corn Police Court on Monday morning,a series of charges were preferred against them. The male defendant WRS charged with being drunk and disorderly in Vicar-st.,also with assaulting P.C. Richardson whilst in the execution of his duty ; and further with breaking a pane of glass a*. the Queen's Head heathens's, doing damage to the ex- sent of 7s. 6d. The female defendant was also charged with being drunk and disorderly, and assaulting P.C. Richardson and a man named James Butterworth. The male defendant, Who appeared with his arm in a sling, pleaded guilty, but his wife said she was ‘• not guilt, ." P.C. Richardson said that en Saturday night he re- ceived information that a man was breaking a window at the Queen's Head beerhouse. He proceeded there and found the male prisoner drunk. He was surrounded by 1 a crowd of about 200 people. He tried to persuade the man to move away,whereupon he'struck witness on the mouth. They closed and fell to the ground together, ; and whilst struggling there the female prisoner came to the assistance of her husband. She kicked witness several times, and used abusive language to him. man named Butterworth came ta witness for his whistle, which he blew in order to summon assistance. Whilst Butterworth was blowing the whistle the female prisoner struck the man (Butterworth) on the lace. Constable Lee then came up and with other assistance the man was brought to the Police Station. He was very violent in the police office and kicked about in all directions. Willis in Duprose, landloru of the Queen's Head, said on Saturday night the prisoner entered his house. He called for a glass of beer, and witness served him, he afterwards noticed that the man had bad sufficient, and took the beer away from him, giving him his money back. 13ie then kicked the door open and wanted to fight. Witness bolted the door, and the prisoner tore the bars away which protected the glass and then smashed it. The damage amounted to 7s. 6d. When the man was 1 outside the house he was more like a lunatic than any- thing else. James Butterworth gave evidence as to seeing the male priadner struggling with the policeman. The fe- male prisoner also kicked the officer. Witness went for the officer's whistle and blew it. The female prisoner then struck witness a violent blow in the face, giving him a black eye. The female prisoner after erees-ex- amining the witness said his assistance was not re- quired at all. Mr. Ball said he went to the assistance of the officer. The male prisoner was acting like a madman. He could not say that the woman was drunk, but she kicked the officer and struck Butterworth. She also got bold of witness and asked him whet he had to do with it.</p>
        <p>William Shaw, jun., and .P.C.Lea also gave corrobora- tive evidence. " The persons asked to be treatkl leniently. The female prisoner denied that she watt drunk. Superintendent Atherton said the prisoners were tramps, It was many years since such a row took place. The Chairman (Mr. James Handley) said it would appear that the row arose through the publickn doing his duty. He thought he ought to be complimented upon striving to conduct his house according to law. The male prisoner would be fined 10s. and costs fls. 4d. for being drunk and dis- orderly ; for assaulting the police he would be commit- ted to gaol for a month's bard labour;• and be also ordered to pay the damage to the window,and the costs, or in default seven days' imprisonment. The female prisoner was fined 10s. and costs for being drunk and disorderly; committed to prison for 14 days hard labour for assaulting the police; and a further term of imprison- ment for 14 days for the assault on Butterworth. Super- intendent Atherton said he desired on behalf of the police to thank /Messrs, Ball, Butterworth and Shaw for their valuable assistance to the police.—The Chairman said the parties deserved complimenting. Ho also thooght Mr. Deeprose acted in a very creditable man- ner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>--•-- MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. RUNCORN BRANCH.</head>
        <p>--•-- MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. RUNCORN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of tbe above mentioned mission ' two concerts were held in the Public Hall, Runcorn, on Tuesday, at which the programme was provided by the band and choir of the training ship " Indefatigable.",i The proceeds were in aid of the Sailors. Grave and Monument Fund. In c mmection with this fund it may be stated that an expenditure of £3O has been incurred in the acquirement of a special plot of ground in the cemetery at Runcorn, and also the erection of a monu- ment, on which the names of those interred are to be engraved. The grave will accommodate twenty bodies, and already two have been interred. The debt at pre- I sent owing is nearly VO. The monument was unveiled on Tuesday afternoon in the presence of a large con- course of people. The band of the training ship" Inde- fatigable." was present. After the singig of the hymn " Hark my soul," Mr. William Shaw, the mis- sionary to the Runcorn Branch, said he was very sorry to state that Mr. Imison who had been announced to unveil the monument had been prevented from doing so owing to important business in his , capacity as chairman of the local body, and, there- fore, it devolved upon him (Mr. Shaw) to perform the duty. He would like to say that the monument had been erected, and the burial ground had been purchased by I the Mersey Mission of Seamen for the express purpose of interring Sailors who might be drowned, or should die away from their homes at Runcorn. They were placed in that grave instead of being buried in a pauper's grave or a public one, as had been the case previously. They had been unable to point to the spot where some sailors had been buried, to the disappointment of the relatives who had come to see the last resting place of departed Bailors. But now the Runcorn Missionary would be able to show relatives of sailors where their loved ones were buried, and their names would be inscribed on the monu- ment. There had been a question asked whether sailors who were Roman Catholics or Non-conformists would be buried there. His answer was that no matter what religion a sailor Might have, whether he was black or white, if he was a sailor and died in Runcorn, they should certainly place him in that sailors grave. The Rev. A. J. Jameson conducted the dedica- tion service.—At the concert afterwards given in the Public Hall, there were were about 300 persons present, including a number of children from Dutton Workhouse,who were afterwards regaled with a good tea, through the generosity of Mrs. Wood and Mrs. F.R. Norman. Mr. Booth, cab proprietor, kindly, proviaed vehicles for the conveyance of the children to Dutton. At the evening concert the chair was occupied by Capt. Crofter', R.N., of H.M. ship " Eagle," who delivered a short address,in which lie referred to the object to which tue proceeds were to be devoted. The programme sub- mitted comprised miscellaneous selections of vocal and instrumental music which were rendered in a most pleasing and creditable manner by the boys of the " In- defatigable," under the conductorship of Mr. A. B.Smith. The band has now visited Runcorn on several occasions and has gained for itself a good reputation.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A pLasa couNentoit</p>
        <p>AT IfkISBY.</p>
        <p>AN OVERSEER IizAVILY FINED. /</p>
        <p>At Frodsharn Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, Philip Priestner, an overseer for Helsby, was sutnmoned on a cnarge of assaulting James Brandreth, a member of the Helsby Parigh Council, and also a district councillor, at Holsby, on the 19th ApriL—Defendant pleaded guilty.—. Tue Chairman (Mr. Reynolds) : Couldn't you settle this without coming into court ?—The complainant said he thought it was far better for it to he _decided there. He was summoned t.) attend a parish council meeting at Helsby the previous Friday. In addition to the mem- bers present, there were also&amp; number of parishioners , listening to the proweedings. Amongst them was the delendaat, Mr. Priestlier. Before the business ot the , Council was over Mr. Priestner got up to ask some ques- tions. The Chairman told him that he could riot do so ' until the meeting was over. After the conclusion of the business the defendant proceeeed to ask complainant cer- tarn questions with reference to a township meeting that had been held about a fortnight previous with respect to the defendant's assessment. The witness then uro- ceeded to enter into details with respect to the assess- ment of the property of the defendant; who was over- seer, and it was regarding some remarks which Mr. Brandreth had made respecting it, that led up to the 'assault complained of. Mr. Priestner asked witness questions respecting this point at the meeting and he answered Nils. When going out of the room tire de- fendant struck witness two violent blows behind the ear, saying that he would take it out of him in that way. He was stunned and did nut know what took place afterwards. Be could not say whether the deism- dant also kit him in the chest, but he felt a pain there afterwards. When he cime round again he found him- self in Mr. Bate's bowie. Mr. Brarnhall White afterwards , took him home.—Defendant said he was guilty of striking Mr. Brandreth, but some of his statements were incor- rect. • Be told the complainant that be ought to be ashamed to pass such rematks,v hereupon Mr. Brandreth said he was a " humbug." He (witness) then atm,* him, and was sorry he had done so. Be had disgraced a holy name.—The Chairman said that public men in the discharge ot public duty should be protected. They considered it was a serious case and they would make an example of defendant. Ile would be fined 4.'5 includ- ing costs or in default of payment, one mouth's impri- loument with hard labour. The money was paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WESTONT PGINT.</head>
        <p>WESTONT PGINT.</p>
        <p>VESTRY MEETING.—This Bev. D. S. Bowen presided at the annual vestry meeting which was held on Friday evening Amongst those present were Mrs. L. Roe,,- berg, J. Jeffs. S. Beresford, R. R. Clark, F. W. Clark. I'. Grounds,T. Jeffs, and It. Cookson.—Mr. Jolin Jeffs pr-- Fentcd the accoun'ts for the yesr,from which it appeared that them was a deficit, cf £2 35. 90. The Chairman, having thanked the retiring officers I, r the valuable ser- vices they had rendered in connection with the chum', nominated Mr. John Jeffs as his warden. On the motion of Mr. T. Grounds, seconded by Mr. F. W. Clark, it was resolved that Mr. S. Beresford be the people's warden. Mr. R. R. Clarke moved, Mr. T. .Jeff seconded, and it was decided that Mr. Herbert Stott be sideman. Messr. L. Rosenberg and R. Cookson were appointed lay repre- sentatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WAR KIN (HON.</head>
        <p>WAR KIN (HON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE</p>
        <p>At the usual ;;kry- Weedni—oTt-he above, on Sunday afternoon,Mr. Sent (president of the move- ment), presided, and Mr. W. A. Sandford gave an admirable address on "A definite purpose in life." Air. Oilier treated the audience to a solo entitled "None need depart," while Mr. Lunt and Mr. Stewart also gave their assistance in the service. Mr. Wadsworth tocompanied on the organ. WRINGING MAORINBS.—GkeLtzrn, Ironmonger, Wan- ngtun.__</p>
        <p>MB WARRINGTON AND BANK QUAY SAIUNG CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. W. Rigbey, the Secretary of this Club, writes us to say that the Committee have now ar- ranged- for the "yacht" race to be re-sailed on Saturday, May 11, from Warrington to Gunton. The yachts will start at about two p.m. from the east end of Monk's Quay and will finish at Garston Lightship. SEWING MACHINES Hands £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 45.w1th handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durrett, 18 and 18 Sankey-et.. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF SUNDAY SCHOOL PRIZES AT r. PAHL'S.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon, Mrs. Edward Greenell, in the absence of Alderman Greensll, who was indis- posed, presented the annual prizes to the boys and girls of St. Paul's Sunday schools. The infants' prizes were presented by Miss Picot. At the con- clusion of the presentation a rote of thanks was sconded to Nine Greenall, on the motion of Coun- cillor Smethurst, seconded by Mr. Chantry:</p>
        <p>.. .....-. , .• ALL 1.!./USIEHOLD I.INRIV, hemmed and ruftrUed flee of charge.—. 7. and W. nurrnm. Sankey-st.. Warrington:L.—Adv. bi USIC IN THE PARK. --- - -</p>
        <p>The St. Paul's Church school band will play the following selection of musk in the Park to-morrow (Sunday) at 3.15 : Hymn March Selection Cornet Solo "The Lost Chord" Sir A. Sullivan Selection Overture God Save the Queen.</p>
        <p>MOST WOMEN detest the thought of washing day, because after their day's work they Suffer from Rheumatism in the hands and arms. COCKATOO SOAP wmild cure all that and make them look forward to we hing day with pleasure.— Apply to ~our Grocer for it, or to N. B. PERCIVAL, A Co., Wholesale Grocers, Butterm aket-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
