      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>CLAIM FOR RENT.</head>
        <p>CLAIM FOR RENT.</p>
        <p>At the WarringtOT County Court on Thuraday,be- fore his Honour JudgeJ. D. Crawford, John Smith, 61, Legh-st., sued Richard Wynne Bretherton, 61, St. James-st., for £4 104. for rent due for a shop lielonging to the plaintiff. Mr. Percy Davies who appeared for plaintiff said that the defendant took a shop in St. James-st., on October let., 1894, at £lB per annum,making an agreement with John Smith, the landlord, to take it for twelve months, and not to terminate his tenancy without giving six months notice. On October 16, the defendant sent the key back again refusing to take the shop. The plaintiff went to see the defendant and claimed twelve months rent, but subsequently he told defendant that if he would bettle the case, he would be satis- fied with £4 109., being' a quarter's rent. The de- fendant, however, thought that a month's rent was sufficient to pay.—John Smith gave evidence of corroboration.—Richard Bretherton, the defendant further said that he was perfectly willing to pay a month's or even five week's sent as he considered that that was suffie.ient,as plaintiff had set the shop again in November.—His Honour, however, con- sidered that the agreement entitled the plaintiff to his claim and gave judgment accordingly, the de- fendant to pay .ss. per month. CYCLES,—GARLAND, Sole Agent, Bridge-Bt., Warring- ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WAS IT A LOAN OR A GIFT 7_</head>
        <p>WAS IT A LOAN OR A GIFT 7_</p>
        <p>Sarah .Wi.0.0-d7 wife of -6iar-i-es Wood, Oughtring- ton, sued Benjamin Oldrovd, of the same village. at the Warrington County Court,on Thursday,before his Honour Judge J D. Cawford, for £6, money lent on February 20th. The plaintiff said that the defendant and his wife on February 20, came to her house and told her that they were in a flx,inas- much as their child was dead,and they ha i nothing 'to bury her, as they had not got the insurance money. They asked her to lend then .£6 or .£7. She thereupon lent tham £6.—Cross-examined by Mr. A. Browne, who appeared for the defence, the plaintiff said that she herself had the child insured for .£2O 12s.,but she denied promising the defendant's wife that if she signed the claim for the insurance money she woukl, bury the ctkild at her own cost.—Mrs Woods' daughter corroborated the plaintiff's state- ment.—Mr. Browne submitted that the plaintiff had had the child heavily insured, and that she had promised to bury the child.—The defendant, who was called, said that the money was voluntarily given by the plaintiff in accordance with a pro- mise she had previously given.—llis Honour said that t here had been no conflict of testimony, it be- ing merely that one party meant the transaction to be a loan and the other meant it to he a gift. It was, however, for the defendant to prove that it was not a loan, which be had failed to do satisfac- torily. He must, therefore, give judgment for the plaintiff for the amount claimed. A SPLENDID ASSOPTMENT of Ladies Skirts and Blouses.— Brags. 17, Sankey-st.. Warrington.— Adv. THEFT FROM WARRINGTON MARKET—"THIS OLD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BIRD" SENT TO GAOL.</head>
        <p>BIRD" SENT TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>Sarah Dignum, 60 years of age, and described as a seamstress, with a long record of crime against her, was charged at the Liverpool County sessions on Tuesday with having at the Warrington market, on the 20th February, stolen a purse containing 125.8 d. and nine Co-operative Stores' cheques from the person of Louisa Elizabeth Hughes. It may be interesting to note that the prisoner is known in Liverpool as "Ann Morn," but is better known as " Cox,' of Circus-street. Liverpool. When brought up at the Warrington Borough Police Court she was very respectably dressed, having a lady-like demea- nour, in fact, she gained the appellation of" The lady thief." She aroused such suspicion in the minds of the local police authorities that she was taken to Mr. T. Birtles' studio in Sankey-street t 3 be photographed. She here displayed all the tactics of" an old hand" by pulling such grimaces and • behaving in such a violent, manner as to render a correct photograph of herself being taken imposs- ible. She was sent to gaol for four months. ' ITNtnntt,t,tB and Suxsrtems in Immense variety. EVAM3. 17. Sankey-st., Warrington.— 'd • .</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF LONG SERVICE MEDALS TO WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, the first presentation assembly of the Warrington Volunteers was held, and opportunity WAR then taken to present the long service Medals to the members of the battalion who .did not receive them at the presentation at Liverpool a few weeks ago. Colonel Ridgway was in command, and the following officers were also present :—Captains Buckton, Edlesten, Appleton, Chorley, and Fair- dough; Lieutenants Ridgway, Fairclougb, and Fletcher; Captain and Quartermaster Hutchinson; Surgeon and Captain Adams ; and Captain and Adjutant J. W. B. Silverthorne. There were 185 merabers of the W arrington corps and also 22 of the Newton corps present. The men paraded at the Drill Hall at four o'clock, after which they pro- ceeded to the grass plot in front of the Town Hall, where the presentation took place, the Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough) giving medals to the following :—Colour-Semeant W. Plinston. Sergeant M. Benny, Corporals J. Beswick ,and J. Walker, Privates A. Caunne. J. Dean. W. Hill, awl C. bib- well. Sergeant J. Bramhall and Lance-Sergeant T. Minshall were also entitled to medals, but were un- able to be present. In inviting the Mayor to make , the presentation, Colonel Ridgway said that one of the pleasantest duties he had bad to perform since he had bad command of the regiment was to-ask Ithe Mayor to present the long service medals to those members of the corps who did not receive them in Liverpool when others were given by General Julian Hall. He trusted that to those who had not yet fe-rved the proper time the present pro- ceedings would serve as an incentive to them to remain in the battalion, and by their attention and attendance be the recipients of Her Majesty's deco- ration.—The Mayor said it had given him great pleasure to be there that day to present the medals. He was also much pleased with the whole batta- lion, and he was certain from what. he knew of the young men of Warrington that if they should ever be called upon, they would render a good account of themselves. After the presentation the batta- lion proceeded to a field in Wilderspool Causeway, where "the attack" was practised. • OUR NOTRD KID riLUVRS. 1.. 63-d. per pair. In all shades.— EVANS, N, tiankey-it.. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
