      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>CHESHIRE LINES WORKMEN'S PROVIDENT SOCIETY. Ihe 20th annual meeting of the Cheshire Lines Platelayers' Provident Society, which consists of the workmen employed in the engineer's depart- ment of the Cheshire Lines Railway, was held at the works, Battersby-lane, Warrington„ on Thurs- day. The chair was taken by Mr. W. G. Scott, chief engineer of the Company, and the meeting was largely attended by officials and delegates from , Lancashire and Cheshire. The Secretary submitted the annual report, which stated that the number of members was 1,083. A large sum had been expended. in aid of 238 sick members, who had received assist- ance from the funds of the Society, and 23 death claims had also been paid. The report and state- ment of accounts having been passed, Mr. Thomas Pratt was re-elected secretary for the ensuing year, and Messrs. G. Walker and W. Hughes were, re-elected auditors. The proceedings terminated with a vote of thanks to the Chairman, officers, and others.</p>
        <p>MANTLES, JACKETS. CAPES and COSTIIMES —the largest sleek in Warrington —Hvasre, 17, Sankey-st.-4d. -- BELLItINGE .R 8 AT GRAPI'ENHALL. • . .</p>
        <p>The ringers at Grappenhall church journey to Frodsham on Saturday, where, by the kinflness of the Rev. Canon Blogg, the bells were put at their disposal. After adjusting the ropes the following peals were rung ;—Seven hundred and twenty College Single by-1. James Mainwaring •2, George Lewis; 3, Thomas Burrows; 4, Samuel dorsfall ; 5, C. R. emotion •, 6, Frederick T. Spence (conductor) also 720 Kent Treble Bob Minor by-1, James Main- waring ' • 2, George Lewis; 3, Thomas Burrows; 4. Samuel Horsfall ; .5, Frederick T. Spence; 6, C. R. Greenall (conductor) ; 720. Canterbury Pleasuro, by —l, James Mainwaring ; 2, Henry Moston; 3,George Lewis; 4, C. R. Greenall ; 5, Frederick T. Spence (conductor) ; and 6. John W. Booth; and 720 Plain Bob Minor, by--1, F. Webb, 2, Frederick T. Spence; 3, Thomas Burrows; 4. Samuel Borden ; 5, C. R. Greenall ; 6, John W. Booth (conductor) ; also 720 Oxford Single Bob, by-1, W. Bibby; 2, George Lewis ; 3, Thomas . Burrows; 4, Samuel Horsfall ;5, Frederick T. Spence; and 6, John W. -Booth ;conductor). Tenor, 15cwt. SEWING MACHINES. Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle RA 4s. with handsome ve ,lnut cover.—J and W. Durros. 16 and 18 Sankey-6f., Warrington —Advt. PRESENTATION TO A WORTHY MEMBER OF THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>_OILDER OF DRUIDS.</head>
        <p>_OILDER OF DRUIDS.</p>
        <p>The officers of the above -society met on Tuesday evening to present an emblem of merit suitably framed in gold t..) P.D.G.M. William Barlow, late treasurer of the district. &amp;c., who has been com- pelled to resign his office through failing health, ibe presentation was made by D.G.M.Samuel Dun- bobbin, who said it wat with the deepest regret that through affliction Mr. Barlow had been compelled to resign his position in the society, and hoped he would be speedily restored to health and strength again to look upon the presentation for many years to come. The following officers of the. society were present :—P.D G.M. -Samuel 'Caldwell, P.D.G.M. Jonn Booth, D.D.G.M. James Massey and P.D.G.M. Thomas Roberts D.C.S. The following inscription is on the emblem. :—" The Order of Druids. Industry bierit Reward. This certificate of merit is presented by the officers and members of the Warrington Equalized District Society and Widow and Orphans fdnd of the above Order to P.D.G.M. William Barlow D.T. as a token of respect and esteem for his valuable and energetic services for 37 years to the Widow and Orphans fund and in promoting the interest and welfare of the order.</p>
        <p>- ALL HOUSEHOLD Luta"( hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W DUTToN. Sankev-at., Warrington,— LAWN SIOWBAS.- -GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington and liarlestoom.</p>
        <p>PRIMROSE LEAGUE MEETING AT dRAPPENHALL, The annual meeting of the members ot the Grap- penhall Habitation of the Primrose League took place on Saturday evening, when upwards of 130 persons assembled in the Latchford parish-room. At about 6.30 an.excellent tea was served, the presid- ing ladies being Mrs. J. C. Parr, Mrs. John Coxon, Mrs. G. Hankey, and Miss Massey.—A business meeting of very short duration succeeded the tea, and was conducted lo Mr. Ackerley, provincial secretary for the league district.—Mr. A. L. Kaye, hon. secretary of the habitation, presented the report of the past year which showed that they had begun the year with a credit of £2 9s. 6d., and at present had a balance in their favour of £1 Bs. 4d. On the proposition of Mr. Leyland, seconded by Mr. G. Hatfltey, the repurt and accounts were adopted. Mr. John Coxon proposed that Mrs. Parr be re- elected ruling councillor for the ensuing twelve months.—Mr. Alan Lister Kaye seconded, and the motion was enthusiastically carried.—Mrs. John Coxon was unanimously elected honorary treasurer, on the preposition of Mr. Lister Kaye, seconded by Mrs. Parr.—Mr. Hankey proposed the re-appoint- ment of Mr. Lister Kaye as hon. secretary.—Mr. Leyland seconded, and the resolution was carried with unanimity.—Gentlemen to form the b.xecutive Council were subsequently chosen, and it was also decided that the tribute be henceforth 3d. instead of 6d.—Dancing afterwards begun and was enjoyed until ten o'clock by a large number of ladies and 'gentlemen, amongst whom were—Mrs. Parr, Mr. and Mrs. John Coxon, Miss Massey, Mr. Ackerley, Mr. Lister Kaye, &amp;c. FOR THE TOILET. for the bath, for the laundry Cockatoo ap iv unequalled.—Advt. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 188. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut oover.—J. and W. DUTTes,I6 and 18 Sankey, Warrington.—Advt. CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual meeting for the Warrington polling district. (comprising the town- ships of Poulton-witn-Fearnhead, Warrington and Woolston-with-Martinscroft) of the Newton Divi- sion Conservative Association was held in the schoolroom, Padgate. Alderman Francomb occnpied the chair, and amongst those present were—Alder- man T. Sutton, Councillor A, Beunett. Councillor J. W. D. Barron (Salford), Messrs. W. Holland Owen (hon. solicitor to the association), H. Mawdsley (Conservative agent for the divison), W. Houghton, G. Mather, T. Houghton, T. H. Granger, and J. W. Houghton. Mr. W. Holland proposed that Alder- man Francomb, J P., be appointed chairman, in place of the late Mr. Joseph Davies, J.P. Alder. man Sutton seconded, and the motion was carried Alderman Francomb briefly thanked the meeting and proposed a vote of condolence with Mrs. Davies and family in their sad bereavement. Mr. George Mather seconded the proposition, which was unani- mously carried. Mr. William Royston was re-elected vice-chairman. On the proposition of Mr. Owen, Mr. Alderman Sutton was re-elected secretary and treasurer. Messrs. A. Bennett, G. Mather. W. Houghton, and F. M. Appleton were appointed dele- gates to attend the Council. The old committee, p.with the exception of Messrs. T. Yates and J. Dobson, who are now deceased, were re-elected, together with Messrs. Joseph Stringer, T. H. Granger, James P. Critchley, with power to add to their number. Councillor Bennett proposed the following resolution :—" That this meeting is of opinion that the domestic policy of the present Government is of the most revolutionary character, and fraught with danger to the best interests of the British Empire. and begs to enter a solemn protest against the Welsh Disestablishment and Disendow- roent Bill now before the House of Commons." Mr. George Mather seconded. and Mr. J. W. D. Barron supported the resolution. The resolution on being put to the meeting was unanimously carried. Votes of thanks to Mr. Barron for having given an address and to the Chairman for presiding brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>ALL 110U81,11OLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, tree et charge.—J. and W. DUTTON. Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt</p>
