      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BEFQRE TIM MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFQRE TIM MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A QUESTIONABLE CASE OF CRUELTY. At the Borough Police Court, on Friday, before Alder- man Harrison and ether magistrates, yhomas Wheeler was charged with ill-treating a horse by working the „same in an unfit state on the 30th of March, and William. Worrall, Wash-lane, was charged with causing the same to be worked.—Mr. Percy Davies appeared for the de , fendants and pleaded not guiltv.—lnsuoctor Luff states that on the 30th of March he was in Mersey-qt., and saw Wheeler driving a grey pony which was lame on the off hind leg. He went to Latchford and saw Worrell who said be had had it two or three days and would not work it again until it got sound. The horse was not his but belonged tea man at Barnum, near Northwich.—Ser- , geant Rowbotham had seen the horse on the same date when out of the shafts and it was then larne.—Air.Davies , thought the Bench would be satisfied th it there was no evidence against Worrall because the prosecution must satisfy them that Worrell had a knowledge that the horse was suffering. the horse was well when he went out and not a slat. of lameness about it.—Mr. Davies thought Mr. Luff might have told Worrall that he in- tended to report the case, so that he might have an opportunity of seeing a veterinary surgeon as to the con- dition of the horse.—Mr. E. Bennett, living at the corner of Wash-lane, declared positively that on the day in question when he saw the horse pass hia door it was perfectly sound, and not a sign f lameness about it.— The Bench ultimately dismissed the case, but fined the boy in the costs. AN 'UNUSUAL METHOD OF COMMITTING AN ASSAULT. On Monday, at the Warringe on Police Court, before the Mayor (chairman), Messra.Francomb, Harrison, and Hayward, John Kilrain was charged with assaulting Corporal Gilroy,whilst in Sankey-at. on Saturday night, and defendant was further charged with stealing from the person of Gilroy, a stick .—Corporal Gilroy, stated that on Saturday night he was on duty as oneof the military police, and with Sergeant Stringfellow, stand- ' ing at the top of Bold-street, when the defendant came up to him and asked him to turn round to the light. Complainant did so, when defendant threw him to the ground, took his stick from his hand and broke it in two, at the same time making off wlh complainant's cap.—Sergeant Stringfellow in giving corroborative evi- dence said the defendant accused the corporal of being concerned in a rose.-4 boy of 14 also gave evidence and stated that the defendant dropped the cap which another man ran off with.—P.C. Barnett (311 deposed to arrest- ing the defendant when l,hased by the soldiers along Sankey-st.--Sergeant Taylor explained that the defend- ant belonged to a gang, one of whom the police had locked up and had been helped by the soldiers, and this was the cause of defendant attacking complainant —The magistrates fined defendant 10s. and costa or one Month's hard labour. CATCHING A TARTAR. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday morhing before the Mayor and other magistrates, John Delooze, 62 Lythgoes-lane, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in john-street, on Saturda. night ; he was further charged with having wilful .; damaged a window to the extent of ss. and also wilfully damaged the uniform tunic of Sergeant Cheney.—Ser- geant Cheney stated that at 10 30 on Saturday night he re eeiveda mesea ge frpm a Miss Norris, and going to her shop he found the prisoner very drunk. Miss Norris told witness that the prisoner had broken a window. Witness asked prisoner to pay for the damage, hut the latter said he would do what he liked,whereupon Cheney told him he would be locked up. Some friends ot prisoner then attempted to take him home, hut he broke loose and ran at witness who closed with him, and they fought for about 20 minutes, during which time the officer's tunic. was torn down the front and his whistle- chain broken and thrown away. Ultimately with the assistance of Sergeant Stringfellow and Corporal Gilrnv, of the military police, the prisoner was taken to the Police Statien.—The prisoner was sent to gaol for two months'hard labour,he having previously been convicted for assaulting the police. ANOTHER CLOTHES. LINE TREAT. At the Borough Police Court, Warrington, on Thine- day before Messrs. Chandlev and Sutton, Patrick Connelly was charged with stealing from the hack yard of 123, Mersey-street, about 8 30 on Tuesday night, a flannel shirt, the property of Joseph Wadswortit.—The Chief Constable stated that the complainant was a pro- vision dealer and prisoner was a single man without any regular place of abode. On Tuesday evening last about 7.45 Mrs. Wadsworth saw the flannel singlet, value 3s. 6d., on the line .1n the yard and some 20 minuten afterwards it was missed. The matter was reported promptly to the police and enquiries set on foot. On Wednesday morning, Mr. Mather, pawn-broker. saw the Shirt in his shop and informed the police, after which the prisoner was arrested by Detective Sergeant MOnaghan. After Mrs. Wadsworth had corroborated Mr. Hunt's statement, Margaret Mather Paid that the prisoner pawned the shirt at her father's shop for 15 pence. Detective Sergeant Menaehan clepneed that from a description of the man he arrested him in Horse- market-stv et and brought him to the police station where he was charged, and said he boughs "10 shirt in Bridge-at. about 8,7'5 week.' ago—The hence considered the charge proved and sent the prisoner to gaol for one menth hard labour. "Ha -• e HAVE BUILT A Row OE ITOUSEI ON Boxes." O'i " lay morning tat the B nemeli Police Court be- fore al • etiandley, chairman, And Alderman Sutton, Willisiennor and Patrick Sohn were charged with sitealine from the Market Plac.e, on Wednesday night. a fi=h box value 3i. the property of James Mare—Mr. Hurt explained that the complainant, James Mart, was a fish dealer from St.flelens,and a stallholder in the War- rington Market. Both prisoners were well-known, and about 11 o'clock on Wednesday night Detective Serge- ant Monaghan was standing near Lyme-stewhen he Saw a number of young men, the prisoners among them,close to complainant's stall, and drawing nearer be presently saw Solan pickup a box and go eff towards King-street. Monaghan followed and on getting into King-st., saw r.c. Haim :worth approaching, and at once went up to Solan and questioned him,Solan saying that the box was for firewood. At this juncture the officers heard Connor coming up, and the latter on getting nearer said he had given the box to Solan. Beth were tfien arrested and broueht to the Pollee Station. Mr. Hunt asked for a re- mand until Saturday to enable the complainant to be present.—Detectives Monaghan gave corroborative evi- dence and Connor, objecting to the remand, asked why should he take the box when he could get them any time as they were not returnable. He had had enough during the winter " to have built a row nf hnuees nn."—Mr. Place,market inspecter,nbserved that there were numer- ous complaints about the boxes disappearing, and the case was remanded till Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MINOR, CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR, CASES.</p>
        <p>-- WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT, FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chlirman), • Councillor Roberts, and Dr. Gornall. As ADJOURNED CRUELTY Case.—Flarriet Hill. 36, Watkin-street, was charged with having on the 14th March crurlly ill-I-re-sled her childran, Elizabeth, Mary, Joseph, Julia, and Thomas. At terst hearing of the case the woman promised to go to a home, hut having a small child she could not he admitted. Mr. Buekton, solicitor,. who prosecuted,said that the woman had been 'found drunk again and in congequenre the children were , again neglected.—lnspector liScA"eer said there was no</p>
        <p>improvement in the condition of the family.—Mr.Taylor I P.C.M., said he bad several conversations with the woman who had :ultimately signed the pledge, though she had been drunk since the first hearing of the case. The husband had had think in the taouse and this had tempted the woman, and Mr. Taylor thought the husband was more to blame than was thought a month ago.—The case was adjourned another fortnight. USING TIIELEAI a.— Thomas Bridge, 3 Rodney-st., was charged with having on the 9tlt of iapril made Use of threats to Sarah Jane Whitehouse, 60 f urner•st.—The complainant, Sarah Jane Whitehouse, deposed that she was quarrelling with defendant's wife when the defend- ant came and th ned to use a knife upon her.— Evidence was given on both sides and the case was ulti- mately dismissed. , ALLEGED THEFT.—Catherine Congolly, 23 Dutton-St., csas charged with .tealinca shawl on the let of April, dalue 75.—Mr. Hut% said the Complainant was Agnes Hussey who had lent the shawl to defendant at the work- house on the lst of April. The defendant promised to bring it back, but inste ci she p -wned it and drank the proceeds.—Corroteratjve evidence was g;ven and de- fendant tent to gaol for ene month's hard labour. MONDAY.—Before the al,-yor (chairman), Aldermen' Harrison and Francomb, and Mr. J. W. Hayward. ' BREACHES .OF 'llls FEACll.—George Hopkins Was charged with committing a breach of the peace by fighting, in Crown-st., on the 27th of Sep' -mber.—Pri- soner was dismied on promising to leave the town for six months.—George Brookt-:, 61 Eerfoot-st., and Joseph Currier,3 Chapell-st.,fQr committingi the same offence on the 13th April, were bound over to In fp the peace for Fix months in a surety of £5; for also committieg the same offence on the same date in Winwick-st.. William Smith, Cockhedge-lane, and Henry Dagnall, 55 Oliver- at,., were de,dt with in like manner. DRUNK AND DISORDSELY.-- Mrtwara Griffin and James Griffin, 64 Dallatn-lane, and 2 Clothe3hall Yard, respec- tively, were fined ss. and costs, for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-St,, on the 61h of Aril; a similar offence committed in Fennel-se., on the Sth Anail. earned Carry O'Brien, Green's C,ouat, a fine of 2s. 61.; Elizabeth Kearns. 20 Silver-st., was similarly dealt with for a like offence in Silver-se, on the 16th April. ANOTHER CAP-DRINER FlNED—Thomas 'Maguire was charged, that'he hcing a licensed driver, on the 13th of April, failed to keep the lamp of his cab properly lighted and also neglected to wear his tau-inv.—P.C. Efol- lingworth stated that be met tine defendant in Wilson Patten-st. without the lamps of his cab being lit. Wit- ness cautioned defendant, but saw him later on with the lamps still out and himself withour his badgo.—ln- spector Bowen. in answer to a question from the Bench, said that drivers on the ton stand at the Central Station were not compelled to wear a badge, and the police had no control over them. As the defendant claimed to be stationed there the Bench dismissed the charge for the badge and fined the defendant 2.e. 6i including costs, for not having his lamps lighted. WEDNESDAY. —Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman) and Mr. J. Geddes. • DRUNK AND DISOTIDEFLY.—JOIM Whittaker, 37, Scot- land-road, was fined sa. and costs or seven days hard labour for tr.inz drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket- st., on Tuesday. P.C. Turner proved the case. THURSDAY.--Be[ore Mr._ S„ Chandley and Alderman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Sutton.</head>
        <p>Sutton.</p>
        <p>DPIJNIE AT ISE POLICI3 STATION --James CiVile, rciddle-Igc 1 man, was charged witn being drunk in tee Police Station at 3.15 of Wednesday afternoon.—De- fendant pie &amp; I guilty.—P.C. Phillips eta d that the r defendant r .me into the office on Wednesday afternoon lv drunk. He made some unintelligible state- ment and was therilocked up.—ln Court the defendant said he bad had influenza only a short time aga, upon Alderman Sutton remarked that infloetza wee rcsponsiblefor'a good many things.—The defendant was dismis:. 1 with A caution.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PErrY SESIONS. Moisney.—Before By. James .Handley (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Mr. F. J. Norman. NEW PCOR RAlZ9.—The magistrates affixed their sig- nature to a new poor rate of Is. 41. in the £ for Halton; and of one of ls. Ci.in the £ for Stnckham. The former rate is estimated to produce £405 16s. 6d.; and the latter £53 194. 53. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Athur Allahy, Mersey-st., was fined S. and costs or in default of payment seven days' imprisonment. for being drunk and disorderly in Church-St., on the 16th April. P.C. Gregory proved the</p>
        <p>case. MAINYRNANoB Olunne—A painter named William Horatio Fletcher, Vicar-st., was summoned by his wife for neglecting to maintain her and four children. The complainant stated that she had berm married to the defendant almost eight years. He left her on the 12th Decent: r last year and had not since paid her any- thing towards her maintenance. He signed an agree- ment at Mr. Linaker's office to contribute 15s. per week to—ards the support of the children and herself, but he h. d never paid her anything. She received as- sistance from the. Relief Committee, hut the. distribution of relief wes now stopped. About a month azo she rent the children to the defendant. who was living with his mother, and he had kept them ever since. He did not, however, contribu' , towards her support. The defend- ant said his wife ran away from him. He had been out of work for almost four months, and had been un- able to pay anything towards her support. fle was now maintaining the children.—The malistrates ordered the defendant to pay 7s. El. per week towards his wife's maintenance. and to pay the costs. InallNK.—John Freeman, St. Helens, was fined ss. including costs tor being drunk in Devonshire Square on Saturday.—Superintendent Atherton said the defendant .was one of those who visited Runcorn on Saturday to witness the foothill match_ TriEFr BY A YoUTa.—Flenrip Ellis (16) tat Wivern's Place, was charmed with having felOninusiy stolen 3 jersey, the property of Jabez Unsworth.—Percival Unsworth, said the jersey produe,d was his father's pro- perty, and he valued it at 104. 61. 1-te AIM it hanging just outside the shop in High-street at-nt eight, o'clock on Saturday night.—Threnas Hulse, 8, Lock-street, stated that at a quarter-past eight on Saturday kght he met the defendant at the bottom of flelpia Bridge: And Proceeded with him to the Forester's Hall, in Devonshire Square. He left him there, but, he afterwards met him amain in High-street about nine o'clock. Defendant had a jersey under his grm and lie asked witneqas to pledge it for.him,but witness refused. The defendant afterwards got another hoe to pledge it for him, who got 4s. advanced on it at Smith's pawnship. in Alcock-street. When the boy took it to he pawned clefen lent told wit- ness that he he had gnt the jersey from Unsworth'S. In cross-examination witness admitted recteivinn, 91. from the defendant.—James Cuniffe gave evidence as to pawnine the jersey for Ellis whn told him that he had got it from Mrs. Clarke's. Ellis gave him a penny for pawning it.—.T. Smith s,,akq as to receiving the jersey in pledge, and P.O. Gregory steed that the prisoner told him that he stole the jersey from Unsworth shon, but Hulse was with him when he did so.—The magistrates fined Ellis 104., or seven days imprisonment, and the Superintendent afterwards reprimanded Hulse for his conduct in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FRODSHAM PEIFY SE3SIOI</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM PEIFY SE3SIOI</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Charles Reynolds (chairman) Dr. Steele, arid Mr. T. Handley. POOR RATES.—The Illagistcates affiired their si,mstnree to a new poor rate of is. 4-1. in the £ Frodsham Lordship. estimated to realise £fi32 12. 91. and also one for Frodsham Township of is. 2d. in the £ estimated to realize £797 14s. 2d. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Mary Butters was fined sq. including costs for b:sing, drunk and disorderly in Vulun- teer-street, Frndsham, on the 30tla March. ILLF.GAL REMOVAL OF GOODS.—Cha ries Percival, Frodsham, was summoned for unlawfully and fraudo- handy removing goods to avoid distraint for rent £1 13s. 1110., due to John Walton, Frodsham Lordship, on the Bth April, 1.395.—J0hn Waltun gave evidence as to the amount stated ("Ting dun for rent. The defendant removed his goods on the night of 29th March.—Defen- dant said the house was not fit for a dog to live in, and had bees condemned. The amount owing was ordered to be paid WithirLone month, with costs, or in defa Ilt fourteen days' imprisonment. A CYCLIST IN TROUBLE.—John Gilbert, Morley Cot- tages, Barrow, near Dunham-'oh-Bill, was fined 51. for riding a bicycle without having a lighted lamp after 8 p.m. on the 31st March. He alto gave a wrong name and address to the officer. CIIARGE OF PERMITTING DRUNKEN:4E9i : A 70 nuns* TIMANCT.—Mary Bickerstaffe was summoned for per- mitting drunkenners on her liceneed premises a". Five Crosses, Frodsham Lordship, on the 6th inst. Mr. W. U. Churton, solicitor, represented the defendant, and stated that she was an old lady, 90 years of age, and unable to come out.—P.o Boise stated that at a quarter past eight on the night of the 6th of the present month he visited the defendant's house in company with Con- stable Price. in the taproom he saw two men very drunk. Walter Lickerstaffe, the landlady's son, was in the room at the time he went in. He drew Bickerstaffe's attention to a man named Charles Percival who was Sit- ting by the table with his head resting on the table. He was so drunk. Witness afterwards drew Bickerstaffe's attention to a man named Joseph Allman who was sit- ting in a roam with two other' men.—P.C. nice gave corroborative evidence. The man Percival was refused drink earlier in the afternoon at the Bear's Paw public- house.—Mr. Churton said that the defendant had been at the house in question for something like 70 years ' without any complaints being made. She was just 90 years of age. and it would be us thousand pities if her escutcheon should be spoiled by a small Case of that kind.—Eyidence for the defence was given bv Walter Bickerstaffo, George Spruce, Kingsley, Charles Percival, and Frederick Bickerstaffe.—The magistrates then re- tired, and on their return the Chairman said the Mfl.is trates considered that it was not in the knowledge of the landlady that the people were drunk when they were supplied. and therefore they would dismiss the rase.— Charles Percival was afterwards fined 10s. including costs, for being drunk on the licensed pre:nises of Mary Bickerstaffe.</p>
        <p>The trial of Mr. F. M. Co'dwells, M.P., and other directors of the " Balfour group" of companies on various charges of fraud was on Monday, on the appli- cation of counsel for the prosecution, postponed until the next sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>rHE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 27, 1895.</head>
        <p>rHE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 27, 1895.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Played at Runcorn on Saturday in beiutiful weather. flotn teams wee strong, Sr. 'Helens started about four o'clock. Davies responded, and Abrams getting up again, took the ball past the centre of the ground by a tricky run, St. Helens forwards Made a shert visit to the home end of the ground, but Myers made a,, dash barkond with judgment punted the:ball well into touch. Houghton was prominent with several lofty, punts and generallv-fourid touch, thmbabin gained a freekick, to widen Pounces resnoneed, eleverly,clearing,his ground, kleaug • well intci touch. The home . forwards, Firmer, Taylor and Johnson, broko away, and a short bout of 1 passing br.Paulkr , .. Ahra tes and W. Faulkner gave the ' St. Helens backs a very difficult task ti keep the home 'side 1 tom se:oring. A to-ate', however, cleared the pack, and pasaing to J. Fa 'Javier a try was scored under the _posts, Dunhabin e' 'ly kicking the goal. After the re- start St. Helens made strong efforts to get level. Sudlow and l'hiherty made short rims, but the tackling was ton strait by the home forwards to admq of any chance to cet away, the Runcorn forwards continually vetting possession in the scritnmagea. Abrams and J. Faulkner made the most of their chances,the St. Helens hack divi- sion having to taekie dery surely to .prevent any further Isnoring. Farmer made a rush on Ilia own account, which Myerelurther snpolemented, making a grand sprint up, to the St. Helens goal line. Foulkt sand Sudlow got up in time to prevent a score. From the succeeding scrim- tnage J. Faulkner crossed,but could uotground the ball. , 'rite score at the interval was Runcorn one goal. St. ! Helens nothing. Ater changing over, Farmer started, Little making a good return. Following some loose scrambling play. l' tyter claimed a free kick, and sue- eeeded in findine ' iie the St. Helena Garters. By it •sv stages St. Helens forwards got the ball to the home ' .1 ~ Owieg to Abrams receii,ing injuries, the Runeprn Ii 'V the retrainin,,: rotzion of the game tl,e3. Ah tte.getting to work ae,ain Sudlow i a'.ner livened up the prow. lines by a sprint,but Hough- ton cut his journey very short by a smart tackle. St. Helens warmed to their work, and by the aid of Doherty ,and Liti.le got ttl,te ball to the home :line. A change was qeickly made bythe home forwards, sad a splendid kick by Myers put the ball into touch close to,the St. Helens line, whereable",a several strong attempts were made by Davie., J. Faullzner, and W. Faiiikrier to break through tliA defence. This, ht,wever, Was soot' brooght about, W. Faulkner reaki^.• a srearr, pas i to Myees.and the lat- ter noel over, StOr.ki, .1 ..or)(1 try.. Tele Denbabin made into a goal by ifood kick. The remaining portion of the . play was of a -,Vety keen •description, but ultimately , Runcorn won itf twO goala to nothing. ' WARM norm? Ex.u.r..i,oa 'v. Sane Asa FlELD.—Playee at sale cis S4t e tday list in grand weather. Sale co,. menc3d a:vinst a al,eitt tut eg..., and for some time play iNWP3 coot, "l! d in neetc.ll gnat. a:s. r Tie visitors' for- wards soon took play ou.: of ['airier and some grand pass,ag by their tt.cks saw "iv, 4.1 running in a good try, Carey incre„ sing its value with a neat goal. The v,sitora still kept up the pre •o re, but minors was their . only reward, the interval found the score a goal to nil :in favour of the Excelsior. Renaming, play was of an interelting CFIAI.k.:. ,AM a splendhl pi'ssitag movement, initia'..ed by MiSon and Wood and taken part in by Siddall Carey and Gleave saw the la' r running in a OCid try, Carey making a, good but unsuccessful attempt at goal. Fran the drop-oto the visitors again attacked and an- other round of passing star Siddall scoring a capital try, Carey failine. Mason. again Fecured the ball and da3h- lag away in good style passed to Siddall ' who posed to Gleave . but • the Warrington captain missed a grand ohance of scoring, and Roterts relieved. Knight here got hold, and working himself in good position drepoed a pretty goal, the remit of a p'easant and in- terei; ing game ending in favour of the Excelsiors by 2 goals 2 tti, s to nil. Purrcomv v. Sr. fivtatws " A" Tsiats.—These teams met at Sr, Helens on 8 tturday in grand weather. The following compc..ed the Runcorn team :—Jolley, hack; Richardson, Hickson, Gregory and West, three-quarter backs; W. Pendlebuiy and Woodward,, half backs; , Stubbs, Shaw, Urmson, Clarke, Derbyshire, Peacock, J. Pendlehury, and C. Pendlehury, forwards. Shaw started . and Runcorn immediately assumed the aggressive, and' after a dribble hy W. Pendlehurv, who crossed smartly, Shaw scored. Jolley kinking a magnificent goal from a difficult angle. From the rezeart St. Helens paid a brief visit .to the Rimeorn end, and a passing movement be- tween their backs was responded to by Jelley, with interce. The 'Runcorn forwards dribbed down, and from a scrimmage dose, to toe line, Woodward passed tn.W. Pendleburv, who in turn gave to Gregory, who ran in easily. Jolley failed to improve The drop out give hut little relief to the home team, and another bout of 'passing between the Runcorn hacks gave Gregory a try at the. corner, no goal resulting. St. Helens now played more strongly hut were completely overpowered, the score, at the, int-rval being Runeorn two gals four tries to nil. Restarting, the Runcornians were almost over in the first few minutes after a frhe run hi' West. The final result of a rather one•sided game was Runcorn, ,hree goala,seven tries ; St. Helens, nil. .‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>( 1 80. G. 33U3SET to no. the leuding Fowls 'Manistee- T tnrsrs, of Loudon. S.E , ;VIVO pnblishsd a Illl3t corn- pieta csslogue It is bound to inte -est Oriaketers, Godrrs, Trnnisers, Croquet Players an4l th, like. If you 0,1301: obt.sia a copy frorn the Loud Deller, send a post card to G. G. B. rind Co., 35 a love. Cricketers should get the G. G. B. Miry, price 6!_f L. post free.</p>
        <p>W-ARRINGrON V. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>These clubs met on Saturday at the Wildersorroh en- closure to play off their return erytagement. The fir 4 meeting at Widnes resulted in a pointless draw after a capital game. The ‘Vidnesians have had kmost success- ful season, andi•therefore, -.7hen ,they, stepped on to the field on Saturday, they were confident of victory. A noticeable feature of their matches this season was the fact that previous to Monday last w th4r lines had never h -en crossed twice in one match. Runcorn, how- ever, accomplished that teat on Monday, and to the astonishment of Widoes., tVarrington went even one bet- ter than that ; for on Saturday•Creir .lines were artually crossed thre •'; A. crowd .of between 5,000. and 6,000 assembled to watch the garrie.on Saturday,end the following is the list of the two opposise teams .--- Wartington Boscow,. full back ;, B Heesom and Broady, thrr 1.-quartns ; Bite and' lina2h- ton:, • teks ; Turner, Taylor; Berry, Swift, Dakin, •Bailey, ; !nderd and Burrows, forwards. Widnes : Brennan, lull hack ; Peters,Neoitt, Rispan and Clark, thr •-qtaarters ; Berner and Doihrlas, half- hacks; Derbyshire. Wheeler.). Handley, Forster," Alford, R.?e3e, Peters and Ernmery, forwards. Turner lot ti and had to kick off in the face of a •Zazzling, sun. _Drennan exchsr.,:.; ' and Elperrom found t Nick past the cen•re g</p>
        <p>e"0</p>
        <p>_ ceme danger,us fur Douglas ended in the latter -•a, tackled by tea. front of the goal pests. Tnis danger to WarringtaiN was only temporary for from the ensiiieg scrimmage the Warrington forwards, beaded by Taylor, Turner; Dakin, and Beiley took the play to the opposite end of the field. A brilliant piece of passing, taken part in by Houghton, Bate, fleesom, Burton. and Broady ended in the latter being knocked into touch. Taylor now secured, and forcing his way through his opponents pot within an ace of scoring b4ore he cool I be socurely tackled. This let off was only temporary for Berton brolco'hway and cot over, where he lost ranssession, but Houchton by dropping on the ball opened the scering, for Warrington. Heesom's kick for goal almost Came off, the ball striking the upright. Sean after the re,tart Widnes were attacking,and good passing between Clarke Barber, and Douglas, saw the litter get over. Barter landed a goal from a difficult angle. Warrineton com- menced again with a will to wipe off the arrears, and Widnes had a hot time of it: Their defenders had a very anxieus time of it and did not prove equal to the strain, for Turner securing from a line out forced his way for- ward,and passing to Taylot at the right moment.t he lat., ter ran almost hag the length of the field literally forcin, himself through his ooponente, and got over amid great enthusiasm,carrying:Bren n an and Forster over along with him. Burton made a good attempt at goal from the eiffi-, cult place.bnt the ball fell short. Warrington were a pain the fore less than a minute later and a magnificent round of passing. beeween Bate,Hoeghton, Burton, llepeom and Barber, saw the latter race away to the line. Great was the disappointment when it was d'ee,oveied that he had grounded on the wreng side. Widnes now had Miele relief for shortly efter this narrow escape, another round of passing betveeen Bate. Barber, Fleesom and Berton ended in the latter romping over. and Fleesom further auemented the score by kicking- a goal. Widnes for a time after this were put on the offensive by Barber, and Peters broke away at the centre line and got a clear field. Broady however at the opposite side of the field pay, chase, and succeeded in flooring him just on the line. Play from now until half time was in favour 'etf War- rington, and the interval arrived with the score War- rington one goal two tries, Widnes one goal. Barber re-started the second half, and play throngh- out was of the most enjoyable description. Roland.' of passing be' ween the whole of the Warrineten backs were continually witnessed which equalled any display of the famous Oldham hacks. Soon-after the commencement Flouehton„ Broady, Burton and Barber hall a round of passing and the latter w,o; just run into touch by Rispen at the nick of time. Clarke then by a good run trans- ferred play tn4the neutral territory, hot this was only temporary, for probably the best run of passing of the afternoon ensued. It was taken part in be no fewer than six players, namely Houghton, Bate, Barber. Heesom, Berton, and Broady. The latter made a grand run, but went in touch just at the corner. The same player had similar leek a few minutes later. The great speed at which the players had been going the whole of the afternoon began to tell towards the close of the game and though the play was of an open charanter.the players slackened their efforts. and the game was fought elair fly between the visirrs' 25 flee and the centre. Full time arrived with the same score as at the interval, viz., War- rington one goal, two tries (11 points), Widnes a goal (5 ,aoints).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RUNCORN v. WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>- ' The return match t.?tween Thincorn and Warrington took pier.. at Irwell-lane enclosure on Wednesda,v night. before 3,000 spectators. the weather was very fine,and ths f'e'd in good coaarlition,so that all the conditions for ,a capital game were present,. These expectations ware realised, fora splendid exposition of Rugby foothill was the rr suit, in whie,,howpvor, luck favoured the Runcorn team more than Warrington. The following weir: the contestants :—Runcorn : Houghton hack ; Warder, Crompton, and Conner (rtrarlforel), and Dinhabin.thr-e- -qualter backs ; J Faulkner and G.Rnbinson,half hacks; W. Faulknee Langley, Taylor, Giyter,Fatmer,Williams, Lightfoot, and Walker, forwards. Warrington Hos- cove, back ; Bartr:r, Heesom, Burton, and Brnariv.three- rtuart:ir hacks ; Bateand Flntaghton.half hacks ; Turner, Dakin, Taylor, Bailey, Berry, Sander*, Swift, and Bur- rows, fnrware.s. Referee : J. H. Smith. Widnes. Turner lost the trigs and had to kick off in the face pf a blinding sun which was alMoit, setting. Rougliton returned well to near' half way. From the line out, J. Faulkner took weil,fint found Dakin too alert 'to let him get far. A free kirk was soon ferthcaming against , Wairingtnn. and W. Faillialer made good use, of it, rut. tins:, his side on the offensive. A round pf passing betvreen Cooper, Crompton and Dunhabin took play to the front of the coal PCIStg." and a Warrington player attempting to kick in touch sent the hall right into Dianb hands and the latter was thus enabled to get over without any effort. The same player took the place but failed. Runcorn again attacked, and Berber tackled Crompton well in a dangerena position. At length the ba'l got lenze behind the Warrinenn players and another score seem,ed inevitablr, when Fieeaom cleared the clan- ger hv kicking into touch. The Runcorn forwardsa gain rushed away and Bate saved i ist in the nick of time. Belief came to Warrington at length by a break away lam Taylor. and Bate further supplemented him, tiles bring'- in 7 the ball to the centre. This relief was only tempor- ary, for passing between Warder, Cooper and Crompton ended in Baeeow saging in a marvellous manner under the gnal-posts. This attack was broug,ht to an end hi' a minor being conceded From the kick. put. Warder secur-d and pas,•erl to Cooper. and sulzsequently the ball . vv,rat through about hair-a-d, ten hands, and at length Farmer got rarer the line in the same place al the last try which was gained. Dunhahin's kick was a C.I.V near thing, for the hall struck the goal posts. A mark Was made by Taylor soon afterwards, frarn which Crnmaten made his tnawk in a favourable position. Conp,?r tn-k the place and landed a fine goal. Warringtrin commeneed to pre'd alter this, and got within the homesters' 25 for the first time. This Was however only temporary, and fins pass- ing among the Runcrarn for yards. and afterwards Crnmntnn and Dunbahin regained moat of the lost grnund. Barber now became prominent and by a long run, kick, and dribble again took play well into Run- cOrn territory. Ralf time soon afterwards arrived with the teams in mid-field, with Runcorn leading by a goal and two trles (10 points) to nil. W. Faulkner restarted the second half, and Broady —taking a dishing run .a.tP winqinn "he advantage, nil further passing between Burton, neesnm, Broady, and Barber took play to the Runcorn line. After a short break awes-, Houglatrin was collared an the line, and the ball waa at length sot over, hut only a minor resulted. Warder made a spurt, hut Bur- ton securing ran well, and this Wag continued by Burrows, hut the latter was tackled just in time. Warrington kept rip the ttack. and Burton aernss to Barber, the latter just failed to aeon', Donbanin kicidng dead. Another minor was ,soon forth- coming by a drop at. goal by Burton, and less than two minutes afterwards, two rather minors had been con- ceded. Runcorn scarcely had a look in at this portion of the game, hut Warrington could gain nothing hut minors of which seven or eight were conceded. The play was of a very open description, and once Burton get clear away from all but floughtnn No one was near to support hirn,however,and a splendid opportunity was lost. From the ensuing scrimmage. Bate again got away but he succeeded no better than Burton. Bready now attempted a flying kick, and followed am an well that ha was almost on to Houghton before the latter touched down. Near the clew of the game,Runcorn paid a hrief visit, to the Warrington anarters, but were promptly re- pci!ed, and Saunders. Turner, and Taylor headed a dribble to half war. Warrington were attacking hotly again at the close of the game hut nothing further was scored, and Warrington had to retire b*aten by ten points to nil. For the winners, finughtnn played a fire game in the first half, hut his display was equalled by that of Bmenw in the last half. The rival threequarters were a brilliant lot, the hest inan on the Runcorn side being Warder, whilst the Warrinenn men were more 07 an equality. Houghton at half,plared a good rome. hut Rate was not so safe as he usually is. The Runnorn for- wards undoubtedly won the eame,fer their passing was almost equal to that of the threrquarters. The match was a very enjoyable one, free from any rough nlar,and the referee'a duties in this respect were very</p>
