      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. MO.SSLEY Hitt.—This match was played on Saturday t Runcorn. Mossley Hill played J. Clarke of the Runcorn team left half, having only ten players of their own club. The teams were as follows :—N4 oss- ley Hill : Goal, W. Evans ; full backs, Atkinson and Lodge ; half backs, Cohan, Burgess, Clarke ; forwards, McGee. McKay, Smith, Kilburn, and Cottier. Runcorn : Goal, McLeod ; full hacks, Ledsham and Carter ; half hacks, rye. F. Burrows, and Green ; forwards. Hough, Jones. Baylis'. Peacock. and C. Burrows. Peacock de- feated Lodge in the spin and Smith kicked off against son and wind. kn evenly contested game was wit- nessed in the first half, as is indicated by the score which stood Runcorn, three, Mossley Hill, three. Baylie restarted and Peacock got away. but Burgess got pos- session and sent the ball up the field. The visitors kept un a severe pressure', but the home backs played up Pye and F. Burrows being especially prominent with some smart tackling. Eventually the leattaer was worked back to the vi.iters' quarters by smart passim". Lodge relieved, and Burgess getting hold ;sent on to I Smith, who again added to the visitors' score. The Blues now put in all they knew, and some dangerous attar:its were made open Evans, and play for some time raged in his vicinity. The siege was, however, raised and Smith and Co. went along in fine laThion,and from a paFt by Cottier,‘McGee, who was waiting a trifle too near the h,.ine goal; cent through, hut was of course ruled offside. The Blues again made strenuous offorts to equalise. and had C. Burrows been in a centering humour the game would certainly have been pulled out of the fire. Time was fast approaching', and although the Blues continued to play for all they were worth, the equalising • goal failed to come, and a splendidly contested and exneylingly pleasant game resulted in a win for Mossloy aill by four goals to three. ST. MARY'S Y SOTTON VICTORIA (RESERVE) —St. Marys paid a visit. to Sutton on Saturday to play their last match in the Warrington and District League. Both clubs worn well reoresent9d, Sutton playing several of their first team. Play in the first half was very even, neither side chiming the advantage, and at the interval the score stood—Sut:on, 2 goals; St. Miry's. 1 goal. The opening stages of the second half were mostly in favour of the visitors who failed to take advantage of the chances. To homeaters now had a chance. but the Saints' defense was safe. Play now settled more even, : both goals being visited in turn hut nothing came of it. The vi-itors had several good chances of scoring, but owinl• to erratic shooting could not manage to score and h 0 to retire defeated by 2 goals to 1. Newirois-tv-Ws T. LOW RRSERV a V. Wmwtca.—This re- placed League fixture came off on the Newton ground on Saturday last. Pennington kicked off for Winwick, the home team taking up the running, but met with a Atubborn defence, Ratcliffe fisting oat some warm shots. Grind forward nlay enabled Dudgeon to notch the first point. a feat that was greeted with loud applause. A second goal was not long in coming, Beresford and Houghton tAing the hall right through the Winwick dEfetare, the fatter putting on the finishing touch. Just before the interval, after a grand combined run, Lee scored again for the homesters who were leading at that part of the game hr 3to O. In the second half Conpull soored for 'Winwick, and Beresford and Holcroft for Newton, and when time was called, the homesters had won a very pleasant game by 5 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
