      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>GRES r WISES'.</p>
        <p>The annual, vestry meeting in connect;on with Great Sankey Church, was held on Tuesday at the church and then by adjournment at the Sunday 'school. There was a goal attendance of parishioners, and the chair was occopied by the Vicar (Rev. E. S. Jackson.) There were also present, the Rev. J. R. Jones (curate). Messrs. W. F. Skelton. J. 8. Aitken, J. Millington, J. Dignutn, W. D. Jeans, W. Longshaw, W. Taylor, T. Newcomb, O. Clarke, J. France, Jones Forster, H. M. Cartwright. and J. Hayes.</p>
        <p>TIM /ATE MR. RICHARD BOARDMAN. The Vicar slid that before the business of the meeting began, he would rc ter to thf ir late sexton. He had known him for a great many years and had worked v!itil him fora great many years, and a more courteous, obliging, and willing man he had not met. , Do• •frin the eleven yeas that he had worked with Mr. Boardman, there nad never been an unpleasant word passe ,i betw,en them. He regretted very much that he (the vicar) was not at Sankey, when thwaeparture of ther acacton took rlace, or he would 'eertaiely have been pi e,ent Le testgo his lespect.</p>
        <p>CHURCEIWARDEN'S ACCOUNT. Mr. J. B. Aitken E,titi that be was sorry to sav that the arr,,unts were not SO favourable this year as in the pre- i.evlify. year. It was not due, however, to ay falling in the collections, in fact, they had rather increased,but the expenditure had increased and they had unfortun- ntely to pay a large number (Cold accounts of log Etandinz, which amounted to about £25. The year com- menced with a balance of £53 Ls., an the church col- lections for 53 9nndays amounted to £243 4s. 51. From this amount £72 Bs. I.ld. must he ts ken for special col- lections, whicli thus left 1123 184. Cl. to be accounted for by the wardens. The balance in hand was only 13. when all the expenses were paid. Mr. Aitken suy,gested , that auditors be appointed to. audit the ' accounts.— ' Mem&amp; G.Clarke and J. France were acccrdingly ap- , pointed, and after auditing them, they certified them to be correct.</p>
        <p>TPF STATE OF THE DRURCII. Mr. W. J. Houghton called attention to the dilapidated state of the church. fie thought that something should be done. lie also thoughtthat the accounts for Penketh should be submitted separately to the meetinr,.—The V.car said that Penketti did not receive anything from the church save a small amount towards Mr. Parkinson's salary, and a small sum for insurance. He thought that the church was in good order. He believed he had a rritic.l eye, and he could not see very much. wrong with it. Of cause it was an old building.—Mr. Digninn and Mr. Newcomb still contended that the accounts for the Penkf th mission should be submitted to the meeting so that they cauld know how matters straid.—Mr. Aitken said that they had nothing to do with Penketh. If they wished to know the position of affairs there, they could see it in the parish inagarine each year. APPOINTMENT OF AUDITORS. Mr. Dignum ',moved that Messrs. Newcomb and G. Clark be appointed auditors to go over the accounts which were to be submitted next year.—'l.r. Warburton seconded.—The motion was carried, the Vicar also sug- gesting that the accounts be printed. • ELECTION OF CAURCHAVA RORER. ' The Vicar announced that he had chosen Mr. Peter Dumbed as ha warden for the ensuing year.—Mr. W. P. Shelton proposed the election of Mr. William Taylor as people's warden.—Mr. Aitken secon•le,l, and Mr. Newcomb supported the motion which was.carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ELECTION OF SIDESICEN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF SIDESICEN.</p>
        <p>On the Motion f Mr. Newcomb seconded by Mr. W. D. JEADS.Messrs....T. Mainley, J. Fairclough, J. Warbur- thn J. Forster. 1. Millington, J. Woodward, H. Ireland, W. Longtori, P. Norman and H. M. C.trtwrifeht,were ap- pointed sidesmeu. • LAY REPRESENTATIVES. On the motion of Mr. jeans, seconded by Mr. Long. shaw, Messrs. Aitkin and Dignum, were appointed lay reptv3entatives to the ruri-deconal confe:iencc. APPOiNTiaNT OF SEXTON. A vote of condolence was passed to Mrs. IL Boardinan and family, after which the Vicar said that the appoint- ment of a new sexton lay entirely in the hands of him- self and the wardens. He would promise that they would do their best to choose a suitable man.</p>
        <p>• TOTES pr WANKS. The Vicar proposed, and Mr. Jeans seconded a vote of tharks to all the officials in connection with the church, and this was passed unanimously. A hearty vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding, which WAS proposed by Mr. Skelton, and seconded by Mr. G.Clarke, brought ti.e.tneeting to a terminati an.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BURTON WOOD.</head>
        <p>BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>, The St. Michael's vestry meeting, was held on Monday niglit, the Rev. E. M. Mitchell (vicar) presiding. The Vicar said that, having had such a successful year, he hoped thdre would not be any • attempt on the part of any foolish people to disturb the harmony which had prevailed. He was prepared to receive the name of any cammunicant for the post of people's warden.—Mr. Haslam doubted if it was absolutely necessary they should be communicants.—Mr. Gordon nomin-;•ed Mr. Perrins, and Mr. Gleam seconded.—Mr. Haslam moved an amendment that Mr.T. Dutton should be the warden. —The Vicar read the notice convening the meeting. He said it was for the election of a churchwarden, and de- clined to accept Mr. Dotton's name.—Mr. Dutton : On wl. it ground P—The Vicar: I declime to state the .glo mds. You are not eligible.—Mr. Dutton: State your grounds, Mr. Mitchell.—The Vicar: I beg to differ w6h you.—There being no further nomination,the Vicer declared Mr. Perrins,appointed. Liter in the proceed- ings, Mr. Bradley asked if the Act stated that church- wardens should be commanicants.—The Vicar : In what Act ?—Mr. Raddley : In the Act concerning vestry meet- ings.—The Vicar : It is not stated ; it is implied.—Mr. Mr. Ellddley : I understood any parishioner.—The Vicar : You may take it for granted that means nothing whatever. If you like to try the case you may do so. I I shall be very glad if you will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ALL SAINTS' MISSION.</head>
        <p>ALL SAINTS' MISSION.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting was held on Monday even- ing, the Rev. H. V. Pigot, vicar of St. Paul's, Presiding' and there were also present the Rev. A. H. Townsend, Mr. T. P. Worley, and Mr. H. Williams. The church- wardens' accounts for last year were submitted, show- ir,c, an income, including the balance from last year, of £29 3s. oti. The expenditure was £24. 13s. 9d., leaving a balance in hand of £4 9s. 311. Mr. Peter Slidlow was appointed vicar's warden, Mr. T. P. Worley people's warden,and Messrs. Butler and W. Hall sidesmen. After a vote of thanks had been accorded to the Chairman for presiding tbe meeting closed.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. . THE NAVIGATION OF THE MERSEY. SERIOUS OFFICIAL REPORT. The annual report of Admiral Sir G. El. Richards, act- ing conservator of the Mersey, dealing with the state ,or the navightion of the estuary during 1894, has been published this waek. The greater part of the report is devoted to the Ship Canal, and to the discussion of the question as to whether, in accordance with section 35, sub-section 3, of the Ship Canal Act of 1885, the tidal waters of the Weaver which flow into the canal pass into the estuary at a point not below the outfall 'of the Weaver. Admiral Richards, upon consideration of the whole case, expresses the opinion that when this Act was passed sufficient data did not exist to enable a correct judgment to be formed on the question of whether the deposited plans of the tidal openings in the canal were or were not sufficient to satisfy the provision laid down in this section. liowetfer, he now thinks it be certain that after the level of the tidal openings has been reached a considerable amount of water remains in the Weaver estuary which should be discharged into the MerseV according to the section of the Act of 1885 just quoted. a This, nofortunately, is not the worst feature of the case. The acting-conservator also points out the deteriorating effect of the Ship Canal works on the upper tidal reaches of the Meney. Be says :—The question as to the upper portion of the canal is probably of a more serious character, inasmuch as the tidal por- tion of the river at Warrington. and for some miles below it, has silted up,occasioninga considerable loss of depth, which has been partly caused by the diversion of the upper waters from their normal course. This silting up has decreased the sectional area and tidal capacity of the river; those navigating in this vicinity assert that since the canal was opened the tide does hot rise so high as it used to do, and there can be little doubt of the cur ectness of these statements. Another important matter- is that by the construction of the piers of Runcorn Bridge and the Ship Canal the original width of theriver. at Runcorn-1,400 feet—has been reduced by nearly one- fourth, or 305 feet in_all. Ihe conservator remarks upon the fact that by sub-section 12 of section 88 oi the Act the Canal Company were not allowed to abstract from the Mersey more than oneitenth of the downward flow past Howley Pier at Warrington,and it was enacted that they should construct anoi maintain certain works to ensure the due carrying out of the clause. The con- servator states that the works constructed have failed to effect this purpose, for the greater portion of the upper waters has followed the straight line of the canal, rather than flaw into the serpentine reaches of the river. thus aiding the silting up of that portion of it referred to. Thee upper waters, of course, find their way into some part of the estuary by flowing over the Latchford locks or through the Fluicos, partly through the tidal opening at Old Quay, Runcorn,or lower down the canal, , hut the upper river between Warrington and Runcorn is , deprived of the scour,and unless adequate measures are taken must become at no distant time unfit for naviga- ble or other purposes. The Warrington authorities are protected by the /1,•,:t of 1885 from any damage that may accrue ty the construction of the canal, and it may be • briefly mentioned that a law suit has just been concluded • between Messrs. James Fairclough and Sons, of Mersey Mills, Warrington' under section 90 of the Act, which section is entirely for the protection of the interests of that firm. The canal company are no undertaking works in the hop of remedying this serious condition of things. The Mersey Conservancy is equally interested in the preservation of the navigation of the river up to the tidal limits) and is, moreover, deeply concerned that</p>
        <p>the fl ow of the upper waters shorld b 3 maintained in their normal channel in the interests of the estuary itself."</p>
        <p>.11.I:EGED RECONSTRUCTION OF THE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool "Shipping Telsgraph " of Tuesday states :—lt will have been noticed that the shares in the Manchester Ship Canal have remained steady in spite of the approaching crisis it: the affairs of the company, which must come to a climax in June neat, when the money must be found to pay the interest then due on debentures. Although the investment would not thus appear to be a particularly favourable one it seems that a certain amount of buying-in has prevailed in Manches- ter and other stock markets. The reason for this is said I to lie in a report which we have received on good auth- ority, and credited by business men, that a syndicate is being formed in London to acquire the property by pav- ing on the basis of £4 per ordinary share, £6 for each of the RIO preferenc. shares,and in lull for the debentures, leaving out of consideration the amount advanced by the Manchester Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PARISH COUNCILS.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Parish Council was hold on Monday evening in the infants' classroom at the Board School. The following parish couneillors were present —The Rev. IL 1. Wacleson (hon. clerk a.,1 vicechair- man), and Messrs. Joshua Ball, Sanwel Thohason, and With. n Crane Southern ; also Messrs. Reginald Owen, son. (chairman of the Council), and Lewis Oirchad,,sen. (oversee, George' Brimelow (assistant overseer),Henry. Ernest Southern. James Cawthorn°, and James Gleave. The Rev. H. I. Wadeson was voted to the chair pro tern. —Mr. Ball propo.?ed that Mr. Owen be again appointed chairman. This was seconded by Mr. Thomen seri unanimously carried.—Mr. Ball moved' and Thomason seconded the re-election of the Rev. H. I. Wadeson as vice.chairman add clerk,which was also carried. Messrs. Lewis Birchall, sen., and Reginald Owen, ssu. were ap- pointed overseers.—The Parish Council could not inter- fere with the appointment of assistant overseer, the pre- sent assistant overseer's (Mr. Georee Brimelovr) .ap- ' pointmenidating 30 years ago.—Mr Ball was appointed , waywarden, and Messrs. James Gleave and rharles Wild were also ,re-elected burleymen for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>PRECEPT. It was stated that a precept would be signed and served on the overseers for the sum of LW for the ex- penses ot the Council up to the 23:M Of Sep:ember next This was all the public business. LATCHFORD wrra OUT. The annual meeting of the Ltteliford Without .Parish Council was held in the Baptist Schoolroom on Monday evening. when there were present—Councillors' Percy Davies, 11. Roberts, W. Pierpoint, and J. Lee, Mr. W. Riehaidson, and Mr. G. Pilling (clerk). ELEcTioN OF CHAIRMAN AND VICErCHAXRAI Mr. Pierpoint occupied the chair pre ten.—Mr. Roberts proposed the re-appointment of Mr. DAvies as chairman for the ensuing twelve months.— Mr. Richardson seconded, and it was carried.—Mr. Davies briefly returned thanks. If there was any assistance he could render the Council he should be only too pleased to give it.—Mr. Lee , proposed, and Mr. Richardson seconded the appointment of Mr. Piereeint as vice-chairman. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Tltg OVERSEE fn. Mr. Lee proposed that Messrs. Vierpoint and Richard- son be appointed oversoers.—Mr. Roberts seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>ADOPTIVII ACTS. The Clerk read a letter fro. Mr. J. Ashton, clerk of the Runcorn Rural District Council, who said he begged to call their attention to sectioo 53 sub-sections (1) and (2) of the Local Government Act, 1894, and to request that the Council would be good enough to inform him as early as possible whether any of the adoptive Acts were in force in their parish and if so (1) whether any corn-I mittee hacilbeen appointed under sub-section one, and (2) whether arvy joint committee had been appointed I under sub-section two. With respect to the latter they would obsek ve the appointment of a joint committee was compulsory, not permissive, and if not already acted upon, should be acted upon without delay.—Mr. Davies remarked that they had not adopted any of the Acts at present, and were hardly in working order yet, and Mr. Pierpoint obServed that they were in a state of wonderment as to what would come next. They did not know exactly where they were.—The Clerk was in- structed to answer in accordance with what Mr. Davies had said.</p>
        <p>_ ELECTION OF CLERK. Mr. Davies proposed, and Mr. Lee seconded the re-appeintment of Mr. Pilling as clerk, Mr. Lee referring to the services Mr. Pilling had rendered the Council. The motion having been unanimously carried, Mr. Pulling returned thanks and the meeting concluded. LITTLE SANKEY. The annual meeting of the Little Sankey Parish, Coun- cil was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday night. There were present—Messrs. Matthews, Corra- dine, Grimshaw„ Greenwell, Brunch, Jackson. and Wil- liamson, with Mr. J. L. Tunstall (clerk', and Mr.,Toseph SMith (assistant clerk). Mr. Greenwell was voted to the chair pro. tens.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Mr. Matthews was unanimously electe4 chalrman on the motion of Mr.Williamson,seconded by Mr.Greenwell. Mr. Matthews thanked the Council for re-electing hint. He hoped now that the excitement of the borough enguiry was over,they, would be able to do a little useful work in the parish. He did not know that ther was very much to do, but what there was to do thee would endeavour to carry out to the best of their ability. He thanked them very much for re-electing him. Messrs. Greenwell, Williamson.and Corra.dine were elected over- seers, and Mr. Greenwell, vice-chairman of the Council. On the motion of Mr. Williamson, seconded by Mr. Brough, Mr. Matthews was,appointed waywarden for the township.</p>
        <p>COLLECTION OF RATES. The Chairman said the next business WAS to " eon eider the advisability of adopting Section 4 of the Poor Rate Assessment and Collection Act, 1869, with respect to the rating of small tenements and to take such action as may be deemed fit." He would ask Mr. Tunstall to explain the subjtmt.— Mr. Tunstall explained the effect which the adoption of this section would exercise, and added that such section had been adopted by most of the large towns. It not only benefited the administrative body, but also the property owner, the latter being entitled to a discount of 15 per cent. under certain conditions en uninhabited tenements of the rateable value of £8 and under. It was just questionable whether it was necessary for the Coun- cil to pass this res.obition, seeing that Warrington had passed a similar resolution. But as they were doubtful whether such a resolution would affect them, it WAS just as well to pass the resolution now themselvez.—ln reply to Mr. Greenwell,Mr. Tunstall said the Act was in force in all large towns.—On the motion of the Chairman. seconded by Mr. Williamsod, it was resolved to adopt Section 4 of the Act named. TER AUSEEED NUISANCE NEAR BUTTERMILK BRIDGE.. Attention was called by the Chairman to a notice which had been given subsequent to their last meeting concern- ing a nuisance alleged to be created by the depositing of manure on the banks of the Sankey Canal adjacer.,t to Sankey Hall Farm. A ratepayer had mentioned the matter, and had expressed a wish that something should be done by the Council at their present meeting to have the nuisance abated. The Chairman thought, however. that the site alluded to was outside their jurisdiction. The Council were in a very awkward position. for they I would be interfering with the business of another town- ship, and had no power in the matter.—Mr. Carradine said there was no one in Sankey Bridges who was not convinced that the manure was a nuisance. Those who lived near it very nearly eat• it sometimes let alone smell it. He should like something to he done.—The Chairman thought the only course the Council could pursue was to call the attention of the Rural District Council to the matter. They were the sanitary authority and ought to attend to it and if tey failed then the Parish Council could call the attention of the County Council to it.—On the motion of Mr. Cerradtte,sedoncled by Mr. Jackson. it was resolved to call the attention of the Rural District Council to the alleged nuisance. It was also decided, on the suggestion of the Chairman, to write to the Great Sankey Parish Council asking for,their support.</p>
        <p>ELECTION EXPENSES. Mr. Jackson observed that be had been looking over the expenses of the elections, and he had been told that one of the counters had had no fee for counting. HP should like the Councfi to instruct the Clerk to writ. tl Mr. Sutton asking for a list of the authorised counters, and who were paid.—Mr. Tunstall said he had inquired of Mr. Sutton, who had informed him that there were four counters.. Three were paid apd the fourth declined to be paid. The counters were paid by Mr. Shearer on Mr. Sutton's behalf, and Mr. Thompson declined to re- ceive his.</p>
        <p>THE CHARGING FOR PAILS. Mr. Jackson asked if anything had been done with re- gard to the matter brought forward by Mr. Restock at the parish meeting, namely, the Corporation oharzing for pails when they had already been provided by the owners of property.—The Chairman said he saw the Town Clerk at the Inquiry and he said they were not to be charged. He also said it was very unfair if any- one had to be charged.—Mr. Jackson thought the land- lords who had not already put pails in snonld be asked to do it now before the hot weather came. The middens were a great nuisance last summer. This concluded the business. I' MOORE. The annual meeting of the Moore Parish Council took place at the house of Mr. Henry de Prez on Friday even- ing. The following councillors were present Messrs. Thomas Dwerrybouse, Isaac Lawton,William Dunhabin, William G. Wilson, Peter Gleave, JohnHighfield,Edward de Prez, and Henry de Prez (clerk). Mr. Gletive took the chair pro tem. Mr. Wilson then moved, and Mr. Dunbabin seconded that Mr. Dwerryhouse be re-elected I chairman of Council for ensuing year, which was carried unanimously. On the motion of Mr. Lawton, Messrs. Henry Middleton and Peter Gleave were unanimously , appointed overseers of the poor. The Chairman an- nounced that he had received from the Clerk of the County Council model standing orders for the Parish'</p>
        <p>Ultirnateiy _G wad re ,-!,1 loLada, i.llO or(1 obtain a doz 0 copies for the use cf the councillors, an that their adoption be deferred to th 9 next meeting. It was agreed that a precept be made on the overseers for £2O, and that a cheque for a Us. 3d be signed for cost of election of parish councillors. The Clerk was in,..- structed to purchase; box for the keeping of paris" I books, kc., the Cost of which was not to exceed 455. The election of delegate to act with the Vicar of Dares: bury in the distlibution of the Hall-lane. Her ar,FI Bennett's Charities, then took place, and on the ref)°,4" thin of Mr. Gleave, seconded by Mr. Duistai, n, Dwerryhouse was appointeitto the positibn. To the question of footpaths, he., and it. was uaanlmou:l.s' decided that the clerk write Mr. C. L. Evans, caiii"g attention to the dangerous eta t 3 of the footraw,h at t":, hottom of the "Eight Acre" field in the octiupati)a y' Mr. Thomas Weir, and ask him to be good enoup improve it. A vote of thanks to the Chairman or Pr</p>
        <p>siding terrnicsted the proceedinnis. GRAPPENBALL. The annual meeting of the above Parish Council held on Friday evening, April lath, but owing to jlea 111,11, understanding in time the meeting was declared• and ths foal meeting was held on Monday ev"a1,11::, the National Schoolroom. There were preterit A. Lister Kaye, W. Lomas, T. Burrows, R. f3oardinarnj R. Warburton, J. IFa'rinorst, S. Jenkins, Bradford. a S. Wcodcock (clerk). In the absence of Mr. W. rill who was indisposed, Mr. A. L. lilye took the chair, proposed that the business of the previous mee_t; - read over and confirmed. Mr. Jenkins said that I! last meeting was illegal they ought to go through business again,just as though there had been no inert. t'r,, before. Mr. Kaye withdrew his proposition Floa , business was fully proceeded with. Mr. A.L. posed that rn. 7Tilliam Long be chairman for toe an'o ir'r 'i, - ' P. Boardmac, a d • to Mess,a. i.daarunian • Loa p 3 overseers for the ensuing year. It was propos" Kaye that Mr. S. Woodcock be appointed assistion.,%,,l seer for the ensuing year. This was secondou_icta carried. Mr. Kaye proposed Mr. Woodcock as rho l; the Council. 'fins was seconded and carried no salary was decided by a majority of one to be ioog Kaye proposed that Mr. John Murray, of Parr Company and the Alliance Batik Limited, Warrin'Csas be appointed treasurer of the Connell. 'Cllgirrie seconded by Mr. Jenkins, and unanimously This concluded the business. •</p>
        <p>LOWER WALTON. parish The annual meeting of the Lower VValton Council was held at the house of Mr. W. Palin ou day evening. Mr. F.Morilts,J.P.,C.C.,occupied tb° Mr. John Weir proposed, Mr. John CartwriJlt )il he ' and it was unanimously cirried that Mr • „loh° appointed chairman for the ensuing year. o„"isapth Weir was appointed vice-chairman, Messrs:l- and Burgess, and Henry Dodd were re-elected overseers;aing Mr. Joseph Whitfield, assistant overseer for the e° , j, year. Messrs. John Weir William Turner, a°" Cartwright were appointedburleymen, hut no °°7„s was appointed, Which has been usual heretofore ." township. e')UR LOCAL CHURti Es—WARRIP</p>
        <p>ViTYCLIFFE P.S. A. At the Usual meeting on Sunday afternoon VIC of was occupied by Mr.J.A.longe,l3.A..of Wake,field.s°!;p4 the Rey. John Yonge, acid secretary of the Zion GI:1„: be P.S.A. at Wakefield. In the course of his addre%.`,3se:.' said he had peculiar gratification in presiding ticUbeciarlf he was greatly interested in P.S.A. worl,,,end par in P.S.A. work at Wycliffe. He did not beaeve ig five adverse criticisms of the mettement 'which were quently offered by persons who had never them3o to, been eye-witnesses of it. He was coofident tb,,oleee rtioVement was a very great blessing to a larg.o.l!",resD' of men, not only at Wycliffe .bfit elsewhere. -.1- '9 O icY many schemes were being ventilated in these persons, all of them anxious to uplift their fellowwiso but one fatal objection appeared to him to cliam'" tee . most of them, via e—Tnat they affected troret and external conditions of life than influenced the ownt°' We spiritual conditions. To attempt to out man's 'ni ilt;) right without plyiug regard to the inner, wagls likeccocl6 ting the hands of a clock right and expectiug to continue right While the internal works were als,'""wes 'Med. He had every ,confidence that the P::eg a accompliehing a great moral reformation in the "reetli. multitudes of men. Mr. James Evans of were fled, a also gave a good address which was listened tecre °roe great amennt of attention. Nan violin solos dered.by Mr. R. H. Pearson with his accustage--_,„,, ;b of lk Considei able interest was manifested in the reau".; oh( letter from one of the members of the moveMell: " lai gone to reside in America, and Which Fi10074 werit though he himself was far away his heart and mil' tong still with them. Messrs. S. Rowlands and A. I'll 00, part in the meeting at which there was a• 5 attendance. SERVICES FOR THE PEOPLE- qtr. The usual People's Service was held in tile 130"-'n P Wesleyan Chureli on Sunday afternoon, and sided over by Mr. W. Ashton. The address w3g„"titof by Mr. 