      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WELSH CRURCII.</head>
        <p>WELSH CRURCII.</p>
        <p>DISESTABLISHMKNT .OF THE</p>
        <p>AIEETING AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Friday night in the Co- operative Hall, Warrington, under the auspices of the Liberation Society, to support the Goveruniont, measure for the disestablishment of the . Episcopal Chinch in Wales. The chair was occupied by Mr. E. J. Guest. BA., J.P., and among those present were: Major E. Jones, M.P., Revs. David Walters, J. Yonge, W.J. Kirkland and J. S.Hughes,Councillor Roberts, J.P., Messrs. A.H. Crosfield, T.l3irchall J.P., J. Ay. Hayward, E R. Roberts, M.A., L.L.8., S. B. Stringer,W. Roberts,W. Hickman, I. Hayward, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said they had been called together that night not because the promoters of the meeting bad any love of controversy, neither did they wish to import into the subject any feeling of bitterness. Be did not think there Should be any bitterness. It was a question of right or wrong, justice or iujustiee, and he thought it was only fair that they should Show their side of he question as those who believed in the Eetehlishment had already spoken in that Hall and shown their side. It had been said, by Mr. Gladstone he thought, that the Episcopal Church in Wales is a 'church 'of the few and that the Nonconformists are the church of the many The Epi copal Church in Wales was the church of the rich and the Nonconformist the church of the compare. tively'poor, and the Episcopal Church as established is contrary to the wishes of the great majerity f the people. (Hear, hear.) There could be no doubt that the Episcopal Church was the church of the few. In the year 1851 it was found that of all the accommoda- tion for diOne worship the Episcopal Church only provided 30 per cent. ' and the Nonconformists 70 per cent., and of those who attended divine worship only 22 per cent. were found in the Episcopal Church, and 78 per cent. in Nonconformist churches. It might be said that within the last 40 yerirs the Epir copal Church had made great advances, and no doubt it had in the large towns and in the watering places, but he thought it could also be shown that the Nonconformists had made equal advances. (Hear, hear.) When they wenefrotn these large towns and watering places into the country places they found the churches almost empty, with congregations varying from eleven and upwards, whereas the 'Nonconformist churches were oftentimes occupied mien to suffocation. The Episcopal Church was the Church of the rich and the Nonconformist the church of the comparatively poor, and yet the Noncon- formists, out of their comparative poverty contributed something like £400,000 to the meintenanCe of their churches , while the Establishment had an endowment of something like £250,000. And besides csntributing to their own churches the Non. conformists had to contribute, to some extent at any rate, to the endowments of 'the Established Church. This was not right, fair, or just. (Hear, hear, and ap- plause.) In regard to divendowment he could not say that he understood the question fully, but. he would deal with the Established Church with the utmost generosity. (Hear, hear.) As far as he was concerned, if the endow.. , meets could be shown to belong 'to the church let it have them. (Hear, hear.) But a great deal of the endowments of the Church in Wales undoubtedly belonged to the nation. (Applause.) They were given for the good of the nation, and not fora small minority. (Hear, hear.) Dealing with the charge that the Nonconformists were , seeking disendowment in order to benefit themselves, I the Chairman said the Nonconformists had not been noted for their greed, but for their self-sacrifice in the years gone by. The history of Nonconformity was the history of self-sacrifice. ,(Applause.) They . believed that if the Episcopal Church was (Reestablished it would win the hearts and the sympathies of the Welsh nation, and would do infinitely more good than it was doing at the present time. (Hear, hear and applause. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was received with ap- plause, moved the first resolution, as follows : " That this meeting cordially approves of the Bill ter the dis- establ'shment of the Episcopal Church in Wales, and trusts that it will speedily receive the assent of both Houses of Parliament." When he was asked to take an active pelt in that meeting ho felt that it might SEEM rash and presomptuous to do so because he could not pretend to any special ecclesiastical lore nor as he wired in the history of the Welsh Church. But he did take a great interest in Liberal politics,and that was one of the questions to.the fore. (Hear, bear.) Macauley said that edtication ought to consist in knowing every- thing about something and something about everthing, and he thought that was rather a useful definition for obscure politicians outside the House of Commons, and if they were to believe everything that was whispered about those ineidee` was sometimes one which might be' ,convenient even for them. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He hoped Major Jones would excuse him saying so. (Laughter.) In reference to that very question of Welsh disestablishreent and disendoWtnent he should not be surprised to hear that there were a good many , membets inside the House;who were not versed in all the intricacies of those ecclesiastical questions. (flear,hear.) Still, even if one only has a general knowledge, after Macauley's definition one was not obliged to write one- self down as, alesolutely illiterate. He certainly did take an interest in that question of emancipation in Wa!es. The main objection that was raised to the movement was raised by Mr. Balfour when he hinted that the. Liberal party was simply going in for spoliation. Well, they should no doubt hear more of these attacks which were made by high dignitaries of the Church who were donning their armour and were ready to beat as loudly as they had beaten before the ecclesiastical war-drum they were all femiliar with. Speaking from the point of view of an ordinary Liberal, he thought that what they had to do in dealing with this kind of attack was to bring public opinion to bear. As amg as they had public opinion behind them in the United Kingdom they I were right, but without the public opinion they were nowhere. And they had to say to the public that the Church in Wales occupied a false position, a position that was notadvantageous to the community am.l not advantageous to the church itself. Ho ad- vised them to read Mr. Asquith's able mei admirable speech which had now been reprinted as a penny pamphlet. Referring to the great .majority of Welch representatives in favour of (Imes- tahlishment he thought that what they had to ask them- seiveF,was whether if they had a similar majority in England the Church of England would last for a Binel6 year. They knew it could nor, and that being so had they any right to absolutely ignore public Opinion in Wales, as they should be doing if they swamped the Welsh majority and insisted upon the perpetuation of the present establiehment (hear, hear, and aPplause). _ . . . .</p>
        <p>Major JONES, M.P., then addreased the m-- e'in. ' said that he always approached that question rtlrietn bering :hat it was a controversy between branches of the great Christian family. He tried to make due allow- ance for the circunistances of life which caused a certain set of people to take one side, and placed another set of people to another' side. He however ~ contended that there was no authority in the book of the constitution of the Christian rligun which gave any colour or warrant for associating r&amp;igion with political power. Constan- tine made the cOurch a political power for Hellish reasons, and he was not a Christian—he had not been baptised— at the time he established the Christian religion. Not one of the religious wars which had taken place would have been urged had the rulers of that time recognised that they had no power over the opinions and sentiments of men. They had no quarrel with the Episcopalian form of worship. They only objected"to its connection with the state. Religion was an affair of tho heart and ,affections and sentiments, and it was little short of a mockery that church discipline should he controlled by the House of Commons by men of every degree.of mor- ality and every shade of religious belief and unbelief. It was to put an end to that that Walos sent 31 memners out of a total of 34, with an average majority which thev could not get anywhere else—an average majority of 2.000. (Applause.) He contended that although the torms of attempts to eradicate the characteristics of the Welsh people and to crush their desires' had altered, the desire was still there. There . was nothing which would please a Welsh bishop better than to, be told that the Welsh language was dying Out: The Bishop of St. David's had said that there was no such a thine as Wales. It was simply a geographical expression. But he (the •speaker) con- tended that the Welsh nation had customs and character- istics which had existed for centuries. (Applause.) One of the first men to raise his voice, and the morning star f Nonconformity was John Pernimi,and Lord A ty-rdare ! a devout churchman, had said that but for Noncon- formists, Christianity would have died Out in Wales. Wales had supplied paces hr pubic wer-hip for 65 per cent. of the people which wts much larger than in Eng- 1-md. In Angl, sea one single denomination provided places for public worship for 80 per cent, of the popula- tion. fie was certain that they could not find a similar imtance in the whole world. With rtgard to the rim. hors of the various denominations in Wales counting the number of church adherents upon the same basis as tle‘y did of the bioneeniormists bodies by multiplying the eommunicants, the, church scited-enta numbered 258.902 and the NoncoMformists 888,357, a au stantial maj rity, he was Rire, considering that the total population was. a. million and three- quarters. (Applause.) The number of communicants in the Chtirottof En&amp;anii according to the Church year te.,,k in 1894 was 118,756, while the communicants of tie four ii cipal N, no, rmist and not taking; in the tvinor tie-nominations, was 381.705. ;le, tl7ere- fore,showed that the third h was in a hopeless minority iind it waa not just to thrust a church which was in such a hopeless minority ureic the Welsh people. (App). . .</p>
        <p>Thp Iv. David Waiters also euppoiteci the resolution which vi as tlun put to the meei.ing and carried with Gray awe, diestintientß. A vote of thanks proposed by the Hey. J. S. Hughes tn the speakere and chairman brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>ST. EDWARD'S BRANCH OFTHIB LEAGUE OTTER CROSS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ryan pr,sided at too monthly council meeting f the above mentioned society held in'the League Rail, Tr vell-lane Runcorn. on.Se,nrclay evening.. The Council murred their sincere thanks to Miss Mack for her Li —ad gilt of two piztuies.</p>
