      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Zetters to the (Motor.</head>
        <p>Zetters to the (Motor.</p>
        <p>THE YACHT RACE. Sir,—The members of the Warrington and Bank Quay Sailing Club had their annual yacht race ore Saturday •last the 13th inst., but owing to an error in the starting of the yachts at Whines, the Committee have decided that the race is void and that the Cup will again be sailed for some time during the season.—Yours truly, GEORGE W. RIGBEY, Secretary. 79, Padgate-lane, Warrington, April, 1895.</p>
        <p>TRADE. • Sir,— A few days ago there was a paper hanging out- side a public-house in Warrington, which seemed to clearly indicate that the military authorities would use their influence to help time-expired soldiers into situa- tions in governmental and other working departments. Now some of these men have Government pensions which masters must naturally learn when enquiring into their characters, and can it be supposed that in these times of class interests, masters will long resist the temptations that may thus be placed in their way, of trying still more to bring wages down and these men who have perhaps fought and bled for their country may be expPeted to be the instruments to work this wrong against men who have to entirely depend upon the work of their hands for a living.—Your, Arc., A READER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SOCIALISM.</head>
        <p>SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, "Tony Lumpkin," would have your readers believe that for the workers to recover that which has been taken from them by fraud and force is robbery. We can trace historically that in early times when agriculture was the chief indbstry in this country, those willing to work had the opportunity to work. The willing worker to-day is denied that privilege. The Craft Guilds prove to us that they did not produce com- modities for profit but for the benefit of the community and reputation of their trade, thus showing the differ- ence between the present system of production and that which then existed. We find that the Poor Law was not in existence at that period,and pauperism was unknown. The brutal, cowardly and debased scoundrel, Henry Bth, entered into a conspiracy with his land retainer; and in 1536 they destroyed the monastries, colleges and hospi- tals, also appropriating the funds of the Guilds for their own use, yet the law allowed this. From 1760 to 1835, 7,000,000 acres of land were er.closed by Act of Parlia- ment. Can law alter the nature of robbery? The House e Commons has been and is still to-day a meeting place of capitalists and landlords. The National Debt is another example, of how ths down-trodden workers have had their interests looked after by these so-called intelligent men. It was estab- lished in the interest of the landowners of 1688, to rid them d the obligation of paying the expense of wars,hy taxing the labour of unborn generations. For the last 100 years the interest of this monstrous piece of robbery, has been £26,000,000 per year, besides the sacefice of thousands of hunosn lives. These wars are made in the interest of your so-called representatives and props of the present system of civilisation. When the workers begin to think for themselves and get their minds rid of the superstition and cant, then there will be no need of keeping an army of hired assas- sins, to butcher those who demand their rights. What we Social Detrrcrats (the only English S-..cialist Party) want is Liberty, Fraternity, and Equality. The only way to obtain it, is by the socialisation of the means and in- struments of production.—Yours, J. TUNGATE: Manchester, April 23rd, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WHICH IS Trip, TRUE CHURCH ?</head>
        <p>WHICH IS Trip, TRUE CHURCH ?</p>
        <p>Ran]</p>
        <p>•• - Sir,—As Mr. John Peers has taken up the cudgels in defence of "lona," perhaps he will be good enough to supp/kanent his " reply " to myself by attempting to adduce proof of his protégé's assertion that the Church of Rome, in addition to her other "iniquities," places the temporal interests of her followers unreservedly in the hands of an Italian Pope. I make the suggestion because I think that, in a discussion of this kind, it is much more satisfactory to essay the demonstratlon of even only one definitely-stated point, than to indulge in reckless gener- alities and attempt to prove nothing. It is all very well to describe the Catholic Church as a hardened sinner,ancl as an institution with its roots deep in Paganism. etc. ; but the ipse dixit of even a John Peers is open to ques- tion. When your correspondent " concludes " that, if there be a Church of Christ it is without pope,presbytery, or priest, he means, I take it, that there is no Divinely instituted and authorised teaching and governing body anywhere in the wild. His agnosticism of course inclines him to the assumption that the bible is simply a collection of myths and fables. Hence be has no press- ing inducement to familiarise himself with its teaching, and to this, I suppose, may be attributed his ignorance of the fact that the bible does bear witness to the neces- sity and existence of a teaching and governing body in the Church of Christ. The Messiah gave authority to govern when He said to His Apostles: "What things soever ye shall bind on earth shall be bmnd in heaven, and what things solver ye shall- loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven" (Matt. xviii 18). This same authority was given pre-erninently to Peter when his Master' declared : "I will give unto thee the keys of the king- dom of heaven; and whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth Shall be bound in heaven, and whatso.ver thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven " (Matt. /col 191. Again, Chiist gave authority to teach when He said to His Apostles : " Make disziples of all nations. . . Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I:com- manded you" (Matt. xxvii 10. 20). " Obey your pre- lates (or them that have the rule over you) " says St. Paul, "and submit to them, for they watch in behalf of your souls" (Heb, xiii 17).' In Acts xiv 23 we read that Paul and Barnabas ordained presbyters in every chutch. a Let the presbyters that rule well be count'' worthy of double honour, especially those who labour i7l the word and in teaching" (I Tim.. v 17). Christ "gave some to be anostles ; and some, prophets ; and some evanpr'' ; and some, pastors and teachers, . (80. 'i). If these passages, to which' a numb- ,:rnilar ones could he added, do not make it clear ti the Church of Christ is composed of teachers disciples, of rulers and subjects, what in the name of common sense can be their meaning ? And if they do prove the existence ot a teaching and governing bode in the church, it follows that the church is visible. This, surely, is a proposition which needs no demonstrating. The Jewish Church was undeniably visible, and characterised moreover by an elaborate ritual pre.e.ribed by God Himself. Why then ought the Christian Church to be devoid of ritual? Are Christians less in need of in- centives to devotion than were the Israelites? Is not their imagination just as susceptible of Patel nal influ- ence as was that of the Jews? Is not, for example, the smoke of incence as potently symbolical of prayer la the one case a 3 it Was in the other? Mr. Peers is " quite unconvinced " by the arguments advanced in my former letter, but he makes no attempt to disprove them. It Is true he alleges that the Scrip- tural quotations I gave are as applicable to the Greek Church as to the Church of Rome, hut he omit.; to ex- plain in what way the prophetic similitude of the net.fer example, finds its fulfilment in the Caesarian Church of the East. If there is one fact which the similitude in question points out more clearly than another, it is the Catholicity of Christ's kingdom on earth ; and if Mr. Peers' maintains that the Greek Chinch is Catholic, he cannot know the meaning of the word. In taking leave of my courteous critic I will only add that the " florid ritual" and "pompous observances " of the Roman Church are no proof that her doctrine., whether of faith or of morals, are not the doctrines of Christ ; andl that, because a man wears cope and mitre in the public dis- charge of ecclesiastical duties, it does not neces- sarily follow that he is devoid of the poverty of spirit which Christ has inculcatated. " Pies V., under his gorgeous vestments, wore day and night the hair shirt of I a simple friar ; walked barekot in the streets at the head of processions; found, even in the midst of his most pressins., associations, time fer private prayer : often regretted that the public duties of his station were unfavourable to growth in holiness ; and edified Ms</p>
        <p>flock by innumerable instances of humility, charity, and forgiveness of personal injuries." (Macauley's Essay on ke's History of the Popes.—Obediently yours, AUG. J. HILTON. 31, Arthur-road, North Brixton,London, S.W., April 16th, 1895.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Judging Mr. John Peers by his last performance which appears in your last issue, and the very great number of his previous efforts, whieh grace your columns, one is tempted to regard this " philosopher" as a slave of partisan reason ! The Ego pervades his compositions in a very marked manner ; and he rejoices in the fact that he belongs to that class of individuals , who presume to claim for their exclusive system of " reason," the dogmatic conclusions of their poor, nar- row, prejudiced minds that it ie the only infallible way to pure unadulterated truth !. There is such an air of " Podsnappery " about your even, or odd, "Rational- ist," that is really amusing. Anything he cam:at understand, he immediately puts behind him! "-Finita est," yours Podsnap ! The school of which our estimable Mr. P. (not Podsnap)isa pupil, would have us believe—A (the Mythical School)—that Christ was but a myth ; B. The older rationalistic school in Germany by the same process of" Reason " (by way of illustrating the infallibility of their pure system) tell us that Christ lived (!) was a real person (!) but that the whole of His life was really like our human lives; wherein was nothing obnormal, nothing miraculous ! This method, which the Germans Semler and Dulus misused so wearisomely, has quickly succumbed to the derision of the Mythical school itself. Thus, briefly stated, we are called upon to reject Christ, because one section of these wiseacres by their " God, Reason," tells us he was a myth! whilst the other section (who give them the lie), affirm that He was a reality t The younger Pliny and Tacitus give abundant proof of the reality of the exis- tence of Christ. Jesus Christ is the great name in his- tory. He who bears the name is known throughout the world. Even among the savage and degenerate tribes of the human race, his apostles preach . with- out ceasing that he died upon the Cross, and the off-scourings of mankind may be saved by loving him. Even those who are neutral, in the modern world, recognise that none is better for the weak and miserable. The greatest intellects of the past would be forgotten if memorials, as palaces, obelisks or tombs; if written testimonies,as papyrus or parchments, bricks, columns or medals had not preserved their memory. Jesus survives in the conscience ef the faith- ful; there is his witness and indestructible monument. The church founded by him fills time and space with his name. She knows him, she loves him, she adores him. This church of Christ founded by him, christianised and civilised the human race. If Jesus were in reality a mere man, as these " r easoners" tell us he was, how comes it that be has marked on the earth so deep a fur- row, and founded A religion which dominates the world - —Yours truly,</p>
        <p>HISTORICTJS. [Witb these two replies to Mr. Peers' lettor of fort- night ago, this correspondence is closed.—En.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FRODSHAM:</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM:</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MERTINO.—The annual meeting os this council was held on Friday evening. Mr. Heywood presided and there were also present :—Messrs. J. M. Williams, T. Arthur Rigby, W. D. Ringrose, T. Earlam, W. E. Shore, James flidge, W. Youd, P. Booth, J. Barrow, Joseph Corker, George Rodgers, T. Davies (over- seer), and G. Jones (clerk). Mr. Williams said he had pleasure in proposing Mr. Eflywood as chairman —Mr. Booth seconded, and it was carried nem. con.—Mr.. You proposed. and Mr. Corker seconded, that Mr. Stone be one of the overseers.—Mr. Williams proposed that Mr. I. Rothwell be re-appointed, and that Mr. Charles E. Linaker be the other one. Both gentlemen having expressed their willingness to serve together.—Mr. Barrow seconded.—Mr. Rodgers then proposed Mr. Earlarn, this being seconded by Mr. Youd.—Mr. Eerier's, however, withdrew, with the consent of his proposer and seconder. On a show of hands being taken four votes were recorded for Mr. Shore, and six each for Messrs. Linaker and Rothwell, and these gentlemen were deelared to he duly elected. It was understood that Mr. Linaker would attend to the books and accounts of the parish.—The Chairman briefly stated that the late senior overseer of the poor, Mr. Thomas Davies who was present, had held this office of trust for 28 years and during which time he had discharged his arduous duties in a most efficient manner, but he had expressed a wish ' to be released from the office on account of infirmity,and the Chairman proposed, and Mr. Williams seconded, that the best thanks of the meeting be accorded to Mr. Davies.—Mr. Shore supported the motion, and the vote was a unanimous; one.—Committees were then appointed for work as under :—The boundaries question, mainten- ance of footpaths, right of way, standing orders, and the charities. ARCHIDIACONAL VlSlTATlON.—Archdeacon Barber at- tended Frodsharn Parish Church for the purpose of ad- mittine the wardens and sidesmen for the following churches :—Alvanley, Appleton Thorn, Astnu, erne/ton, Daresbnry, Frndsham, ntapnenhall, Halton, Fielshy, lace, Kingsley, Latchford, Little Leigh, Lower WhitleY, Norlev, Runcorn, Stockton Heath, Stretton, Thelwall, and Walton. The usual declaration was signed by the following wardens and sidesmen :—Alvanley, Thomas Wright and S. H. Noden (wardens) ; Appleton Thorn, William Henry Sutton, Joseph Ilthhert, (wardens) ; Aston, Henry 'Talbot. and John Edwin Bate (wardens) ; Daresbury, Peter Harnblett and T. Weir (wardens) ; Frodeham, Charles Edward Linaker and R. Bate (war- dens) ; G. Gleeve, F. W. Spencer. T. D. Timmins, G. Aston, Joseoh Gorst, and John .T. Wilkinson '(sidesmen); Clrappenhall, Alan Lister Kaye, and Leigh Ley- land (wardens) , Halton, John Bente:, Horton and Joseph Stokes (wardens) ; flelshy,William Nil Waters (warden); Ince, Walter Greenway and John Warberthn Gasmen (wardens) ; Kingsley, Henry Moreton and M. ; Latchford (Christ Church), John Loxon ; Little Leigh, Daniel Massey and Ralph Francis Horton (wardens) ; Lower Whitley, Charles E. Riimney and Peter Hayes (wardens) ;Norlev, William Walley (warden) ; Runcorn (All Saint's), J. E. Meredith and John Littler (wardens); William Mellor,Walter Timmins, W. T. Crosby and John Yates (sidesmen) ; Runcern (Hole Trinity). Charles A. Titnmine and William Hankinsen (warden.) ; Stockton Heath, Edward WOalli and William Richardson ; Stret- ton, John Renshaw and John Rtitter (wardens) ; Then- wall, Admiral Jones Parry ; Walton, J. Weir (warden). Tea was afterwards partaken of in the schoolroom. ORDINATION OF MR. A. H. 9AFF,RS.—Ori Thursday, Mr. A. H. Sayers, pastor of Union Church, Frodshem, was ordained. At the afternoon service the Rev. John Yonge (Warrington) presided, and there were also pre- sent : Rev. G. Whitfield (Over), Rev. F. Carter (North- wich), Rev. J. W. Clark (Chester), Rev. H R. Didceck, Mr. A. H S tyers, Mr. T. Right., and Mr. W. M. Wil- liams. The proceedings commenced with the singing of the hymn "Lord of the living harvest," after which the Rev. G. Whitfield read the scriptures and prayed. —The Rev. John Yemen in the course of a short arldr.ss. said being present there that afternoon took hie mind end ' memory hack to the day when he was ordained to the ministry of Christ's gospel at East Cowes, Isle of Wight, irl 1853. It had been his privilege to have enmething to do with the history of that church at Frndsham Bridge. Not only had he conducted the services when the 1 church was at the mill, lent he al9l conducted the opening services in that building. Fle was hoping most' sincerely that mane bright and prosperous days were in store for them undet the ministry of Mr. Sayers. He must explain that by that ordination they were not going to confer Any powers upon Mr. Sayers that he did not possess before. They met to give their cordial and brotherly recognition of the fact that Mr. Sayers believed and gave evidence that he had been called by God to the ministry of CITe gospel of His Son. They conferred no priestly function or priestly office. By that service .Mr. Sayers gave public expression to the fact that he be- lieired it was a Divine call, and they were giving their brotherly acknowledgment that as as far as they knew he was right in his conviction. He thought they would all feel that it was a very important and solemn occa- sion. Be hoped it would be the beginning of many bright and blessed days for that church—The Rev. F. Carter gave an exposition of Free Church principles. He said they were Protestant Dissenters. As Protestants they, lifted up their voices against the'priest standing between the soul and the Saviour, whether they found it practised in the Papal or Episcopal churches. They claimed their right to criticise and protest against all that was unscriptural. They held that it was the Privilege of each one to find guidance of the Father through the beloved, without the intervention of any priest. That in all matters of religion it was the heri- tage of everyone to think and judge for himself,and that concerning faith and truth the Holy Scriptures were the supreme authority and guide. They were called upon to protest against the sacerdotalism of dome of the churches. As free Churchmen they could • not allow the State a voice in the con- trol of their church. Congregationalism was not an Invention of three or four centuries ago as some super- ficial controversialists said it was. It was rather a rediscovery and a return of the practices of the primitive church. The sole qualification for membership with the Congregational church was discipleship. Their testimony had to he turned against all forms of unriehtenusnese. Their foundation was spiritual..--Mr. T. Rig,by then gave a statement on behalf of the church in which he alluded to the careful consideration they had given in selecting Mr. Sayers as their pastor —The Rev. J. W. Clark then asked Mr. Sayers the ordination questions which were aratwered by Mr. Sayers at length. Mr. Clark then offered the ordination prayer, and the after- noons proceedings concluded with the benediction —Tea was afterwards partaken of in the schoolroom. —ln the evening a well-attended ordination service was held, when addressee were delivered by prominent Con- tregational ministers. WESLEYAN MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—In connection with this class a coffee concert was held on Wednesday even- ing, under the presidency of Mr. J. M. Williams. An interesting programme of vocal and instrmental music was submitted and a pleasant evening was spent.</p>
        <p>Earthquake shocks, some of considerable severity. continue to be experienced at Laibach. in anstriA. Certain streets deemed to be unsafe have been closed by the authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EXAMINER( SATURDAY; APRIL 27, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER( SATURDAY; APRIL 27, 1895.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A SLIIIPER OUT. • At the Warrington Occasional Court, en Monday afternoon, before Mr. James Tinsley, John Morris was sentenced to seven days' imprisonment for hav- ing that morning slept in an outbuilding belonging to Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., at Raydock, and</p>
        <p>not being able to give a good account of himself.</p>
        <p>BOWLING HANDICAP AT NEWTON. The first handicap of the season in connection with the Newton Nursery Bowling Club took place on Saturday. There were 32 eutries, and the green being in Rood condition some fairly contested games were witnessed. In the result Mr. L. Murphy took first prize, G. McCormick second, J. Lightbody third, F. Burgess fourth. SONS Or TEMPERANCE. On 'Wednesday the Samuel Morley branch of Cat det of Temperance met at seven o'clock in the Co- operative Hall. Sister E. Profit, W.A. presided. One new member was proposed. The G.W.P. Bro. John Judson instaikd the officers for the current quar- ter. The Britain Need Division met at eight o'clock in the same room. Bro. S. Foden. W.P., presided. One new member was proposed. The delegates to the Grand Division gave their reports, and were accepted. Bowdon was recommended for the pic- nic. Arrangements were made tor the holding of an open air meeting on the Market ground, on Wed- nesday, May Bth. CONCRRT AT EARLESTOWN. On Saturday evening a concert was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, for the benefit of V. Hughes, a blind man. There was a large audience, and an excellent programme was provided, consist- ing of vocal and instrumental pieces. All the ar- tistes were well know local ladies and gentlemen, and individually they performed their tasks to the satisfaction of the audience. Those who gave their services were Misses May Clare, Davies, E. Jones, Messrs. F. P. Clare, Sudwortb, T. Clare, J. Howard, J. Nichols, J. Higham, and J. R. Barr. Miss , Greenall accompanied in her usual efficient manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GOOD I'EMPLATtS.</head>
        <p>GOOD I'EMPLATtS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive • Hall, on Monday evening. Bro. J. Miller, CT., presided, and there was a large attendance of mem- bers. Six candidates were initiated- and several others wen proposed for membership. After the transaction of other business, a number of visitors from the. "Gleam of Sunshine," lodge Tranmere, were received with honour,, and invited to occupy the various chairs. An open meeting was•then de- clared, and an excellent entertainment was given by the Tranmere friends. An instructive address was given in the course of the ev.ening by Bro. Cross- thwaite, which was listened to with much interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>(EBENEZER LODGE):,</head>
        <p>(EBENEZER LODGE):,</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session of the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge, No, 75g, was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Tuesday evening. The ' session was presided over by Bro. J. Taylor, C.T., there being a good attendance of visitors and mem- bers. Arrangements were made for the members of the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge to pay a visit to Latchford Bonehill Branch Lodge on Monday next by waggonettes, The grogramroe of the evening was "Musical night," Bros. W. Brown and A. Morris being responsible. The following took part :—Sister Mrs. Witty, Bro. W. Brown, trnd Bro. Bothwell. Songs were also submitted by Bros. Crowther, Bro. Hopkins, Bro. W. MB, Bro. J. Dickson, Bro. J. Singleton. After having spent a very profitable and interesting evening a vote of thanks was ten- dered to the various members who took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BABIES AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>BABIES AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>During the past week two cases of rabies in dogs has been discovered in this district. The first case was that of a small-cross bred collie dos belonging to Arther Cooper, of 29, Legh-street, Earlestown. The dog was noticed to be foaming at the mouth about 11 o'clock in the morning, and was shortly after seen snapping at imaginary objects. It was kept in the back yard until about one o'clock when the owner had it shot. It was afterwards examined by Mr. S. Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, Newton, who discovered unmistakable presence of rabies. The other ctve was a brown Irish sheep dog belonging to Mr. Peter longton, Alder Firm, Winwick. The dog has been confined to the chain for several month, and a few days ago began snapping in a suspicious manner, and as it did not seem to improve, it was shot. It was afterwards examined by Mr. Hewitt, and found to have been suffering from rabies. PAINFUL SUICIDE AT THE VI/LOAN. _ . .</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Jr.N S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry at the Wargrave Inn, Eariestown, into the circumstances attending the death of James Shep- herd, of 6, Derby-row, Vulcan Cottages. -67 years of age, From the evidence of the widow it ap- peared the deceased had been unwell for some con- siderable time, and had not worked for about three , years. He had been suffering from bronchitis and asthma, and had been in a very poor state of health. About half-past four on Sunday afternoon he left the kitchen and went into the closet inthe yard. After being there a short time his wife heard a sound in the closet as of glass breaking. She went into the closet and saw her husband with a small bottle in his hand, which he Was trying to break. , She asked him what he was doing, and he said lie was breakinit a bottle, he Lad been taking lauda- num, as be was tired of his life. He then went back into the kitchen, and appeared sleery and drowsy Dr. Harold Watkins, of Newton, was sent. for, and arrived about six o'clock. He applied the stomach pump, and used other remedies for over two hours, but without avail, and the man died about 8.15 at night. A ve-dict of "Suicide whilst temnorarily insane" was returned. VOLUNTEER CHURCH PARADE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The annual church parade of the local conting- ents of hussars and volunteers stationed at Newton took place on Sunday. The weather was beautifully fine, and there was in consequence an exceptionally large concourse of spectators, from the various out- lying villages. The hussars' band turned out first, and under the conductorship of Bandmaster Batley played a selection of music in 'front of the residence If Mr. J. E. Pardey. The troo:s followed soon after;. and were drawn no in line behind the band. There were on parade 4S troopers, under the command of Lieutenant. H. MeCorquodale, and Sergeant-Major Strange. The local Company of 6th V.8.K.L.R., mustered st the orderly room, and headed by the bugle band,marched up to the'rendezvous'and fell in behind the hussars. There were 64 on parade includ- ing nand; and they were in command of Captain N. and Lieutenant, S. NfeCorquolale. The other offilers on parade wore Colonel Wood and Sergeant-Instruc- tor Bird. Following these came- the local Company of the ist V.B.P.W.S.L. Regiment, 44 strong. under the command of Major .Widtl'hws and Sergeant- Instrintor Woof-ling. After forming fours they marched to St. Peter's Church, the hussars' .hand playing a lively quick step. The bearing of the men was most soldier-like, and their marching remarkably steady. The sermon in the church was of a character appropriate to the'occasion, and was preached by the Rev. F. W. Johnston. Fowl 0P TEMPERANCE. - _</p>
        <p>The annual Meeting of the Earlestown Grand Division of the Sons of Temperance was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestowo, on Saturday. Bro. Richard Higgins, G.W.P., presided. Representa- tives were present from Haydock, St. Helens, and Widnes, as well as from Earlestown. After the opening of G.D. in due form,and the credentials of representatives had been approved, the following . brethren who attended the GO). for the first time ' were initiated and took their seats as members : Bros. D. H. Robinson, Earlestown ; J. A. Miller,and James Houghton, St. Helens. A balance sheet was presented and reports given by the G.W.P., Bro. Higgins. G.S. Bro. William Pearson and G. Treas. Bro William Clare, from whith it was seen that the working of G.D. for 1894 had been fairly satis- factory. The membership had gone up from 336 in 1893 to 409 at the end of 1894. Entered for sick benefits there were now 295 members with an.aver- age of 30 years. 62 members had been on the funds during the year, as compared with 78 in the year 1893. Cash paid for sickness had averaged 14s. 41. per member, as compared with 184. 4hd. in 1893, and for funerals 2a. 5/d. per member, as against 34. 51d. in 1893. The average number of days' sick- ness per member in various centres was 1 in Widnes, 181 days; Ffaydock, 14; St.lielens, 91; and Earlestown, 5. The average number for G D.. 9 days per man, as against 11 days in 1893. The re- ceipts had exceeded the expenditure by £B4 15s 11d. The rate of interest, on investments had bee'n over 41 per cent. ; present worth of O.D. £697. The G.St stated that the return F) for the first quar- ter of 1895 gave signs of further progress, and he looked forward to being able to report the year 1895 AR the best year of all through which they as t' Sons" tad passed. Officers elected for coming year :—G.W.P. William Burrows. G.W A. William Wheatley, G Chan. J. Stockley, G Con. E Auriertne, G. Sentl. A. Chalmers, P G.W.P. It. Higgins, G. Treas. William Clare and G.S. William Pearson. The next meeting is to be held in Widnes.</p>
        <p>sup. Thn Priven nl Wales will viwit Douglas in :limn or; Vv. occasion of the great regatta in which the Britannia will compete. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.)</p>
        <p>The Easter vestry meetings have come and gone, and not a single discordant note has been raised at any one of them. Such a patting on the back has not been witnessed for some considerable time,and we can only hope that strifes and petty jealousies will be an absent member for a considerable time longer.</p>
        <p>I fully expected that with the advent of finer weather the sickness which is playing havoc with the schools here about would' speedily disappear. But this does not seem to be the case. The infantile disease of measles is still rampant,and at Wargrave Schools the officials have decided t• give the chil- dren in the infant school a little longer holiday. The pinch of non-attendance is severely felt at the other schools, and the masters and mistresses are ' much downcast in view of the near approach of the annual examinations. Of course, every means is taken to stamp out the disease, but unless the hearty co-operation of parents is given, the task will be &amp;difficult one.</p>
        <p>The need of a ratepayers' association is very greatly felt in Earlestown,and especially is this the case in view of the recent action of the majority on the District Council, which is really the last straw—but whether 'it will break the proverbial camel's back remains to be Seen. The people of Earlestown at the present time seem to be most , apathetic, and are evidently content to be trodden underfoot by thoe whoa few months ago professed themselves the friends of the workingman. I know we have in times gone hy had,ratepayers' associa- tions,and they did excellent work,but they fell into desuetude, and so at the present time our:represen- tatives (?) do pretty much as theyplease. Now, at Newton they have such an association,at least there is a committee, and they claim to represent the ratepayers. But do they? Who can tell?</p>
        <p>Anyway they meet now and again in solemn conclave, and project schemes, some hairbrained, and through the medium of the Council majority launch them on the township. They met this week, and decided to.go in for that £35 which they say the Council owe them, and with the aid of the majority, who of swim vote as one man, good or bad, they will get it. And yet some of the coun- cillors sarthey are working for the best interests of the to gnship. It is for our good as ratepayers that we should hand over £35, to pay the out-of- pocket expenses of a few private individuals at Newton. Here the services of a Ratepayers' Asso- ciation in Earlestown would be useful. •</p>
        <p>But this is not all. At this same private gather- ing they introduced a new water scheme,the details of which I am not in a position to give. From what I know howeder, it means saddling the town- ships with a needless and foolish outlay of money, some thousands of pounds, in order to get a supply of water independent of the Railway Company. We pay to the company 6d. per thousand gallons for the water they supply to us, but if these wise gen- tletnen can show us a way of getting it cheaper, without burdening the rates with an initial expen- diture, then lam with them. But I am afraid neither they nor the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, who pro- fesses to be in favour of a new scheme, can do this. We are perfectly satisfied in Earlestown with our water supply, and especially so since the Railway Co.,heve gone to the additional expense to improve the supply and I venture to assert that not any single representative of any ward in Earlestown or Wargrave has been instructed to support Newton in their hair brained scheme.</p>
        <p>Here again the aid of an 'association would be beneficial. Nvidently the majority of the rate- payers are to be subservient to the wish of the minority, but if the,Earlestown working-classes do not wish to pay higher rents than they are at the ptesent time paying, they must raise their voices, and tell their representatives whaf the:consequences will be, if they vote for such a scheme without first consulting them en the matter.</p>
        <p>'The annual meeting of the Council was held last week, but it was held at an hour when none of the working men could be present. And the result shows that in future the ratepayers shall not be present if the rn,ljority can help it. By the Parish Councils Act it was most certainly intended that the public should take an interest in he Township work, but the majority on the Newt oT Council fear the criticising eye of the public, and so have fixed the meetings for the future at four o'clock in the afternoon, instead of Mx o'clock, the workingman's time. Election addresses, promises,and the interest of the workingman, were all thrown to the wind, and nothing but private convenience was con- sidered. If ever the workingmen of Earlestown wera snubbed they have been effectually snubbed on this occasion, and the majority of 8 could not have given plainer evidence of their wish to keep out the public than they did when they voted for four o'clock. They forgot there were workingmen on the Couneil, who in order to attend the meeting, would have to sacrifice their hard earned wages. Thus from a democratic council it descends again to an arigtocratic assembly. • If it was inconvenient for certain members to attend at six o'clock, why did these gentlemen put themselves forward for election. We can wait patiently however, for the day of reckoning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FUNERAL OF TffE LATE MRS. WYNNE</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF TffE LATE MRS. WYNNE</p>
        <p>FFOULKES.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mrs. Wynne Ffciulkes,wife of his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, the County Court Judge for the Warrington district,took place at the Chester General Cemetery on Saturday. The deceased lady who was in her 73rd year, died On the previous Wednesday. A celebratibn of the Holy Communion was held at 8 a.m. at St. Oswalel's Church, and the first portion of the burial servira was conducted at 11.45 at St. Oswald's The Revs. C. l. Lowndes and 11. Grantham (St. Mary's), con- ducted the service, and the hymns "The strife is o'er" (135), Peace, Perfect Peace" (537), and the 00th Psalm were sting in Church. As the body was being removed the " None Dimittis " was chanted. The chief mourners were Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, Mr. .1. W. Ffoulkes. the Rev. 11. Ffoulkes, Mr. P. J. U. Foulke, Mr. and Nlrs. Reginald Dickson,Colonel Waters (military attache), Drs. William and Henry Dohie. Major Jocelyn Ffoulkes, Miss Joycelyn Ffoulkes, Mrs. E. H. Ffoulkes, Colonel Polton, Mr. and Mrs. 13. Jackson,Mr. R. L. Barker, Mr.A.Barker, &amp;c. In addition to the officiating clergymen, the Revs. B. N. Atkinson.F. E. B. Wale and Paton were also present at the service. Allusion V 734 made at the Warrington County Court on Thursday to tho bereavement of his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. Mr. Percy Davies addressing his Hnnnur Deputy Judge J. D. Crawford,said that of course 19 is Ronnur would he aware of the great loss their worthy judge had sustained through the death of his wife. They, the Members of the legal profession, felt it SVO.3 their mournful duty to ask him to direct the Regis- trar to convey to Jodgo Ffoulkes what they fA for him, and the hope that it would not affect him so that it might keep him away from them for a considerable tisie. Of course they all appreciated how well he (Judge Crawford) anted as his deputy, but he did not speak for himself,but for everybody attending that court when he said that he did feel very much indeed what Judge Ffoulke's sufferings under the circumeances must be.— Mr. A. Browne endormad evero hingMr.Davies had said. ' Die Honsur, Judge J. D. Crawford, said that he would ask the learned renistrar to convey to his Honour Judge Wynne Floulkes the words of sympathy which the gentlemen had expressed not only on behalf of them- selves, but also on behalf of every other practitioner in that court. He sincerely hoped that for a considerable number Of years Judge Foulkes would continue to pre- side in that court.not only in the intarests of themselves and himself. 1)4 in the interests of justice. The Registrar (Mr. T. J. Ridgway) promised to convey the message of condolence to his Honour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FAMILY EXTERMINATED BY • SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>A FAMILY EXTERMINATED BY • SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>--- - A domestic tragedy, marked by sad and touching circumstances. was enacted on Monday night at No. 48, Rue Montmartre, Paris. A portion of the house together with a shop, was rented by a money changer named Pietri, at a rent of ip,ooo francs a year. Pietri's business was exceedingly prosperous,and he and his wife were devoted to each other. They had. in fact, everything to malte them happy, had it not been that they were inconsolable on account of the death of their only ann. The latter, who was only 18 years of age, committed Sluici:le during the latter part of .last year owing to disappointment in a love affair, and his mother had never recovered from the blow. On Monday even- Mg, whilo her 'husband was absent from the house, Madame Pietri took a pistol and shot herself in the temple. M.Pietri.on returning home about seven o'clock, found his wife dead. Ile shortly afterwards took the pistol, which he found lying by her side, and blew out his brains. The husband and wife now lie side by side on the bed on which their son ended his life last year in the same manner.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT AT ARIIS AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The Beo3nd annual assault at arms in connection with the Earlestown,'Newton, and District Gymnasium was held on Thursdayevening at the Town Hall,Harlestown. Owing, no doubt, to the unfavourable weather, the attendance was not AS large as was expected. The chair was occupied by the Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P., and there were also present, the Rev. T.R. Matthews,Drs.Watkins and Patterson, and Messrs. C. B. F. Borron9 CO., R. Stone, J.P., T. Thompsoa, C.H. Bodmer, and B. Burton. A lengthy but interesting programme was gone through and some excellent work was done, showing that the members of the gymnasium had not been remiss in their practices.</p>
        <p>The programme opened with a tight dumb-bell exercise, in which the following members of the gymnasium took part .—Messrs. J. Cook, F. Wagg, R. J. Hill, N. H. Percival, Percy Percival, W. Sankey, H. Pearson J. Mather, and Dr. H.E. Watkins. The exercise was Sale in excellent time and rhythm and with very slight mis- takes. A dumb-bell race followed in six heats, the prize being a silver medal, presented by Mr. John Simpson. The following was the result of the heats . let, IJ. Greenslade, 1 yard start ' • 2nd, T. Cook, 1 yd.; 13, R. floothroyd, .1 yd. ; 4, .1. Bartley, 1 yd. ; 5, F. Fletcher, 3 yds. ' • 6. H. Mayor, 2 yds. In the semi final. J. Greenslade, H. Boothroyd, and H. Mayor were the winners, and at the final heat.H. Mayor ran out the vie- ter,after an exciting race. The chin over bar handica p for, a silver medal presented by el r.J.Lythgoe,was a very in teresting event. There were seven contestants. The follow- ing was the result of the cam petition : R J. Hill, handi- capped 5 pts.,scored 29; P. Percival, 12,31 W. Sankey 12, 32 ;J. Hodnett, scratch, 33; N. Percival, 9,33; J. Mather. 5, 28,F. Wagg, scratch, 30. J. Hodnett, and N. Percival tying, they contested again.and the latter won by 30, pts. to 28. A silver medal was given by Mr. G. A. Williams for weight lifting and four members of thei gymeasium competed for it. It. J. Hill was the victor' with 31 points, the other competition by W. Pellan, 28, Dr. H. E. Watkins, 20, and N. H. Percival, 13. The competition consisted in lifting half a cwt. above the head and then touching the floor, the one who could do this the greatest number of times winning the medal. The winners of the National Gymnastic championship for 1895, (Liverpool team), gave several fine exhibitions of their skill during the evening by the horizontal bar, and barbell exercises. They and also members nt the Earlestown Gymnasium also performed some good feats on the swinging rings, parallel bars, and the vaulting horse. An exhibition in high jumping was also given and P. Percival gave great satis- faction in his clean jumping, but tie failed at sft. 6ins which was topped by Messrs. Aitken and Buckley, of Liverpool, but these did not. clear 51t. Bin. The pro- . .</p>
        <p>, gramme was varied by an exhibition of sword feats by Sergeant-Major Storrnr who did many wonderful per- formances including tbe cutting of a piece of zinc stand- ing on edge, cutting of a potato on a man's head and afterwards upon his neck, cutting of a stick suspended on paper hanging upon the sharp edge of a razor with- out cutting the paper,the cutting of a sheep in two with a single blow, etc. The sergeant-major was loudly ap- plauded for his feats. He alki gave an exhibition in fencing' with Dr. Street, of Haydock, and each showed himself an adept at this branch of athletics. Messrs. C. Simpson and G. A. Williams acted as judges, and the 'ion, director was Mr. R. Bunton, and the hon. sec. Mr. 1. Cooke. .</p>
        <p>At the close of the programme, Mr. Legh presented the prizes to the successful performers. Be expressed , his pleasure at being there that night. He was usually at a place where he derived very little pleasure, but that had been an entertainment from which he derived both amusement and instruction. The development of the physical powers was very important. Their mental development was sufficiently looked after by the State. It was an old tradition that an Englishman was equal to he did not know how many foreigners, but the foreigners had now bestowed much attention to athletics, and he did not think that at the present time, they were much behind the English in this respect. He considered that the gentlemen from Liverpool deserved their befit thanks for adding to the pleasure of the evening, and he hoped that not jealousy, but sincere emulation would be arotred among • the local gymnasts to attain their excellence. _ _</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks tn Mr. Legh, proposed by Dr. Watkins, seconded by Mr. Dorton, brought the meeting to a termination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT</p>
        <p>Continued from page 3.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>_ In the House of Commons on Thursday evening. on the order for the third reading of the London and North- Western Railway Bill, Mr. LLOYD-G EORGR objected to the Bill being passed now, on the ground that he desired to give the Company an opportunity of reconsidering their polition with regard to the dismissal of monoglot Weigh workmen. The SPEAKER said this was a matter of general policy which did not affect this particular Bill and it appeared- t him that Mr. Ltoarn-GEougu's course of argument was out of order. Mr. LLOYD GEORGE then moved that the debate be adjourned for a month. Mr. D. PrzbutEr denied that there was ground for the corn plaints made. If this Rill were adjourned or thrown I out, the results, he said, would be much more injurious to the people of Wales and the other portions of the country affected than to the Company. He had no ob- _ jaction to the inquiry,but he thought a month's adjourn- ment was a very long time. Mr. BRYCE thought a fort night would be sufficient, and in the end proceedings on the Bill were adjourned for that period. Mr. A. CHAMBERLAIN inquired what action the Government proposed to take on the reports of the Aged- Poor Commission. Mr. SIIAW-LEFET RE said the Govtrn- merit intended to take meaaures for a further examina- tion of the matter. Sir J. T. Manua added, in reply to further questions, that the Treasury was about to introducs a Bill to amend the Friendly Societiea Act which would introduce a number of the proposals made. It would enable juvenile societies to be merged in the adult societies.</p>
        <p>Sir J. F. HIBBERT was questioned hy Mr. fivava re- specting. an alleged attempt to ta,roper with the consti- tution of the Royal Liver Friendly Society. Mr. BRYCE informed Mr. WEIR that he was in commu- nication with the Local Government Board with a view to devising some means to prevent kitcherr boiler expb- sions in winter. -- -</p>
        <p>- Mr. ACLAND, replying to a question, said the Govern- ment were not without hope of being able to deal With the question of teachers' superannuation in the course of the present session. The debate on the motion for leave to bring in a bill to amend the Acts relating to crofters' holdings in Scotland was resumed by Sir 0. TRRVELYAN, who, after explain- ing the provisions of the measure, said it was designed to make legislation on the subject as complete as Parliament could make it. The hill was, after some discussion, brought in and reai the first time. Mr. BRYCE introduced his Light Railways Bill, which, he said, was based upon the recommendations of the Committee appointed at the Board of Trade Conference which he convened some months ago. It committed the authorisation of light railways to County Councils. After Mr. CHAPLIN and other members of the Opposition had criticised the Government proposals, the bill was read a first time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>JAPAN AND ITS VICTORIES.</head>
        <p>JAPAN AND ITS VICTORIES.</p>
        <p>The Japanese Government has replied to the Netes presented by the representatives of Russia, Germany, and France protesting against the acquisition of Chinese territory on the Continent of Asia. 'Pie Mikado's Minis- ters state that the Japanese people are " intoxicated " with their sumessestand that if the Government were to yield to the demands of the three Powers there would be civil war. The Russian Government, however, "firmly tadheres" to its demands. Telegrams from the Continen- tal capitals show that the extraordinary alliance be- ween Germany, France, and Russia for the purpose of depriving Japan of the fruits of her victories is causing much excitement.</p>
        <p>filE BEGINNENOS OF A BIG FORTUNE. Some millionaires lay the foundations of their fortunes by picking up a pin, others by entering London with half-a-crown in their pockets. Mr. Leiter, the father of Mrs. George Curzon, began his successful career by washing windows. Thirty-five years ago •he was in Chicago without either the pin or the half-crown, but xvith a recommendation to Mr. John V. Farwell, the head of the great " dry goods " firm. Mr. Farwell had nothing for him, but as he turned to go young Leiter looked at the offine windows, and said," Your windows need washing., sir. Let _me clean them. Anything is better than nothing." At lunch time Mr. Farwell handed the young man two dollars and said, " Hang up your coat in the outer office. I like your pluck, and will find you something else to do," Mr. Leiter in course of time became head of the firm, and his fortune is now said to be worth 20,000,000d015.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CLIFTON.</head>
        <p>CLIFTON.</p>
        <p>PROPOSRD PARISH Coomm—On Friday evening a meeting of the ratepayers of the township of Clifton was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom for the pur- pose of receiving nominations in connection with the proposed parish council. A vote was taken which re- sulted as follows: John Gondy,4 votes ; William Fletcher, 15; Peter Massey, 8; William Pope, 5; wamuel Row- land, 8: Joseph Stokes, 17 ; John Thomas, 13; William Wild, 8; and John Wilson (Runcorn) 8. There only being five members required, and there being three Of the candidates who had received an equal number of votes for the fourth and fifth places a difficulty was ex perienced in making a declaration. Under the circum- stances a poll was demanded by Mr. Pope.</p>
        <p>POLICE CHANGES AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Longworth, who has been promoted to an inspectorship, as stated in our issue last week, was on Monday transferred to the InBe district of the Wigan division. The vacancy has been filled by the appointixont to Newton of Sergeant Bancks who for ten or 11 years has been stationed at Par- bold and Standish.</p>
