      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-27_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH Y.P.S.C.E.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH Y.P.S.C.E.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PUBLIC MEETING.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS BY THE REV. D. W. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the third anniversarz of the t above society Was celebrated in the schoolroom over , Arpley Station where a tea was held, which was par- taken of by about 100 persons. After tea a public meet- ing was held in the same room.when the chair was taken by the Rev. E. T.Reed (vicar),who was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. D. W. Robarts (late hon. sec. of the Warrington and District Christian Endeavour Association), Mr. William Hickman, and Mr. Mounfield. There was an excellent attendance, the room being crowded. The Rev. E.T. REED said he could not allow that meet- ing to begin without expressing the hearty welcome which all their members and associates felt at seeing so many hind friends from other docieties who had coins together that evening in respense to their invitation to help them to celebrate their birthday: He hoped that this kind of re-unionlor one society with another on an occasion like that would be continued. Further, they would be glad to reciprocate the kindly feeling shown on this occasion. As they had the honour to commence the movement in the town they felt that God had indeed blessed them very much since their commencement three years ago. During the past year they had evidences of growth,and be thought there had been evidences of very real growth amongst their members and increased in- telligence as to the real purposes for which the society was brought into existence. He still thought there was great room for expansion in connection with their own society and he ventured to say with the other societies in the town and district ; and he also thought that the various Christian Endeatour Societies in Warrington might do a great deal more than was being done now to evangelise people who did not know Christ as their personal Saviour. Miss LEES, the recording secretary, read the annual report which stated that their weekly meeting 'continued to be of much good to all of their members. Their mem- bership was not quite so large as last year lent their spiritual growth was greater. They had 30 active members against 42 last year, of whom three had been tranferred to the junior branch,six associates as compar- ed with 11 the previous year, and six honorary members as against three previously. Altogether their three years' experience of Christian Endeavour had tended to con- vince them that it was a work of love. Mr. REED observed that the juniors' work had been a very encouraging one from the beginning. Mrs. NORMAN, superintendent of the Junior Branchen presenting her report said that they begun with a mem- bership of 13,3 active and 10 associates, while now there were 12 actives and 34 associates, showing an increase of 9 active and 24 associate members. The average at- tendance had been 56. The CEIAIRMAN said that they were all aware that owing to ill-health their dear friend Mr. Roberts was compelled to resign his pastorate in this town a short time ago,and he was also obliged td resign hi 1 position as hon. sec. of the Warrington and District Association. They knew how earnestly Mr. Roberts worked, and he (the chair- man) did not hesitate to say that the success which bad followed the movement had been due in a great measure to the earnest and self-denying efforts Mr. I Roberts had made. Her was sorry because of the public work Mr. Roberts had done in this town. He had taken a stand that few people would have taken in the drink traffic which was doing such harm amongst them. It was through his persistent efforts that one of the worst gambling-houses which existed until a short time ago in the town was interfered with. The speaker knew what Mr. Roberts did in order to bring about that champ, and be honoured him for the stand he took in connection • with the cpeestion of the drink traffic. The rev, gentle- man had the pleasure that night to ask the Rev. D. W. Roberts to accept a volume which be hoped would be a source of great pleasure to him and which was an account of a jeerney round the world undertaken by the Rev. Francis E. Clarke and his wife. The members of the C. E. Society were in a position to make Mr. Roberts a present of something else besides that and that WAS a Swan fountain pen. The inscription on the book read as follows :—" Presented to the Rev. D. W. Rcberte by the Young Peoples Society of Christion EndeavOur,War- rington and district Association,as a mark of esteem and appreciation of .his,Services as secretary of the associa- tion, April 1895." The Rev. D. W. Roberts said he was taken by surprise and could not say much as he felt so strongly their kind- ness and crMsideration in presenting him with a book</p>
        <p>;;licii-1;-e-should always consider hallowed by the recol- lection of .the time he ha:l.spent_in Warrington and. with</p>
        <p>the Warrington and district Association'. of Christian Endeavour. Ha would not have done what he had done if it had not been for the help of Mr. Reed. He was sure they would excuse him saying much on account of his illness, but he thanked them one and all who had contributed to make up that present. The subject of his address was "The dangers tube avoided and the methods to be pus- sued in order to make Christian Endeavour work really successful." He supposed there were dar gem to be avoided in this work as in all organisations, and the dangers in Christian Endeavour work were spirit that he had'noticed there and in different societies, and that was a criticising spirit. He had found members at times get up at a Christian Endeavour meeting and in- stead of saying a few helpful words they had taken it upon themselves to criticise the paper in an extreme manner, and in a way that would not add to the success /of the Christian Endeavour Society. It was their duty to avoid such spirit as far as they could. Another dangerous thing was a for- ward spirit—a feeling they met with sometimes in Endeavourers who wanted to be the" first horse " and unless they were right nothing was right. Such a spirit was not conducive to the success of Christian Endeavour I work and it Was their duty to guard against it. The next spirit was " Our selfish spirit." It was their work to reach out to the masses and not keep in their own circle, hut so far as possible to evangelise them. That was the spirit they wanted. Another danger was" an unfaithful spirit" which ought also to be guarded against. This was found in committee work and not taking part in meetings they had pledged themselves to take part in. The methods to be pursued were those written down in the constitution of the Society which was for Christ and the Church and its aim was to promote an earnest Christian life amongst it members and make them more useful in their service to God In order to make the work really successful the Endea." vourers must undertake to evangelise the whale world. In the first place there must bee total surrender to Christ and in the second place it could only be truly successful when they gave themselves to God. His partifte message he gave to them with.all solemnity and with all the en- thusiasm he possessed, and it was that in the name of God and His Church he asked thowt to consecrate them selves this day unto the Lord. The Rev. E. T. REED then referred iii felicitous terms to the services of Miss Lees.and in the name of the active and associate members he had great pleasure in asking Miss Lees to accept a handsome new testament and two molumes of Dr. Cunningham Geikie's " Life and Works of Christ," as a token of their respect and esteem. After a short address by Mr. Mounfield (sec. in place of the Rev. D. W. Roberts), the meeting terminated and a Consecration service was held.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday Mr. Justice Hawkins, referring to the paucity of actions down for trial, said that the question as to whether these assizes should be continued was one that deserved serious con- sideration. There was a great deal of work to be done in London, and yet the judges we're sent down to waste I about two weeks between Liverpool and Manchester before the real ass,zes commenced.</p>
